{\"conversation_id\":\"\",\"created_at\":1791382494,\"error\":null,\"id\":\"resp_20261007221436d45c29cebe894cd6\",\"incomplete_details\":null,\"instructions\":\"You are PIA (Pi-ABAP Agent), written in ABAP, running on the model glm-5.3 (z.ai) through backend OSG-STORE. If asked which model you are, say exactly that; never claim another model or vendor. Tools: outline(name), read_method(name, method, class), write_method(name, method, source - one METHOD block, class), read_object(name, include main|testclasses), write_source(name, source - FULL source, include), activate(name), run_tests(name). Rules: prefer outline, read_method and write_method for changing existing methods; use write_source only for new methods, changed declarations or a new test include, and then write the FULL source; read before write; always activate after write; if activation says publish pending, finish the turn and run_tests in the next turn. Answer briefly in the user language. Your own code: ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR (agent loop), ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (LLM client: builds requests, parses responses and tool calls), ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL (JSON helpers), ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION and ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION_STORE (conversation), ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY (tools), ZCL_PIA_15_T_* (the tools), ZCL_PIA_20_B_* (backends), ZCL_PIA_30_* (terminal, turns). Unit tests live in the testclasses include of a class.\",\"metadata\":null,\"model\":\"glm-5.3\",\"object\":\"response\",\"output\":[{\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Let me start by understanding the bug. The model responds with two tool calls. The first call's arguments contain a string literal with a single opening brace, e.g. `lv_open = '{'.`. The arguments arrive with extra text after the JSON (\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}) and the second call is lost.\\n\\nThis sounds like a JSON parsing issue in ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (LLM client: builds requests, parses responses and tool calls). Probably there's a brace-counting JSON extractor that scans for a balanced `{...}` \u2014 a single opening brace inside a string literal would break the balance counting, causing the extractor to consume too much (including the closing brace of the tool call structure) and produce arguments with trailing junk.\\n\\nWait \u2014 the symptom: arguments arrive with extra text after the JSON: `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"})`. Hmm, that looks like the arguments were parsed as `{ ... source ... }` where the extractor ended at the wrong closing brace. Actually, the trailing text `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"})` suggests the arguments JSON was cut at a brace inside a string, and then... Actually let me think.\\n\\nThe tool call structure might be something like:\\n```json\\n{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"METHOD m.\\\\n lv_open = '{'.\\\\n ENDMETHOD.\\\"},\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}\\n```\\n\\nIf the JSON extractor uses naive brace matching without string awareness, then when scanning the `arguments` object, the `{` inside the string literal `lv_open = '{'.` would increment depth, so the extractor would continue past the true end of the arguments object and capture text up to a later `}`. Hmm, but then the arguments would include trailing `,\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` minus a brace...\\n\\nActually, the reported symptom: \\\"Its arguments arrive with extra text after the JSON (\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}) and the second call is lost.\\\" So the first call's arguments = the correct arguments JSON followed by `,\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"` and maybe `}` \u2014 hmm, with a `)` at the end? The paren might be part of the bug report formatting. Let me not over-interpret; I need to read the actual code.\\n\\nLet me look at the classes: ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL (JSON helpers) and ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (parses responses and tool calls).\\n\\nPlan:\\n1. Outline/read ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL and ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP.\\n2. Find the extraction logic for tool calls (probably extracting balanced braces).\\n3. Write a red unit test in the testclasses include reproducing: two tool calls in one response, first call's arguments contain a string with `{`.\\n4. Fix the code (string-aware brace counting).\\n5. Run tests, show green (except ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR which stays red).\\n\\nLet me start reading. I'll read both classes in parallel.\",\"type\":\"reasoning_text\"}],\"encrypted_content\":null,\"id\":\"rs_20261007221436d45c29cebe894cd6\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"summary\":[],\"type\":\"reasoning\"},{\"content\":[{\"annotations\":[],\"text\":\"I'll start by inspecting the two classes most likely involved: the LLM client (parses responses/tool calls) and the JSON utility.\",\"type\":\"output_text\"}],\"id\":\"msg_20261007221436d45c29cebe894cd6\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"message\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7228011869871531016\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7228011869871531016\",\"name\":\"read_object\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7228011869871531015\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7228011869871531015\",\"name\":\"read_object\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"}],\"parallel_tool_calls\":true,\"previous_response_id\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":1376,\"input_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":0},\"output_tokens\":715,\"output_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":649},\"total_tokens\":2091}}
{\"conversation_id\":\"\",\"created_at\":1791382554,\"error\":null,\"id\":\"resp_202610072214548828f66f13714d50\",\"incomplete_details\":null,\"instructions\":\"You are PIA (Pi-ABAP Agent), written in ABAP, running on the model glm-5.3 (z.ai) through backend OSG-STORE. If asked which model you are, say exactly that; never claim another model or vendor. Tools: outline(name), read_method(name, method, class), write_method(name, method, source - one METHOD block, class), read_object(name, include main|testclasses), write_source(name, source - FULL source, include), activate(name), run_tests(name). Rules: prefer outline, read_method and write_method for changing existing methods; use write_source only for new methods, changed declarations or a new test include, and then write the FULL source; read before write; always activate after write; if activation says publish pending, finish the turn and run_tests in the next turn. Answer briefly in the user language. Your own code: ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR (agent loop), ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (LLM client: builds requests, parses responses and tool calls), ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL (JSON helpers), ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION and ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION_STORE (conversation), ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY (tools), ZCL_PIA_15_T_* (the tools), ZCL_PIA_20_B_* (backends), ZCL_PIA_30_* (terminal, turns). Unit tests live in the testclasses include of a class.\",\"metadata\":null,\"model\":\"glm-5.3\",\"object\":\"response\",\"output\":[{\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Let me analyze the bug. The report: model answers with two tool calls; the source in the first call contains a string literal with a single opening brace, e.g. `lv_open = '{'.` PIA runs only the first call. Arguments arrive with extra text after the JSON (`\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`) and second call is lost.\\n\\nSo the arguments are a JSON string, e.g.:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"method\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"M\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"METHOD m. lv_open = '{'. ENDMETHOD.\\\\\\\"}\\\"}\\n```\\n\\nThe parse path: `extract_balanced( iv_json = lv_e iv_key = 'arguments' iv_open = '{' iv_close = '}' )`. The problem: extract_balanced does not skip string literals. The arguments value is an escaped JSON string like `\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":...,\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"... '{' ...\\\\\\\"}\\\"`. Hmm wait \u2014 in the responses API format, arguments is a string containing escaped JSON. Let's simulate.\\n\\nAn output item in responses format:\\n\\n```\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"method\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"M\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"METHOD m.\\\\n lv_open = '{'.\\\\nENDMETHOD.\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}\\n```\\n\\nextract_balanced finds `\\\"arguments\\\"` then finds first `{` after it. The first `{` is inside the escaped string \u2014 `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...` \u2014 the char right after `\\\"` is `{`. Wait, actually the arguments string starts with `{` after the opening quote: `\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":...`. Hmm \u2014 actually the escaped string is `\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":...`. So the first character after `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"` is `{` \u2014 but wait, is it? `arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...`. The sequence after `\\\"arguments\\\"` is `:`, then `\\\"`, then `\\\\`, then `{`... So first `{` found is at the `{` in `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"` position 3. Then depth counting: it counts `{` and `}` characters regardless of escaping and string context.\\n\\nThe arguments string contains `'{'` literal: `lv_open = '{'.` \u2014 that adds an extra unmatched `{`. So depth never returns to 0 at the real end `}\\\"`; instead depth stays 1... wait let's count carefully. The escaped JSON inside: `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"...\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"...\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"METHOD m. lv_open = '{'. ENDMETHOD.\\\"}`. Braces: opening `{` (depth 1 from start), the `'{'` inside the string (depth 2), closing `}` (depth 1). Then after that comes `\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` \u2014 the final `}` of the output item closes depth to 0. So extract_balanced returns everything up to and including that final `}`, i.e. `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...ENDMETHOD.\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`. That matches the bug description: \\\"arguments arrive with extra text after the JSON (\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"call_id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"c1\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\"})\\\".\\n\\nThen split_entries: it splits the output array on balanced braces. The first entry consumed ends at the final `}` of item 1 (since the `'{'` inside adds depth), so the second call is lost \u2014 actually wait, the second call would start after. Let's see: split_entries on the output array `[ {...item1...}, {...item2...} ]`. Item1 contains an extra `{` from `'{'`, so its depth doesn't close at item1's `}`; it closes at item2's `}` or later. So item1 entry swallows both items (up to where depth hits 0 \u2014 likely at item2's closing `}`), and then loop continues after that \u2014 second call lost. Actually the arguments extracted from entry 1 would include the extra text after the JSON, exactly as reported.\\n\\nNote that in the chat completions format, arguments is also an escaped string. Same issue. But wait \u2014 for typical arguments without braces in string literals, the first `{` after `\\\"arguments\\\"` is `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"` and matching `}` is the last escaped `}` before `\\\"` \u2014 depth: `{` (1)... `}` (0). That works because normally the escaped JSON braces are balanced. The bug only manifests when a string literal inside contains an unbalanced brace.\\n\\nAlso, extract_balanced has a second issue: it doesn't skip the key properly, and treats the `{` inside the string. The fix: make brace counting skip characters inside JSON string literals, and handle escapes. Specifically for extract_balanced: after finding the first occurrence of iv_open... hmm, but careful: the open might legitimately be inside a string? For our uses: `tool_calls` value is a real array `[...]`, `arguments` value is an escaped string containing JSON. The first `{` after `\\\"arguments\\\"` is inside the string value but is the start of the embedded JSON. To be robust: after finding iv_open, scan and track depth, but also track whether we are inside a string literal (starting from... hmm, tricky because we start in the middle of a string).\\n\\nLet me think about the general fix for extract_balanced. The input `lv_rest = iv_json+lv_off` starts at `\\\"arguments\\\"`. Then we find first `{` \u2014 which lies inside a JSON string (the value). The characters after are: `\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"...`. If we track \\\"in-string\\\" naively from the `{`, we'd be out of string (since we started at `{`), then when we hit `\\\"` before `\\\\` ... hmm. Let's write the actual substring:\\n\\n`\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Z\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"... '{' ...\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`\\n\\nAfter `\\\"arguments\\\"` at offset, lv_rest = `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":...`. First `{` is at the position of `{\\\\\\\"name`. Start there. Now scan forward, tracking in-string state: current char is `{` \u2192 not a quote, depth 1. Next chars: `\\\\` `\\\"` `n` `a` `m` `e` `\\\\` `\\\"` `:` `\\\\` `\\\"` ... \\n\\nIf we process escapes properly: a `\\\\` escapes the next char. So `\\\\\\\"` is not a string delimiter. Then the `\\\"` before `:`? Let's list: `{` `\\\\` `\\\"` `n` `a` `m` `e` `\\\\` `\\\"` `:` `\\\\` `\\\"` `Z` `\\\\` `\\\"` `,` ... So all quotes are escaped, i.e., we never enter string state within the embedded JSON \u2014 braces inside the embedded JSON string values (like `'{'`) would then incorrectly count. Hmm, that's the opposite: the `'{'` is inside the embedded JSON's string value, which is escaped at the outer level? No wait \u2014 the `'{'` is a single quote char in ABAP source, so in the embedded JSON it's just `{` inside a JSON string value. At the outer level (the output item), the whole arguments value is a JSON string, so any `\\\"` inside is escaped as `\\\\\\\"`. The `{` character itself needs no escaping.\\n\\nSo within the outer scan: after `{` start, we need to track whether we're inside the outer string literal. We started at `{` which is inside the outer string (between the opening `\\\"` after `arguments:` and the closing `\\\"` before `,status`). Since we started mid-string without knowing, tracking won't work directly.\\n\\nBetter approach: fix at a different level. Instead of naive brace matching, extract the string value of \\\"arguments\\\" as a string (using extract_str which handles escaped quotes: `\\\"((?:[^\\\"\\\\\\\\]|\\\\\\\\.)*)\\\"`), then unescape it to get the JSON object `{...}` directly. That is much more robust!\\n\\nLook at parse_calls_chat:\\n\\n```abap\\nls-arguments = zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eunescape( zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eextract_balanced(\\n                 iv_json = lv_e iv_key = 'arguments' iv_open = '{' iv_close = '}' ) ).\\n```\\n\\nInteresting: extract_balanced returns `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":...}` (with the embedded quotes still escaped, plus in the bug case extra text), then unescape converts it to `{\\\"name\\\":...}`. Alternative:\\n\\n```abap\\nls-arguments = zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eunescape( zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eextract_str( iv_json = lv_e iv_name = 'arguments' ) ).\\n```\\n\\nextract_str uses PCRE `\\\"arguments\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"((?:[^\\\"\\\\\\\\]|\\\\\\\\.)*)\\\"` which correctly matches the full escaped string value regardless of braces. Then unescape gives the arguments JSON. \\n\\nBut wait \u2014 does open-abap / this system support PCRE? It's already used in extract_str, so yes.\\n\\nHmm, but there might be a reason they used extract_balanced: maybe the arguments in some transcript formats are NOT strings but objects? In chat completions API, arguments is always a string. In responses API, arguments is also a string. But maybe PIA's own replay format? Let's check other usages of these helpers and how tests call them. Also need to check zcl_pia_00_tool / registry to see how arguments are used downstream (they're parsed by the tools with extract_str probably).\\n\\nAlso split_entries has the same brace-counting weakness. In the bug, split_entries on the output array: item1's `'{'` causes the entry to swallow item2. With the fix at arguments extraction level, split_entries would still mis-split! Let's check: output array:\\n\\n```\\n[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{... '{' ...}\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"},{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",...}]\\n```\\n\\nsplit_entries counts raw braces: item1 opens `{` (depth 1), `'{'` \u2192 depth 2, item1's closing `}` \u2192 depth 1, then `,` then item2's `{` \u2192 depth 2 ... item2's closing `}` \u2192 depth 1... then the array's closing `]` \u2014 never reaches 0, so lv_end runs to len-1, entry1 = whole rest, second call lost. Yes! So split_entries must also be string-aware.\\n\\nSimilarly extract_balanced on 'output' key: finds `[`, depth counts `[`/`]` \u2014 brackets. The `'{'` doesn't affect bracket depth for the output array. But string literals with `[` or `]` inside would break it. In our bug case, the braces don't affect the bracket depth. OK.\\n\\nSo the core fixes:\\n1. split_entries must skip braces inside JSON string literals (handling `\\\\` escapes).\\n2. extract_balanced for arguments: better to use extract_str + unescape. But careful about what unescape expects. Currently: extract_balanced returns `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Z\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"METHOD m.\\\\\\\\n...\\\\\\\"}`? Wait \u2014 in the recorded/transmitted JSON, a newline inside the arguments string is `\\\\n` (two chars backslash-n) at the outer level... hmm, actually double-escaped? The arguments value is a JSON string containing JSON. The inner JSON has `\\\"source\\\":\\\"METHOD m.\\\\n...\\\"` where `\\\\n` is backslash-n (2 chars). At the outer level, backslash must be escaped: `\\\\\\\\n` (3 chars: backslash backslash n). extract_str captures `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":...,\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"METHOD m.\\\\\\\\n...\\\\\\\"}` raw, and unescape turns `\\\\\\\\` \u2192 `\\\\` and `\\\\\\\"` \u2192 `\\\"`, yielding valid inner JSON `{\\\"name\\\":..., \\\"source\\\":\\\"METHOD m.\\\\n...\\\"}` (with backslash-n, which downstream get parsed again as escapes by whatever consumes source? Actually the tool would then extract_str the source and unescape it). Fine \u2014 that's exactly what the current code produces in the non-buggy case: extract_balanced returns the same raw substring (up to the matching `}`), then unescape. So replacing extract_balanced with extract_str yields identical results in good cases and correct results in the buggy case.\\n\\nBut hold on \u2014 is there a case where arguments is sent as a real JSON object, not a string? In OpenAI chat completions and responses APIs, arguments is a string. PIA's replay format also uses strings (as seen in ev_assistant_raw: `'\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"' \\u0026\\u0026 escape(...) \\u0026\\u0026 '\\\"'`). The mock LLM \u2014 let me check ZCL_PIA_20_B_* or wherever the mock is; \\\"generic helpers (mock reuses them)\\\". Let me look at test classes of llm_http to see existing tests and conventions.\\n\\nAlso should I fix extract_balanced itself to be string-aware? It's used for:\\n- parse_calls_chat: tool_calls array `[...]`\\n- parse_calls_responses: output array `[...]`\\n- flatten_tools: function object `{...}`\\n- build_input_items: tool_calls array\\n- chat: output array\\n- Maybe other classes.\\n\\nFor arrays with `[` `]` delimiters, string-awareness would also be more robust. But the minimal, safe fix: make split_entries string-aware, and change the arguments extraction in both parse_calls_chat and parse_calls_responses to extract_str+unescape. Additionally make extract_balanced string-aware for robustness? Let's be careful: extract_balanced starts at the key and finds first open char \u2014 for `arguments`, that first `{` is inside a string, so string-awareness from that point is ill-defined (as analyzed). If I make extract_balanced string-aware and it's used for arguments, it would break (it would treat `\\\\\\\"` correctly as escaped, never toggling string state, and count the `'{'` brace \u2192 same wrong result). So better to switch arguments extraction to extract_str. But then extract_balanced string-awareness for `[` arrays: after `\\\"output\\\"` the first `[` is a real array start, and scanning with string-awareness (escaped quotes handled) would correctly skip `'{'` etc. That's a genuine improvement. But any risk? In build_input_items it's applied to ls-content which is PIA's own uniform format. Fine.\\n\\nHmm, wait. Let me also consider: does the bug also affect the `chat` path where ev_answer extraction happens? Not relevant.\\n\\nAlso there's `split_entries` used in flatten_tools and build_input_items, and possibly in the terminal/tools classes (ZCL_PIA_15_T_*). A string-aware split_entries is a general improvement; need to ensure correctness: when scanning, handle `\\\\` by skipping next char, and toggle in-string on `\\\"`.\\n\\nBut careful: split_entries input might be the *escaped* inner content? In flatten_tools, iv_tools_json is tools JSON from registry \u2014 real JSON. In build_input_items, the tool_calls array content \u2014 real JSON where arguments is an escaped string. String-aware scanning handles that: the `\\\"` of the arguments value opens string state, inside it `\\\\\\\"` are escaped, the `'{'` is inside string \u2192 skipped. \\n\\nBut wait \u2014 in build_input_items, split_entries is applied to the tool_calls array extracted from ls-content. And each entry then gets extract_str for 'arguments' \u2014 that's already extract_str there! Interesting: `zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eextract_str( iv_json = lv_e iv_name = 'arguments' )` \u2014 yes build_input_items already uses extract_str for arguments. So the convention exists.\\n\\nLet me now check the tools/executor to see how they parse arguments, and check the test classes of llm_http. Let me read the testclasses include of ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP and ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL.\\n\\nAlso need to find ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR test (known red) to not touch it \u2014 probably in JSON_UTIL testclasses.\\n\\nLet me read both test includes.\",\"type\":\"reasoning_text\"}],\"encrypted_content\":null,\"id\":\"rs_202610072214548828f66f13714d50\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"summary\":[],\"type\":\"reasoning\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"include\\\":\\\"testclasses\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7228021112641154397\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7228021112641154397\",\"name\":\"read_object\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"include\\\":\\\"testclasses\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7228021112641154396\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7228021112641154396\",\"name\":\"read_object\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"}],\"parallel_tool_calls\":true,\"previous_response_id\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":7034,\"input_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":1344},\"output_tokens\":3386,\"output_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":3335},\"total_tokens\":10420}}
{\"conversation_id\":\"\",\"created_at\":1791382558,\"error\":null,\"id\":\"resp_202610072215543909379e6d684d70\",\"incomplete_details\":null,\"instructions\":\"You are PIA (Pi-ABAP Agent), written in ABAP, running on the model glm-5.3 (z.ai) through backend OSG-STORE. If asked which model you are, say exactly that; never claim another model or vendor. Tools: outline(name), read_method(name, method, class), write_method(name, method, source - one METHOD block, class), read_object(name, include main|testclasses), write_source(name, source - FULL source, include), activate(name), run_tests(name). Rules: prefer outline, read_method and write_method for changing existing methods; use write_source only for new methods, changed declarations or a new test include, and then write the FULL source; read before write; always activate after write; if activation says publish pending, finish the turn and run_tests in the next turn. Answer briefly in the user language. Your own code: ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR (agent loop), ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (LLM client: builds requests, parses responses and tool calls), ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL (JSON helpers), ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION and ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION_STORE (conversation), ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY (tools), ZCL_PIA_15_T_* (the tools), ZCL_PIA_20_B_* (backends), ZCL_PIA_30_* (terminal, turns). Unit tests live in the testclasses include of a class.\",\"metadata\":null,\"model\":\"glm-5.3\",\"object\":\"response\",\"output\":[{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"include\\\":\\\"testclasses\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7228021284439844017\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7228021284439844017\",\"name\":\"read_object\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"}],\"parallel_tool_calls\":true,\"previous_response_id\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":7065,\"input_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":6976},\"output_tokens\":26,\"output_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":0},\"total_tokens\":7091}}
{\"conversation_id\":\"\",\"created_at\":1791382890,\"error\":null,\"id\":\"resp_20261007221558d4e914311c654e8d\",\"incomplete_details\":null,\"instructions\":\"You are PIA (Pi-ABAP Agent), written in ABAP, running on the model glm-5.3 (z.ai) through backend OSG-STORE. If asked which model you are, say exactly that; never claim another model or vendor. Tools: outline(name), read_method(name, method, class), write_method(name, method, source - one METHOD block, class), read_object(name, include main|testclasses), write_source(name, source - FULL source, include), activate(name), run_tests(name). Rules: prefer outline, read_method and write_method for changing existing methods; use write_source only for new methods, changed declarations or a new test include, and then write the FULL source; read before write; always activate after write; if activation says publish pending, finish the turn and run_tests in the next turn. Answer briefly in the user language. Your own code: ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR (agent loop), ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (LLM client: builds requests, parses responses and tool calls), ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL (JSON helpers), ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION and ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION_STORE (conversation), ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY (tools), ZCL_PIA_15_T_* (the tools), ZCL_PIA_20_B_* (backends), ZCL_PIA_30_* (terminal, turns). Unit tests live in the testclasses include of a class.\",\"metadata\":null,\"model\":\"glm-5.3\",\"object\":\"response\",\"output\":[{\"content\":[{\"text\":\"The test classes are in ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL's testclasses include. ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP has no test include.\\n\\nNow the bug: two tool calls in a responses-format body. The first call's arguments contain a single opening brace inside a string literal, e.g. `lv_open = '{'.` which in JSON would be escaped: `\\\"source\\\": \\\"METHOD m.\\\\n lv_open = '{'.\\\\nENDMETHOD.\\\"`. Wait \u2014 in JSON, `'{'` is a single quote, brace, single quote \u2014 the brace is NOT escaped in JSON (JSON only escapes `\\\"`, `\\\\` and control chars). So the raw body contains a literal `{` inside a string.\\n\\n`extract_balanced` with iv_key = 'arguments', iv_open = '{', iv_close = '}' counts braces without string-awareness. The `'{'.` inside the string adds +1 depth that never closes within arguments, so the balanced scan runs past the real end of arguments, swallowing `},\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"},{...second entry...}` until some later brace brings depth to 0. Hence arguments arrive with extra text `,\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` and the second call is lost (split_entries gets fewer entries / wrong boundaries).\\n\\nSimilarly split_entries has the same string-blindness for braces inside strings; but in the reported scenario the arguments braces cause the issue. Actually let's trace fully.\\n\\nExample body (responses format):\\n\\n```\\n{\\\"output\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"method\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"M\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"METHOD m.\\\\n lv_open = '{'.\\\\n ENDMETHOD.\\\\\\\"}\\\"},{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"run_tests\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\"}\\\"}], ...}\\n```\\n\\nNote: arguments is a JSON string; inside it, `{` from the ABAP literal is a raw `{` within the escaped string. Wait, in the arguments string, braces... `arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...}\\\"`. The inner JSON braces are escaped? No! In the responses API, arguments is a string containing JSON: `\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"x\\\\\\\"}\\\"`. The braces inside the string are NOT escaped (JSON doesn't escape braces). So the raw body contains `arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"method\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"M\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"METHOD m.\\\\n  lv_open = '{'.\\\\nENDMETHOD.\\\\\\\"}\\\"`.\\n\\nextract_balanced(iv_json=entry, iv_key='arguments', iv_open='{', iv_close='}'):\\n- finds `\\\"arguments\\\"` at offset; then finds first `{` after that \u2014 that's the `{` of `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...` at position of the escaped brace... wait `\\\\\\\"` then `{`. The first `{` is the opening of the inner JSON object. depth=1.\\n- Scan: `\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...` then `lv_open = '` then `{` \u2192 depth=2. Then `'.\\\\nENDMETHOD.\\\\\\\"}` \u2192 `}` \u2192 depth=1. Then `\\\"` closing arguments string, then `}` (end of entry 1 object) \u2192 depth=0. Stop. So arguments = `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...ENDMETHOD.\\\\\\\"}` plus `\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\"...`? Let's recount.\\n\\nEntry 1 raw: `{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"method\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"M\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"METHOD m.\\\\n lv_open = '{'.\\\\nENDMETHOD.\\\\\\\"}\\\"}`\\n\\nAfter `\\\"arguments\\\"`, rest = `:\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...\\\\\\\"}\\\"}`. First `{` at the inner object start. depth=1.\\nWalk: ... `lv_open = '{` \u2192 depth=2 ... `ENDMETHOD.\\\\\\\"}` \u2192 the `}` there closes the inner object \u2192 depth=1. Then `\\\"` (closing quote of the arguments string), then `}` \u2014 end of entry object \u2192 depth=0 \u2192 stop.\\n\\nSo rv_ = from first `{` to that final `}` = `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...lv_open = '{'.\\\\nENDMETHOD.\\\\\\\"}\\\"}` \u2014 hmm, that includes the closing quote `\\\"` and the final `}`. Wait the scan stops at the `}` that brings depth to 0, which is the entry-closing brace, so rv_ includes everything up to and including that brace: `{...\\\\\\\"}\\\"` + `}`. That includes `\\\"` before `}`. Hmm.\\n\\nActually the reported symptom: \\\"arguments arrive with extra text after the JSON (`,` `\\\"`call_id`\\\"\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"})\\\". That suggests the extra text is `,\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"` \u2014 hmm, that's fields AFTER arguments within the entry. That matches a different field ordering: e.g. `{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"...\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`. Then the balanced scan overshoots past the real closing of arguments, includes `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"` and stops... where? Let's trace: entry = `{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"w\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"... '{' ...\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`.\\n\\nAfter `\\\"arguments\\\"`, first `{` = inner object open, depth=1. `'{` \u2192 depth=2. Then `...\\\\\\\"}` \u2192 depth=1 (the inner object's real closing brace). Then `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"` \u2014 no braces \u2014 then final `}` of the entry \u2192 depth=0 \u2192 stop. So arguments = `{\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\"...'{'...\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` \u2014 exactly the reported extra text!\\n\\nBut wait \u2014 how does split_entries handle the entry array with the `{` inside a string? split_entries is also brace-counting: entry 1 starts at first `{` depth=1; the inner `'{` \u2192 depth 2; the inner close \u2192 1; entry close \u2192 0. So entry 1 is correctly bounded (since the spurious `{` is balanced by... hmm, in this case the string contains only one `{` and no `}`, so depth goes +1 inside the string and the real inner-object `}` brings it back... net effect: entry 1 ends at its true end? Let's check: depth counts: `{` entry open (1), `\\\\\\\"{` inner obj (2), `'` `{` (3), inner obj close `}` (2), entry close `}` (1)... no wait.\\n\\nEntry 1: `{\\\"type\\\":...,\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"m '{'.\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`\\nBraces: pos0 `{` \u2192 depth1. `{` of `\\\\\\\"{` \u2192 2. `{` inside string `'{'` \u2192 3. `}` closing inner obj \u2192 2. Final `}` \u2192 1. Never reaches 0! So split_entries runs into entry 2: `,` then `{` \u2192 2... entry 2's inner `\\\\\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...\\\\\\\"}` braces: `{`\u21923, `}`\u21922, then final `}` of entry 2 \u2192 1. Still not 0! Then continues to end of array; lv_depth \\u003e 0 at end \u2192 lv_end = len-1, so the WHOLE array becomes ONE entry. Then extract on that single entry: id = first \\\"call_id\\\" = c1; name = first \\\"name\\\" = write_method; arguments = balanced-scan overshoot as computed = includes `,\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` and also entry 2? Let's see: after `\\\"arguments\\\"` in the mega-entry, first `{` = inner obj of entry1 \u2192 depth1; `'{'`\u21922; inner close\u21921; then `, \\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` \u2192 entry1's close \u2192 0 \u2192 stop. So arguments = entry1's arguments + `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`. And the second call is lost entirely. EXACTLY the reported symptom. \\n\\nSo the root cause: `split_entries` and `extract_balanced` in ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL count braces/brackets without skipping characters inside JSON string literals. A single unbalanced `{` inside a string value (perfectly legal JSON, since JSON doesn't escape braces) breaks both.\\n\\nFix: make both methods string-aware: when scanning, skip over quoted strings (respecting `\\\\\\\"` escapes). This is a generic fix in JSON_UTIL, plus tests.\\n\\nBut careful: changing split_entries/extract_balanced behavior might break existing tests, e.g. `balanced_unclosed` expects tail `{\\\"x\\\":1` for unclosed input \u2014 that stays same. `balanced_no_open_bracket` \u2014 same. The chat parse path also uses these; string-awareness only improves.\\n\\nAlso `extract_balanced` should probably only consider an opening delimiter that is not inside a string \u2014 the initial FIND of iv_open could find a `{` inside a string too. E.g. key 'arguments' followed by `\\\":\\\"...` \u2014 first `{` might be inside... in the responses case the first `{` after `\\\"arguments\\\"` is the inner object's opening \u2014 fine. But to be robust, when searching for the opening delimiter we should skip strings as well. Let's implement a helper scan that is string-aware.\\n\\nPlan:\\n\\n1. Write a red test in ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL testclasses? Or better: the bug is about parse_calls_responses losing the second call. Best reproducer: a unit test calling zcl_pia_00_llm_http=\\u003eparse_calls_responses with a body containing two function_call outputs, the first with `'{'.` inside arguments. That test belongs in the LLM_HTTP test include (currently empty \u2014 I'll create one with write_source include testclasses). But the fix will be in JSON_UTIL; maybe also add tests there for split_entries/extract_balanced string-awareness. Keep focused: add the reproduction test in LLM_HTTP testclasses (parse_calls_responses_two_calls_brace_in_string) and maybe two supporting tests in JSON_UTIL. The user asks: \\\"write an ABAP Unit test that reproduces it and is red, then fix the code and show the tests green.\\\"\\n\\nApproach: \\n- Turn 1: add test to LLM_HTTP testclasses (write_source include testclasses, full source), activate, run tests \u2192 red.\\n- Turn 2: fix JSON_UTIL split_entries + extract_balanced (write_method for each), activate, run tests for both classes \u2192 green (except ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR which stays red).\\n\\nNote: run_tests runs on published/active code; after activate there may be a \\\"publish pending\\\" \u2014 then finish turn and run in next turn.\\n\\nAlso should I consider extract_str with PCRE \u2014 fine.\\n\\nLet me now write the fix.\\n\\nNew split_entries (string-aware):\\n\\n```abap\\n  METHOD split_entries.\\n    \\\" top-level {...} entries of a JSON array string; string-aware: braces and\\n    \\\" brackets inside \\\"...\\\" values (JSON does not escape them) are ignored,\\n    \\\" and every access is clamped\\n    DATA lv_len_arr TYPE i.\\n    lv_len_arr = strlen( iv_arr ).\\n    DATA lv_pos TYPE i VALUE 0.\\n    WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len_arr.\\n      FIND FIRST OCCURRENCE OF '{' IN iv_arr+lv_pos MATCH OFFSET DATA(lv_off).\\n      IF sy-subrc \\u003c\\u003e 0. EXIT. ENDIF.\\n      DATA lv_start TYPE i.\\n      lv_start = lv_pos + lv_off.\\n      IF lv_start \\u003e= lv_len_arr. EXIT. ENDIF.\\n      DATA lv_depth TYPE i.\\n      lv_depth = 1.\\n      DATA lv_end TYPE i.\\n      lv_end = lv_start.\\n      WHILE lv_end \\u003c lv_len_arr - 1 AND lv_depth \\u003e 0.\\n        lv_end = lv_end + 1.\\n        DATA lv_ch TYPE string.\\n        lv_ch = iv_arr+lv_end(1).\\n        IF lv_ch = '\\\"'.\\n          \\\" skip the string literal, \\\\\\\" is an escaped quote\\n          WHILE lv_end \\u003c lv_len_arr - 1.\\n            lv_end = lv_end + 1.\\n            IF iv_arr+lv_end(1) = '\\\\'.\\n              lv_end = lv_end + 1. \\\" skip escaped char\\n            ELSEIF iv_arr+lv_end(1) = '\\\"'.\\n              EXIT.\\n            ENDIF.\\n          ENDWHILE.\\n        ELSEIF lv_ch = '{'.\\n          lv_depth = lv_depth + 1.\\n        ELSEIF lv_ch = '}'.\\n          lv_depth = lv_depth - 1.\\n        ENDIF.\\n      ENDWHILE.\\n      ...\\n```\\n\\nCareful with clamping: inner while may push lv_end beyond len-1. Then after loops, if lv_depth \\u003e 0 \u2192 lv_end = lv_len_arr - 1. Also if `\\\\` at last position, lv_end+1 could exceed; the WHILE condition guards lv_end \\u003c lv_len_arr - 1 before increment, and after `lv_end = lv_end + 1` for the escaped char it could become = lv_len_arr \u2192 then outer while condition fails. OK.\\n\\nBut one subtlety: entry strings contain escaped quotes `\\\\\\\"`. When skipping a string: on `\\\\` skip next char. Good. Also the entry boundaries: split_entries is called on array content like `{\\\"a\\\":\\\"b\\\"},{\\\"c\\\":\\\"d\\\"}`. With string-awareness, commas inside strings are already irrelevant since we only count braces.\\n\\nNew extract_balanced (string-aware both for finding the opening delimiter and for depth counting):\\n\\n```abap\\n  METHOD extract_balanced.\\n    DATA lv_off TYPE i.\\n    DATA(lv_pat) = '\\\"' \\u0026\\u0026 iv_key \\u0026\\u0026 '\\\"'.\\n    FIND lv_pat IN iv_json MATCH OFFSET lv_off.\\n    IF sy-subrc \\u003c\\u003e 0. RETURN. ENDIF.\\n    DATA lv_rest TYPE string.\\n    lv_rest = iv_json+lv_off.\\n    DATA lv_len TYPE i.\\n    lv_len = strlen( lv_rest ).\\n    \\\" first opening delimiter outside a string literal\\n    DATA lv_i TYPE i.\\n    DATA lv_start TYPE i VALUE -1.\\n    WHILE lv_i \\u003c lv_len.\\n      DATA(lv_ch) = lv_rest+lv_i(1).\\n      IF lv_ch = '\\\"'.\\n        lv_i = skip_string(...)? \\n```\\n\\nSince JSON_UTIL has no private helpers, I'll inline the string-skipping loops (duplicate small code) or add a private static method skip_string. Adding a private method requires changing the definition \u2192 write_source full source of the class. The rules say: use write_source only for new methods/changed declarations \u2014 that's exactly the case if I add a helper. Simpler: keep it inline with local loops in both methods, using write_method only. I'll inline to minimize risk.\\n\\nLet me write extract_balanced:\\n\\n```abap\\n  METHOD extract_balanced.\\n    \\\" value of iv_key up to the matching closing delimiter; string-aware:\\n    \\\" delimiters inside \\\"...\\\" values (JSON does not escape { [ } ]) do not count,\\n    \\\" \\\\\\\" is an escaped quote and is skipped\\n    DATA lv_off TYPE i.\\n    DATA(lv_pat) = '\\\"' \\u0026\\u0026 iv_key \\u0026\\u0026 '\\\"'.\\n    FIND lv_pat IN iv_json MATCH OFFSET lv_off.\\n    IF sy-subrc \\u003c\\u003e 0. RETURN. ENDIF.\\n    DATA lv_rest TYPE string.\\n    lv_rest = iv_json+lv_off.\\n    DATA lv_len TYPE i.\\n    lv_len = strlen( lv_rest ).\\n    DATA lv_i TYPE i.\\n    DATA lv_start TYPE i VALUE -1.\\n    WHILE lv_i \\u003c lv_len AND lv_start \\u003c 0.\\n      DATA lv_ch TYPE string.\\n      lv_ch = lv_rest+lv_i(1).\\n      IF lv_ch = '\\\"'.\\n        lv_i = lv_i + 1.\\n        WHILE lv_i \\u003c lv_len.\\n          IF lv_rest+lv_i(1) = '\\\\'.\\n            lv_i = lv_i + 2.\\n          ELSEIF lv_rest+lv_i(1) = '\\\"'.\\n            lv_i = lv_i + 1.\\n            EXIT.\\n          ELSE.\\n            lv_i = lv_i + 1.\\n          ENDIF.\\n        ENDWHILE.\\n        \\\" hmm need to continue outer loop without lv_i increment again\\n      ELSEIF lv_ch = iv_open.\\n        lv_start = lv_i.\\n      ELSE.\\n        lv_i = lv_i + 1.\\n      ENDIF.\\n    ENDWHILE.\\n```\\n\\nCareful with loop increment structure. Better structure: a single loop with explicit CONTINUE handling. In ABAP, WHILE ... ENDWHILE with manual increments. Let me restructure:\\n\\n```abap\\n    DATA lv_i TYPE i VALUE 0.\\n    DATA lv_start TYPE i VALUE -1.\\n    WHILE lv_i \\u003c lv_len AND lv_start \\u003c 0.\\n      DATA lv_ch TYPE c.\\n      lv_ch = lv_rest+lv_i(1).\\n      IF lv_ch = '\\\"'.\\n        lv_i = lv_i + 1.\\n        WHILE lv_i \\u003c lv_len.\\n          IF lv_rest+lv_i(1) = '\\\\'.\\n            lv_i = lv_i + 2.\\n            CONTINUE.\\n          ENDIF.\\n          IF lv_rest+lv_i(1) = '\\\"'.\\n            lv_i = lv_i + 1.\\n            EXIT.\\n          ENDIF.\\n          lv_i = lv_i + 1.\\n        ENDWHILE.\\n        CONTINUE.  \\\" re-check outer condition\\n      ELSEIF lv_ch = iv_open.\\n        lv_start = lv_i.\\n      ENDIF.\\n      lv_i = lv_i + 1.\\n    ENDWHILE.\\n    IF lv_start \\u003c 0. RETURN. ENDIF.\\n```\\n\\nHmm, CONTINUE in ABAP WHILE: yes, CONTINUE works in loops. But `CONTINUE` then falls to ENDWHILE and re-evaluates condition \u2014 but I must not execute `lv_i = lv_i + 1` after string skip. With CONTINUE that's fine. But when lv_ch = iv_open we set lv_start and then increment lv_i \u2014 harmless since loop exits via condition lv_start \\u003c 0 false. Actually after setting lv_start, the `lv_i = lv_i + 1` executes then loop condition fails. Fine, lv_start unchanged.\\n\\nWait, there's a subtle issue with DATA declarations inside loops \u2014 allowed in ABAP (declared once). Fine, existing code does that.\\n\\nThen depth counting from lv_start with string-awareness:\\n\\n```abap\\n    DATA lv_depth TYPE i VALUE 1.\\n    DATA lv_pos TYPE i.\\n    lv_pos = lv_start.\\n    WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len - 1 AND lv_depth \\u003e 0.\\n      lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\n      DATA lv_c2 TYPE c.\\n      lv_c2 = lv_rest+lv_pos(1).\\n      IF lv_c2 = '\\\"'.\\n        lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\n        WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len.\\n          IF lv_rest+lv_pos(1) = '\\\\'.\\n            lv_pos = lv_pos + 2.\\n            CONTINUE.\\n          ENDIF.\\n          IF lv_rest+lv_pos(1) = '\\\"'.\\n            lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\n            EXIT.\\n          ENDIF.\\n          lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\n        ENDWHILE.\\n      ELSEIF lv_c2 = iv_open.\\n        lv_depth = lv_depth + 1.\\n      ELSEIF lv_c2 = iv_close.\\n        lv_depth = lv_depth - 1.\\n      ENDIF.\\n    ENDWHILE.\\n    DATA(lv_l2) = lv_pos - lv_start + 1.\\n    IF lv_start + lv_l2 \\u003e lv_len.\\n      lv_l2 = lv_len - lv_start.\\n    ENDIF.\\n    rv_ = lv_rest+lv_start(lv_l2).\\n```\\n\\nEdge: unclosed string at end \u2192 lv_pos may exceed lv_len-1 \u2192 then outer loop ends, lv_pos could be \\u003e lv_len-1; clamp lv_l2. Original code: `WHILE lv_pos \\u003c strlen(lv_rest) - 1` and then rv_ = lv_rest+lv_start(lv_len). If lv_pos overshoots, substring would dump. Add clamping like split_entries does. Note existing test `balanced_unclosed` expects `{\\\"x\\\":1` tail \u2014 with clamp, when depth\\u003e0 at end, lv_pos = lv_len-1... let's check: input `{\\\"o\\\":{\\\"x\\\":1`, key 'o', rest = `{\\\"o\\\":{\\\"x\\\":1`. lv_start = 4 (the inner `{`). depth scan: pos 5..end, no more braces, loop ends with lv_pos = lv_len-1 = 10 (len 11). rv_ = rest+4(7) = `{\\\"x\\\":1`. Same as before. Good.\\n\\nHmm wait: original code `WHILE lv_pos \\u003c strlen( lv_rest ) - 1 AND lv_depth \\u003e 0` \u2014 for the unclosed case original gives lv_pos = 10, len 11 \u2192 rv_ = lv_rest+4(7). Yes same.\\n\\nNow check the fixed behavior on the bug scenario for extract_balanced with key 'arguments': after `\\\"arguments\\\"`, rest = `:\\\"{\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":...`. lv_i=0 `:` not open; lv_i=1 `\\\"` \u2192 string skip: from lv_i=2: `\\\\` \u2192 skip 2 (the `\\\\\\\"`), ... until closing `\\\"` of the arguments string value. So the whole escaped string is skipped, including `'{`. Then `\\\"` closes at the end of value; then `,` etc.; no `{` found until... entry 2's `\\\\\\\"{`? Hmm wait: for the mega-entry... but with fixed split_entries, entries are split correctly now, so each entry is proper. Within entry 1, after `\\\"arguments\\\"`: rest = `:\\\"{\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\"... '{' ...\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`. String skip consumes the entire arguments value (from the `\\\"` at lv_i=1 to its closing `\\\"`), so the inner `{` of `\\\\\\\"{` \u2014 wait, that `{` is INSIDE the string value (arguments value is a string containing JSON). So string-skip jumps over it. After the value's closing quote, remaining: `,\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` \u2014 no `{` at all! lv_start stays -1 \u2192 RETURN, arguments = initial \u2192 ls-arguments empty \u2192 call with empty arguments! That's wrong too.\\n\\nHmm. Right: in the responses/chat API, `arguments` is a STRING containing JSON, so its opening `{` is inside a string literal. The current code relies on finding the first `{` after the key \u2014 which is inside the string \u2014 and then brace-counting raw. So making extract_balanced fully string-aware breaks argument extraction!\\n\\nSo the fix must be smarter. Options:\\n\\nA) Make extract_balanced string-aware only for the scanning part after the opening delimiter has been found naively (first iv_open after key), i.e. keep \\\"find first `{` after key\\\" as-is (it works because arguments value starts with `{`), but make depth counting string-aware EXCLUDING the outermost string context? That doesn't work either: the whole arguments value is one string; inside it braces must count (inner JSON), but braces from ABAP literals like `'{'` must not... but they're indistinguishable at the raw level \u2014 both are raw `{` inside the outer string. Wait no: inside the arguments string, the inner JSON's quotes are escaped (`\\\\\\\"`). So within the arguments value, string literals of the inner JSON appear as `\\\\\\\"...\\\\\\\"`. So a proper approach: treat the arguments value as: outer quote, then content until unescaped quote. Within content, parse as JSON where quotes are `\\\\\\\"`-escaped. Ugh. Two-level.\\n\\nSimpler robust approach: depth counting that treats BOTH `\\\"` and `\\\\\\\"` \u2014 hmm.\\n\\nAlternative: handle it at a different level. The real problem in the reported bug is split_entries losing the second entry, AND extract_balanced overshooting arguments. Let's think about what a string-aware scanner should do when it starts inside a string (the first `{` after `\\\"arguments\\\"` is inside the arguments string value):\\n\\nActually reconsider: maybe fix differently \u2014 extract_balanced should find the VALUE after the key properly: skip whitespace, expect `\\\"` (string value) or iv_open directly. If the value is a string, the balanced extraction should operate on the UNESCAPED content? No \u2014 callers do `unescape(extract_balanced(...))`. Hmm, chat: `ls-arguments = unescape(extract_balanced(iv_json=lv_e iv_key='arguments' iv_open='{' iv_close='}'))`. The arguments value is `\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":...}\\\"`. extract_balanced finds first `{` after `\\\"arguments\\\"` \u2014 inside the string \u2014 and counts raw braces until balance; then unescape turns `\\\\\\\"` into `\\\"`. So currently: arguments result = `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"x\\\\\\\"}` \u2192 unescaped \u2192 `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"x\\\"}`. OK.\\n\\nWith the `'{'.` case: the ABAP source inside arguments contains raw `{` (JSON doesn't escape it) \u2192 depth miscount \u2192 overshoot.\\n\\nA clean fix while keeping the API: make the depth scan skip over escaped-quote-delimited strings within the value. I.e., when scanning for balance starting at the first `{` after the key, we are inside a JSON string (the arguments value). Within that, inner strings are delimited by `\\\\\\\"` ... `\\\\\\\"`. So the scanner should treat `\\\\\\\"` as quote delimiters! I.e., a two-char sequence `\\\\\\\"` toggles in-string. Let's verify:\\n\\nArguments value raw: `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"METHOD m.\\\\n lv_open = '{'.\\\\nENDMETHOD.\\\\\\\"}`\\n\\nScan from `{`: depth 1. Then `\\\\\\\"` \u2192 enter inner-string mode until next `\\\\\\\"`. Inside `\\\\\\\"...\\\\\\\"` we skip `'{`? The source `METHOD m.\\\\n lv_open = '{'.` \u2014 within `\\\\\\\"`-delimited string. The `'` and `{` are raw chars between `\\\\\\\"` pairs. With `\\\\\\\"`-toggling: after `\\\\\\\"source` opens... sequence: `\\\\\\\"` toggles in-string ON at `\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"`: `\\\\\\\"` ON, `name`, `\\\\\\\"` OFF. `:` `\\\\\\\"` ON `ZCL_X` `\\\\\\\"` OFF, `,` `\\\\\\\"` ON `source` `\\\\\\\"` OFF ... wait the value `\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"METHOD...\\\\\\\"` \u2014 the whole source string is between `\\\\\\\"` and `\\\\\\\"`. Inside it, `lv_open = '{'.` is plain text in ON state \u2192 `{` skipped. \\n\\nAnd the final `}` after `\\\\\\\"}`: `\\\\\\\"` OFF then `}` \u2192 depth 0 \u2192 stop exactly at the right place. \\n\\nBut what about a source containing `\\\\\\\"` (escaped quote in the inner JSON, e.g. ABAP string literal with quotes escaped as `\\\\\\\"` by the model when it embeds JSON)? E.g. source contains `lv_s = 'he said \\\"hi\\\"'.` \u2014 when the model puts this into arguments, inner JSON: `{\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"... lv_s = 'he said \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"hi\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"'...\\\\\\\"}`. Raw body: `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` \u2014 sequence backslash backslash backslash quote. Toggling on every `\\\\\\\"` pair: `\\\\\\\\` \u2014 hmm my scanner looks at 2-char sequences: `\\\\\\\\` then `\\\\\\\"`. Scan char by char: at `\\\\` check next char: if next is `\\\"` \u2192 toggle. `\\\\\\\\` : first `\\\\` next is `\\\\` \u2192 not toggle, advance 2 (skip escaped char). Then `\\\\\\\"` \u2192 toggle. Correct per JSON unescaping semantics! Because within the arguments string, `\\\\\\\\` is an escaped backslash and `\\\\\\\"` is an escaped quote; the inner JSON strings are delimited by `\\\\\\\"`.\\n\\nSo the correct scanner inside the arguments value: treat `\\\\X` as skip-2; if X = `\\\"` it's a quote delimiter of the inner JSON \u2192 toggle in-string state (not skip-2? toggling means we advance 2 and flip state). Hmm wait \u2014 I need to distinguish: in the arguments-string context, `\\\\\\\"` is a quote CHARACTER (inner JSON string delimiter) after unescaping. So scanning raw: when we see `\\\\` followed by `\\\"`, that's an inner quote \u2192 toggle inner-string state, advance 2. When `\\\\` followed by anything else (including `\\\\`) \u2192 skip 2 (escaped char). When we see a bare `\\\"` (not preceded by `\\\\`)... that would be the closing quote of the whole arguments value \u2014 end of value; scanning should stop? For robustness, if we hit a bare `\\\"` while depth\\u003e0, we're leaving the value \u2014 could just continue scanning (current behavior) or stop. Bare quote inside arguments value with unbalanced braces... rare. Keep scanning but maybe treat as end of value and stop the loop \u2192 then rv_ = up to there. Hmm, safer to keep current lenient behavior: continue.\\n\\nBut hold on \u2014 this `\\\\\\\"`-toggling behavior is needed when the value after the key is a STRING (arguments case). When the value is a direct object/array (e.g. extract_balanced(iv_key='tool_calls', iv_open='[', ...) on body where tool_calls is a real array, or 'output' array), the scanning context is regular JSON: strings delimited by bare `\\\"`, and `\\\\\\\"` inside them is escaped quote. Two different contexts!\\n\\nGeneric solution that handles BOTH: a scanner where the in-string state is toggled by quote delimiters and `\\\\` escapes the next char. In regular JSON context: bare `\\\"` toggles; `\\\\\\\"` = escaped quote (skip 2, no toggle). In arguments-string context: `\\\\\\\"` toggles (inner quote), bare `\\\"` ends value.\\n\\nCan one rule handle both? Consider regular context string `\\\"a\\\\\\\"b\\\"`: chars `\\\"` (toggle ON), a, `\\\\` skip `\\\\\\\"`, b, `\\\"` toggle OFF. Good. Now arguments context, inner JSON `{\\\\\\\"a\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"b\\\\\\\"}`: if we apply the regular rule: `{` depth1... `\\\\` followed by `\\\"` \u2192 skip 2, no toggle. So `\\\\\\\"a` \u2014 `\\\\` `\\\"` skipped, then `a`, then `\\\\` `\\\"` skipped... no toggling ever happens; then `'{'` inside source counts. Bad for arguments context.\\n\\nSo context matters. Since extract_balanced is called with a key whose value may be either a string (arguments) or a direct structure (output/tool_calls), the method must detect: after the key, skip `:` and whitespace; if next char is `\\\"` AND the char after the opening `\\\"`-value... hmm, we can't know the value is JSON-in-string without unescaping.\\n\\nDetection heuristic: after key + `:`, if next non-space char is `\\\"` then the value is a string \u2192 scan mode \\\"inner\\\": first find `{`/`[` after that quote (the unescaped-first-char heuristic stays: find first iv_open after key), and use `\\\\\\\"`-toggling + treat bare `\\\"` as value end? But careful: iv_open could legitimately appear before... In practice arguments always starts with `{`. For the direct-structure case (output/tool_calls arrays), next char after `:` is `[` or `{` \u2192 mode \\\"outer\\\": bare `\\\"` toggles strings.\\n\\nImplement extract_balanced:\\n\\n```\\nFIND key \u2192 lv_off.\\nlv_rest = iv_json+lv_off.\\n\\\" find position after ':' \\nfind ':' in lv_rest after key... \\n```\\n\\nHmm, simpler: after key match, scan forward: skip until we pass `:` then whitespace. Then look at char:\\n- if `\\\"` \u2192 mode inner: find first iv_open AFTER that position (naive FIND as today). Scan with inner rules.\\n- elseif iv_open \u2192 mode outer: scan with outer rules.\\n\\nInner rules scan (starting at the opening delimiter, depth=1):\\n- `\\\\` + any char: if next = `\\\"` \u2192 toggle inner-string, advance 2; else advance 2 (escaped char, includes `\\\\\\\\`, `\\\\n` etc.)\\n- bare `\\\"`: end of the arguments value \u2192 stop scanning (value ended without balance \u2192 malformed). Return what we have? For safety, stop and return scanned portion (current code would return tail). I'll stop the loop; rv_ = up to lv_pos.\\n- iv_open not in inner-string \u2192 depth+1; iv_close not in inner-string \u2192 depth-1.\\n\\nWait, subtle: in inner mode, should depth count braces only outside inner strings? Yes: `\\\\\\\"...\\\\\\\"`-delimited strings' braces must not count (that's the bug fix), and `'{'` sits inside a `\\\\\\\"...\\\\\\\"` string.\\n\\nBut careful: the ABAP literal `'{'` \u2014 is it inside `\\\\\\\"...\\\\\\\"`? In the arguments, source value: `\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"METHOD m.\\\\n lv_open = '{'.\\\\nENDMETHOD.\\\\\\\"`. Between `\\\\\\\"` after `source` and the final `\\\\\\\"`, yes, `'{'` is inside. \\n\\nBut what about a source containing an ABAP string literal with a doublequote, e.g. `lv_s = \\\"hi\\\".`? The model escapes it as `\\\\\\\"hi\\\\\\\"` in the inner JSON \u2192 raw: `lv_s = \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`? No wait. Levels: raw HTTP body \u2192 arguments string value \u2192 inner JSON \u2192 source string value (inner-inner). Source contains `\\\"` \u2192 inner JSON escapes to `\\\\\\\"` \u2192 raw body shows `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`? Let's be careful: arguments value (raw body chars) = `{\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"...\\\\\\\"}`. Inside, `\\\\\\\"` represents `\\\"` after one unescape. If the source itself contains `\\\"` (ABAP `lv_s = \\\"hi\\\"` \u2014 not valid ABAP anyway, ABAP uses `'`), inner JSON must escape it: `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` in raw? The source string value in inner JSON is delimited by `\\\"` (which appears as `\\\\\\\"` in raw). A literal `\\\"` in source must be escaped in inner JSON as `\\\\\\\"` which in raw is `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` (backslash-backslash-backslash-quote? no): raw shows the inner JSON escaped once more: inner `\\\\\\\"` \u2192 raw `\\\\\\\\` + `\\\\\\\"` = `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`. Yes 4 chars: `\\\\`,`\\\\`,`\\\\`,`\\\"`.\\n\\nMy inner scanner on `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`: at first `\\\\`, next is `\\\\` \u2192 not `\\\"` \u2192 advance 2 (consumes `\\\\\\\\`). Then at `\\\\`, next is `\\\"` \u2192 toggle inner-string, advance 2. Correct: `\\\\\\\\` = escaped backslash (content), `\\\\\\\"` = quote delimiter in inner JSON. \\n\\nAlso single quotes `'` are never special in JSON \u2014 correct, they're just content; the bug arises only because they wrapped `{`. Fine.\\n\\nOuter rules scan (current-ish but string-aware): bare `\\\"` toggles string; inside string `\\\\` skips next char; delimiters count only outside strings. This fixes split_entries-like counting for direct arrays/objects (output, tool_calls).\\n\\nNow split_entries: operates on array-of-objects text where entries' string values may contain unbalanced braces (arguments!). With outer rules + bare-quote toggling: entry 1: `{` depth1, ... `\\\"arguments\\\"` ... `\\\"` toggles ON at value start; inside: `\\\\\\\"` \u2192 skip 2 \u2014 good (escaped quotes of inner JSON), `'{'` skipped since in-string, value end `\\\"` toggles OFF; then `}` depth 0. Correct split! And commas/braces inside the arguments value are all inside the outer string \u2192 ignored. \\n\\nSo: split_entries = outer-rules scanner. extract_balanced = detect inner (value is string) vs outer (value is direct) and use respective rules.\\n\\nEdge check for existing tests with new extract_balanced:\\n- balanced_object: `{\\\"o\\\":{\\\"x\\\":1},\\\"y\\\":2}` key o \u2192 after key: `\\\"o\\\"` at off 1... FIND `\\\"o\\\"` in json \u2192 match at offset 1. lv_rest = `\\\"o\\\":{\\\"x\\\":1},\\\"y\\\":2}`. After key rest starts with `\\\"o\\\"` \u2014 hmm, my algorithm: from lv_rest start, the key pattern was already matched at position 0 of lv_rest. Then I skip the key itself: lv_rest begins with `\\\"o\\\"`. So after key (2+len chars), expect `:`, whitespace, then value char. Next char after `\\\"o\\\"` is `:` then `{` \u2192 outer mode, scan: `{` depth1, `\\\"` ON, x, `\\\"` OFF, `:1`, `}` depth0 \u2192 `{\\\"x\\\":1}`. \u2713\\n- balanced_nested_and_array: \u2713 similar. `[1,[2,3]]`: no strings, fine.\\n- balanced_missing_key: FIND fails \u2192 initial. \u2713\\n- balanced_no_open_bracket: `{\\\"o\\\":\\\"txt\\\"}` key o \u2192 after `:` value char is `\\\"` \u2192 inner mode: find first `{` after that position \u2014 none in `\\\"txt\\\"}` \u2192 lv_start = -1 \u2192 return initial. \u2713 (expected empty)\\n- balanced_unclosed: `{\\\"o\\\":{\\\"x\\\":1` \u2192 outer mode from inner `{`, depth never 0, ends at len-1 \u2192 tail `{\\\"x\\\":1`. \u2713\\n\\nNew bug case (responses entry): key 'arguments', value string \u2192 inner mode. Let me simulate precisely with the entry:\\n\\n`{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"METHOD m. lv_open = '{'. ENDMETHOD.\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`\\n\\nHmm wait \u2014 where does FIND `\\\"arguments\\\"` match? The pattern `\\\"arguments\\\"` \u2014 also `\\\"call_id\\\"` etc. don't match. Note: FIND is case-sensitive; also the pattern could match inside a string value (e.g. source containing `\\\"arguments\\\"`), pre-existing limitation, ignore.\\n\\nlv_rest = `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":...}\\\" , \\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`. Skip key (11 chars: `\\\"arguments\\\"`), then `:`, then value char `\\\"` \u2192 inner mode. Find first `{` after the value start \u2192 the `{` of `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"`. Scan inner-mode:\\n- `{` depth1\\n- `\\\\` next `\\\"` \u2192 toggle ON, adv2\\n- `name` ... `\\\\` `\\\"` \u2192 toggle OFF, adv2\\n- `:` `\\\\` `\\\"` \u2192 ON... `ZCL_X` `\\\\` `\\\"` OFF\\n- `,` `\\\\` `\\\"` ON `source` `\\\\` `\\\"` OFF `:` `\\\\` `\\\"` ON `METHOD m. lv_open = '` `{` skipped (in-string!) `'. ENDMETHOD.` `\\\\` `\\\"` OFF\\n- `}` \u2192 depth 0 \u2192 STOP. lv_pos at that `}`.\\n- rv_ = `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"METHOD m. lv_open = '{'. ENDMETHOD.\\\\\\\"}` \u2713 \\n\\nThen unescape \u2192 `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"METHOD m. lv_open = '{'. ENDMETHOD.\\\"}` \u2713 Perfect, exactly right.\\n\\nAnd split_entries with outer rules on the output array:\\n\\n`[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",...,\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...'{'...\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"},{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"run_tests\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\"}\\\"}]`\\n\\nEntry1: `{` d1; `\\\"type\\\"` ON/OFF...; `\\\"arguments\\\"` then `\\\"` ON; inside: `\\\\\\\"` \u2192 skip2 each; `'{'` ignored; ... value close `\\\"` OFF; `\\\"status\\\"` ON OFF; `}` d0 \u2192 entry1 complete \u2713. Entry2 similar \u2713. Two calls parsed. Bug fixed.\\n\\nChat path also benefits: body `{\\\"tool_calls\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{...'{'...}\\\"},...]}` \u2014 split via outer rules \u2713; arguments via inner mode \u2713.\\n\\nNow also `build_input_items` and others use extract_str \u2014 fine.\\n\\nPotential edge: extract_balanced inner mode when value does NOT start with `{` after the opening quote but contains `{` later, e.g. arguments = `\\\"see { brace\\\"`? find first `{` after key \u2192 inside value \u2192 scan from there; bare `\\\"` \u2192 value end \u2192 stop, rv_ = `{ brace`. Weird but pre-existing-ish. Fine.\\n\\nAlso inner mode: what if while scanning in-string (inner) we hit bare `\\\"` (end of arguments value) with depth\\u003e0 \u2192 unbalanced braces inside value; stop and return scanned part. I'll implement: IF lv_c = `\\\"` (bare) \u2192 EXIT outer loop (keep rv_ up to here). Reasonable.\\n\\nNow implementation detail: I need mode detection. Code:\\n\\n```abap\\n  METHOD extract_balanced.\\n    \\\" value of iv_key up to the matching closing delimiter.\\n    \\\" outer values (direct object/array): \\\"...\\\" literals are skipped, \\\\\\\" is escaped\\n    \\\" string values (JSON-in-a-string like arguments): the inner quotes are \\\\\\\" and\\n    \\\" braces there ('{' in an ABAP literal) must not count - the old raw brace\\n    \\\" counting ran past the end of the value and swallowed the rest of the body\\n    DATA lv_off TYPE i.\\n    DATA(lv_pat) = '\\\"' \\u0026\\u0026 iv_key \\u0026\\u0026 '\\\"'.\\n    FIND lv_pat IN iv_json MATCH OFFSET lv_off.\\n    IF sy-subrc \\u003c\\u003e 0. RETURN. ENDIF.\\n    DATA lv_rest TYPE string.\\n    lv_rest = iv_json+lv_off.\\n    DATA lv_len TYPE i.\\n    lv_len = strlen( lv_rest ).\\n    \\\" position after the key and the colon\\n    DATA lv_i TYPE i.\\n    lv_i = strlen( lv_pat ).\\n    WHILE lv_i \\u003c lv_len AND ( lv_rest+lv_i(1) = ` ` OR lv_rest+lv_i(1) = ':' ).\\n      lv_i = lv_i + 1.\\n    ENDWHILE.\\n    IF lv_i \\u003e= lv_len. RETURN. ENDIF.\\n    DATA lv_inner TYPE abap_bool.\\n    lv_inner = boolc( lv_rest+lv_i(1) = '\\\"' ).\\n    \\\" first opening delimiter at/after the value start\\n    FIND FIRST OCCURRENCE OF iv_open IN SECTION LENGTH lv_len - lv_i OF lv_rest+lv_i MATCH OFFSET DATA(lv_rel).\\n```\\n\\nHmm, `IN SECTION` syntax: `FIND ... IN SECTION [LENGTH l] [OFFSET o] OF`. Easier: lv_sub = lv_rest+lv_i, then FIND in lv_sub. Or just do a manual char loop for finding the opening delimiter:\\n\\n```abap\\n    DATA lv_start TYPE i VALUE -1.\\n    DATA lv_j TYPE i.\\n    lv_j = lv_i.\\n    WHILE lv_j \\u003c lv_len AND lv_start \\u003c 0.\\n      IF lv_rest+lv_j(1) = iv_open.\\n        lv_start = lv_j.\\n      ENDIF.\\n      lv_j = lv_j + 1.\\n    ENDWHILE.\\n    IF lv_start \\u003c 0. RETURN. ENDIF.\\n```\\n\\nThen scanning:\\n\\n```abap\\n    DATA lv_depth TYPE i VALUE 1.\\n    DATA lv_pos TYPE i.\\n    DATA lv_in TYPE abap_bool VALUE abap_false.\\n    lv_pos = lv_start.\\n    IF lv_inner = abap_true.\\n      \\\" inside the string value: inner quotes appear as \\\\\\\"\\n      lv_in = abap_true. \\\" wait...\\n```\\n\\nHmm \u2014 in inner mode, when scanning starts at `{` (which is inside the outer string value), we are NOT inside an inner string initially (the `{` of the inner JSON object). Inner strings toggle on `\\\\\\\"`. So initial lv_in = false. In outer mode also false. Same initial state; the difference is only the quote rule:\\n\\n- outer mode: bare `\\\"` toggles; `\\\\X` (X\u2260`\\\"`? no\u2014) inside string: `\\\\` skips next char. Outside string in outer mode, `\\\\` shouldn't appear.\\n- inner mode: `\\\\X`: if X = `\\\"` toggle in-string and skip both; else skip both (escaped char). Bare `\\\"` = end of the whole value \u2192 stop scanning.\\n\\nSo unified loop:\\n\\n```abap\\n    WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len - 1 AND lv_depth \\u003e 0.\\n      lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\n      DATA lv_ch TYPE string.\\n      lv_ch = lv_rest+lv_pos(1).\\n      IF lv_ch = '\\\\'.\\n        \\\" escaped character: in a string value \\\\\\\" is a quote of the embedded JSON\\n        IF lv_pos + 1 \\u003c lv_len AND lv_rest+lv_pos(1)... \\n```\\n\\ncareful: need lv_rest+lv_pos+1(1) \u2192 offset lv_pos+1. Write:\\n\\n```abap\\n      IF lv_ch = `\\\\` AND lv_pos + 1 \\u003c lv_len.\\n        IF lv_inner = abap_true AND lv_rest+lv_pos+1(1) = `\\\"`.  \\\" hmm substring syntax\\n```\\n\\nABAP offset arithmetic: `lv_rest+lv_pos+1(1)` is not valid; need `lv_rest+lv_next(1)` with lv_next = lv_pos+1. I'll compute lv_next.\\n\\nLet me write it cleanly:\\n\\n```abap\\n    DATA lv_depth TYPE i VALUE 1.\\n    DATA lv_pos TYPE i.\\n    DATA lv_in TYPE abap_bool VALUE abap_false.\\n    DATA lv_next TYPE i.\\n    lv_pos = lv_start.\\n    WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len - 1 AND lv_depth \\u003e 0.\\n      lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\n      DATA lv_ch TYPE string.\\n      lv_ch = lv_rest+lv_pos(1).\\n      IF lv_ch = `\\\\`.\\n        lv_next = lv_pos + 1.\\n        IF lv_next \\u003c lv_len.\\n          IF lv_inner = abap_true AND lv_rest+lv_next(1) = `\\\"`.\\n            lv_in = boolc( lv_in = abap_false ).   \\\" embedded JSON string toggles\\n          ENDIF.\\n          lv_pos = lv_next.  \\\" skip the escaped character\\n        ENDIF.\\n        CONTINUE.\\n      ENDIF.\\n      IF lv_ch = `\\\"`.\\n        IF lv_inner = abap_true.\\n          \\\" the closing quote of the string value: value is over\\n          lv_pos = lv_pos - 1.\\n          EXIT.\\n        ENDIF.\\n        lv_in = boolc( lv_in = abap_false ).\\n        CONTINUE.\\n      ENDIF.\\n      IF lv_in = abap_false.\\n        IF lv_ch = iv_open.\\n          lv_depth = lv_depth + 1.\\n        ELSEIF lv_ch = iv_close.\\n          lv_depth = lv_depth - 1.\\n        ENDIF.\\n      ENDIF.\\n    ENDWHILE.\\n    DATA lv_l2 TYPE i.\\n    lv_l2 = lv_pos - lv_start + 1.\\n    IF lv_start + lv_l2 \\u003e lv_len.\\n      lv_l2 = lv_len - lv_start.\\n    ENDIF.\\n    IF lv_l2 \\u003e 0.\\n      rv_ = lv_rest+lv_start(lv_l2).\\n    ENDIF.\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\n```\\n\\nWait \u2014 in inner mode, when hitting the closing bare `\\\"` of the value with depth still \\u003e 0 (unbalanced inside value, like our bug's original input?), we back up (lv_pos-1) and exit, returning the scanned part without the quote. Reasonable. Hmm, but should depth\\u003e0 with value-end just stop? yes.\\n\\nAlso in outer mode when a `\\\\` appears outside a string (malformed), we just skip 2 \u2014 harmless.\\n\\nAlso: `IF lv_ch = `\\\\` AND lv_pos + 1 \\u003c lv_len` \u2014 if `\\\\` is the last char, falls through to quote/delimiter checks, not `\\\\`, not quote, in outer mode not in-string \u2192 checks open/close \u2192 no. fine.\\n\\nboolc for toggle: `lv_in = boolc( lv_in = abap_false )` \u2014 yields abap_true if currently false. OK.\\n\\nHmm, one more: in inner mode, do we even need lv_in toggling by `\\\\\\\"`? Yes \u2014 braces between `\\\\\\\"` and `\\\\\\\"` must not count.\\n\\nBut WAIT: there's a subtlety in inner mode with `\\\\\\\\` followed by...: e.g. raw `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` handled: `\\\\` next `\\\\` \u2192 not quote \u2192 skip 2 \u2192 now at `\\\\` next `\\\"` \u2192 toggle. Good. What about `\\\\n` inside \u2014 skip 2, no toggle. Good.\\n\\nEdge: inner JSON string containing escaped backslash at end `\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"a\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` ... fine.\\n\\nNow split_entries with outer rules:\\n\\n```abap\\n  METHOD split_entries.\\n    \\\" top-level {...} entries of a JSON array string; string-aware: braces inside\\n    \\\" \\\"...\\\" values (JSON does not escape them, e.g. '{' in an ABAP literal) do not\\n    \\\" count, \\\\\\\" is an escaped quote; every access is clamped\\n    DATA lv_len_arr TYPE i.\\n    lv_len_arr = strlen( iv_arr ).\\n    DATA lv_pos TYPE i VALUE 0.\\n    WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len_arr.\\n      FIND FIRST OCCURRENCE OF '{' IN iv_arr+lv_pos MATCH OFFSET DATA(lv_off).\\n      IF sy-subrc \\u003c\\u003e 0. EXIT. ENDIF.\\n      DATA lv_start TYPE i.\\n      lv_start = lv_pos + lv_off.\\n      IF lv_start \\u003e= lv_len_arr. EXIT. ENDIF.\\n      DATA lv_depth TYPE i.\\n      lv_depth = 1.\\n      DATA lv_end TYPE i.\\n      DATA lv_in TYPE abap_bool VALUE abap_false.\\n      lv_end = lv_start.\\n      WHILE lv_end \\u003c lv_len_arr - 1 AND lv_depth \\u003e 0.\\n        lv_end = lv_end + 1.\\n        DATA lv_ch TYPE string.\\n        lv_ch = iv_arr+lv_end(1).\\n        IF lv_ch = `\\\"`.\\n          lv_in = boolc( lv_in = abap_false ).\\n        ELSEIF lv_ch = `\\\\` AND lv_in = abap_true AND lv_end + 1 \\u003c lv_len_arr.\\n          lv_end = lv_end + 1.  \\\" escaped character inside the string\\n        ELSEIF lv_in = abap_false.\\n          IF lv_ch = '{'.\\n            lv_depth = lv_depth + 1.\\n          ELSEIF lv_ch = '}'.\\n            lv_depth = lv_depth - 1.\\n          ENDIF.\\n        ENDIF.\\n      ENDWHILE.\\n      IF lv_depth \\u003e 0.\\n        lv_end = lv_len_arr - 1.\\n      ENDIF.\\n      ... (rest unchanged)\\n```\\n\\nNote: `DATA lv_in ... VALUE abap_false` inside the outer WHILE \u2014 DATA with VALUE inside a loop: the VALUE only applies first time? In ABAP, DATA declarations are processed once; the VALUE addition... For static data declared in a loop, initialization happens once at declaration processing? Actually local variables declared with DATA inside loops: the declaration is hoisted; VALUE is set... hmm, in ABAP the DATA statement inside a loop is executed each iteration? No \u2014 DATA statements are not executable statements; they're declarations, processed once upon first pass? The documentation: \\\"DATA ... If declared in a loop, it's initialized once\\\"? Actually inline DATA in loops: the variable retains its value across iterations (it's like a static declaration in the method). Hmm, no: local variables in a procedure are created once per call; DATA with VALUE initializes at declaration which happens at first pass... To be safe, declare lv_in before the outer loop and reset it per entry: after finding lv_start, set lv_in = abap_false.\\n\\nWait, actually entries start at `{` which is outside any string, so per-entry reset lv_in = abap_false at entry start. I'll declare before the outer WHILE and set at each entry start.\\n\\nHmm, but careful: escaped-quote handling in split_entries: in outer mode strings are bare-quoted; `\\\\\\\"` inside \u2192 when lv_in true and ch = `\\\\`, skip next. But also when lv_in false and ch = `\\\\` \u2014 shouldn't happen in valid JSON at top level of an array of objects... except inside arguments values \u2014 but those are within bare-quoted strings (lv_in true). OK.\\n\\nBut there's a catch: within the arguments string (lv_in = true), the inner JSON's `\\\\\\\"` sequences: ch = `\\\\` with lv_in true \u2192 skip next char (the `\\\"`). Good \u2014 the inner quotes don't toggle. And a `\\\"` preceded by... e.g. arguments value ends: `...\\\\\\\"}\\\"` then `}`: sequence `\\\\`,`\\\"`,`}`,`\\\"`? No: arguments value raw: `\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...}\\\"`. Chars: `\\\"` (toggle ON), `{`, `\\\\`\u2192skip `\\\"`, `name`, `\\\\`\u2192skip `\\\"`, ..., `\\\\`\u2192skip `\\\"`, `}` (in string, not counted), `\\\"` (toggle OFF), then `,` or `}` counted. \u2713\\n\\nAnd `'{'` inside: ch=`{` with lv_in true \u2192 not counted (ELSEIF lv_in = abap_false fails). \u2713 \\n\\nNow: FIND FIRST OCCURRENCE OF '{' to locate the next entry start \u2014 if a previous value ended weirdly... lv_pos = lv_end + 1 continues after a complete entry; fine. But the initial FIND for `{` could match a `{` inside a string if the array text begins mid-string \u2014 not our case.\\n\\nNow also double check `parse_calls_chat`/`responses` behavior after fix for id/name extraction: extract_str uses PCRE `\\\"name\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"((?:[^\\\"\\\\\\\\]|\\\\\\\\.)*)\\\"` \u2014 for the mega-entry problem gone; per-entry extract fine. For entry 1, `\\\"name\\\"` \u2014 hmm, the entry also contains `\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\"`... and inside arguments, escaped `\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"` \u2014 the PCRE `[^\\\"\\\\\\\\]|\\\\\\\\.` matches `\\\\\\\"` pairs so inner escaped names won't falsely match... Actually FIND FIRST OCCURRENCE \u2014 the first `\\\"name\\\"` in the entry is the real one (type/call_id/name come before arguments typically; but even if arguments came first and contained `\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"`, the regex would match `\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"` as key?? The pattern is `\\\"name\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"...\\\"`. Inside arguments string: `\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL\\\\\\\"` \u2014 contains `\\\"name\\\"` after the backslash? The regex would try to match `\\\"name\\\"` starting at the `\\\"` of `\\\\\\\"`... In the raw text `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"x\\\\\\\"}`, there IS a substring `\\\"name\\\"` (chars `\\\"`,`n`,`a`,`m`,`e`,`\\\"`)? The raw chars are `\\\\`,`\\\"`,`n`,`a`,`m`,`e`,`\\\\`,`\\\"`,`:`,`\\\\`,`\\\"`,`x`... so the substring `\\\"name\\\\\\\"` \u2014 for the pattern `\\\"name\\\"` we need quote right after `e`, but there's `\\\\` then `\\\"`. So no match at that position. But `\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"x\\\\\\\"` \u2014 could match `\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"x\\\\\\\"` if pattern allowed... pattern requires `\\\"name\\\"` exactly then `:`. After `e` comes `\\\\` \u2192 fail. OK, generally safe-ish. Field order in responses: typically `{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"id\\\":...,\\\"call_id\\\":...,\\\"name\\\":...,\\\"arguments\\\":...,\\\"status\\\":...}` \u2014 name precedes arguments anyway.\\n\\nHmm, one important check: in parse_calls_responses, the guard `IF lv_e NS '\\\"function_call\\\"' AND lv_e NS '\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\"'. CONTINUE.` fine.\\n\\nNow \u2014 the \\\"id\\\" for chat: extract_str 'id' \u2014 in chat entries `{\\\"id\\\":\\\"call_1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":...,\\\"arguments\\\":...}}`. Wait!! parse_calls_chat extracts ls-name via extract_str( iv_json = lv_e iv_name = 'name' ) on the WHOLE entry, and arguments via extract_balanced key 'arguments' \u2014 fine.\\n\\nAlright. Also verify: does anything else call extract_balanced where value-start detection matters? build_input_items: extract_balanced(iv_json=ls-content iv_key='tool_calls' iv_open='[' ...) \u2014 ls-content = `{\\\"tool_calls\\\":[{...}]}`. After key `\\\"tool_calls\\\"`: `:` then `[` \u2192 outer mode \u2713. zif~chat: extract_balanced(iv_json=lv_resp iv_key='output' ...) \u2192 `:` then `[` \u2192 outer \u2713. ev_assistant_raw path: extract_balanced 'tool_calls' on body \u2713. flatten_tools: extract_balanced key 'function' open '{' \u2192 outer \u2713.\\n\\nHmm wait, one more: in zif_pia_00_llm~chat responses branch, `extract_balanced(iv_json = lv_resp iv_key = 'output')` \u2014 the body also contains \\\"output_text\\\" inside items \u2014 FIND `\\\"output\\\"` matches first occurrence `\\\"output\\\"`... `\\\"output\\\"` vs `\\\"output_text\\\"` \u2014 pattern `\\\"output\\\"` requires closing quote; `\\\"output_text\\\"` has `_` after output \u2192 no match there. But there might be earlier `\\\"output\\\"` keys? The output array is the first. OK pre-existing.\\n\\nAnother subtle one: in inner mode detection \u2014 after skipping key and `:`/spaces, what if value starts with `{` directly (outer) vs `\\\"` (inner)? Covered.\\n\\nWhat about extract_balanced calls where the key is followed by the value but my \\\"skip : and space\\\" loop skips ALL leading spaces/colons \u2014 e.g. `{\\\"o\\\" :  \\\"x\\\"}` fine.\\n\\nEdge: what if value is a string that does NOT contain iv_open at all \u2192 lv_start = -1 \u2192 return initial (empty). Previously: e.g. balanced_no_open_bracket expected exactly that. \u2713\\n\\nNow, hmm, one more consideration for inner mode + the `'{'.` scenario where the brace appears in a NON-string part... not applicable.\\n\\nWhat about a source with an apostrophe string containing `}` only, e.g. `lv_c = '}'.`? Inner mode: inside `\\\\\\\"...\\\\\\\"` \u2192 `}` not counted \u2713 (old code would close early). Good \u2014 symmetrical fix.\\n\\nNow the TEST. Where to put the reproduction test? The bug is \\\"PIA runs only the first call ... second lost\\\". Best test: `zcl_pia_00_llm_http=\\u003eparse_calls_responses` with a realistic body. Put it in a new test include for ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (currently empty). I'll write the full testclasses source with a test class ltcl_parse_calls:\\n\\nTest 1 (red reproduction): responses body with two function_calls, first arguments contains `lv_open = '{'.`:\\n\\nBody:\\n```\\n{\\\"created\\\":1,\\\"output\\\":[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"method\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"M1\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"METHOD m1.\\\\n  DATA lv_open TYPE string.\\\\n  lv_open = '{'.\\\\nENDMETHOD.\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"},{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"run_tests\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}],\\\"text\\\":null}\\n```\\n\\nCareful: in test source I'll build the string with concatenation to keep it readable. Note the ABAP string contains `\\\\n` two-char sequences \u2014 fine as literal `\\\\n` inside backticks.\\n\\nAssert:\\n- lines( calls ) = 2\\n- call1-id = c1, name = write_method, arguments = `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"METHOD m1.\\\\n  DATA lv_open TYPE string.\\\\n  lv_open = '{'.\\\\nENDMETHOD.\\\"}` (after unescape, `\\\\n` become real newlines \u2014 expected value must contain real LFs; I'll build with |\\\\n|... hmm. unescape turns `\\\\n` into LF. So expected: `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"METHOD m1.` + LF + `  DATA...` etc. To keep it simpler, avoid `\\\\n` in the test arguments entirely \u2014 use spaces instead of newlines! The bug only needs the `{`. Simpler expected string. Yes: `\\\"source\\\":\\\"METHOD m1. lv_open = '{'. ENDMETHOD.\\\"`.\\n\\nSo body arguments (raw): `\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"method\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"M1\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"METHOD m1. lv_open = '{'. ENDMETHOD.\\\\\\\"}\\\"`.\\n\\nExpected call1-arguments after unescape: `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"METHOD m1. lv_open = '{'. ENDMETHOD.\\\"}`.\\n\\nWait \u2014 also check status field after arguments (to mirror the reported extra text `,\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"`). The user's extra text was `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` \u2014 meaning call_id came AFTER arguments in their body. I'll order fields like that: `{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"...\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`. Then with old code the extra text appears exactly. Let me put call_id after arguments to match the report exactly.\\n\\nOld-code trace on my test body to confirm red: split_entries: entry array text; brace counting without string-awareness \u2192 whole array one entry (as traced before). parse: ls-id: extract_str 'call_id' \u2192 hmm, if call_id comes after arguments... entry = both calls; first `\\\"call_id\\\"` occurrence is c1 (in entry1's tail). name = write_method (first). arguments = inner-mode... OLD code: first `{` after `\\\"arguments\\\"` \u2192 inner obj; depth: `{`1, `\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"` no braces... `'{` \u2192 2, `ENDMETHOD.\\\\\\\"}` \u2192 1, then `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` \u2192 entry1 close \u2192 0 \u2192 arguments = `{...\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` \u2192 unescaped = `{\\\"name\\\":...ENDMETHOD.\\\"}\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`. So test asserting arguments equal the clean value fails, and lines=2 fails (only 1 call, name run_tests? no: name = first \\\"name\\\" = write_method; second lost). RED on two asserts. \\n\\nAlso add a test for parse_calls_chat with same scenario (chat format: `{\\\"tool_calls\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"...\\\"}},...]}`)? The chat arguments are inside function object; split_entries same issue. Good to add \u2014 shows both paths fixed. And a JSON_UTIL-level test for split_entries/extract_balanced? The instructions: \\\"write an ABAP Unit test that reproduces it and is red\\\". One test suffices; I'll add the responses one (the reported path \u2014 PIA uses responses API presumably, given the extra text mentions call_id/status) plus maybe the chat one for coverage. Keep both in LLM_HTTP test include; plus optionally one in JSON_UTIL for split_entries with brace-in-string. I'll add: in LLM_HTTP testclasses: responses_brace_in_string (repro), chat_brace_in_string. That's enough.\\n\\nOrder of work:\\nTurn 1: write_source testclasses for ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (new test include) \u2192 activate \u2192 run_tests \u2192 expect red (my new tests fail). Note run_tests runs on PUBLISHED code \u2014 the new tests must be published first; activate then run in same turn might fail if publish pending. Rule: \\\"if activation says publish pending, finish your turn and run_tests in the next turn\\\". I'll activate and see.\\n\\nTurn 2 (if needed): run_tests \u2192 red confirmed.\\nTurn 3: write_method split_entries + extract_balanced in JSON_UTIL \u2192 activate.\\nTurn 4: run_tests JSON_UTIL and LLM_HTTP \u2192 green (except ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR red in JSON_UTIL, expected).\\n\\nAlso verify existing JSON_UTIL tests still pass after fix (balanced_* etc.).\\n\\nOne more check on my new split_entries: existing tests? JSON_UTIL has no split_entries tests. flatten_tools/build_input_items rely on split_entries \u2014 behavior for well-formed input unchanged (strings now properly skipped; previously quotes toggled nothing \u2014 wait, OLD split_entries had NO string handling at all, so quotes didn't matter; braces inside strings miscounted. For well-formed JSON without braces in strings, same result). \u2713\\n\\nNow write the test class. Note: test classes need `FOR TESTING` and the include is currently empty. Full source:\\n\\n```abap\\nCLASS ltcl_parse_calls DEFINITION FINAL FOR TESTING\\n  RISK LEVEL HARMLESS DURATION SHORT.\\n\\n  PRIVATE SECTION.\\n    METHODS responses_two_calls_brace_in_string FOR TESTING.\\n    METHODS chat_two_calls_brace_in_string FOR TESTING.\\nENDCLASS.\\n\\nCLASS ltcl_parse_calls IMPLEMENTATION.\\n\\n  METHOD responses_two_calls_brace_in_string.\\n    \\\" regression: a single '{' inside a string literal of the first call's\\n    \\\" arguments made the parser swallow the rest of the body: the first call\\n    \\\" got the fields after arguments as extra text and the second call was lost\\n    DATA lv_body TYPE string.\\n    CONCATENATE\\n      `{\\\"output\\\":[`\\n      `{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":`\\n      `\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"method\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"M1\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":`\\n      `\\\\\\\"METHOD m1. lv_open = '{'. ENDMETHOD.\\\\\\\"}\\\"`\\n      `,\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"},`\\n      `{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"run_tests\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\"}\\\"`\\n      `,\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`\\n      `]}` INTO lv_body.\\n```\\n\\nHmm careful with CONCATENATE and backticks \u2014 fine. But wait: `\\\\\\\"` inside a backtick string is just backslash+quote chars. Good.\\n\\nExpected:\\n\\n```abap\\n    DATA(lt_calls) = zcl_pia_00_llm_http=\\u003eparse_calls_responses( lv_body ).\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 2 act = lines( lt_calls ) msg = 'both calls must be found' ).\\n    READ TABLE lt_calls INDEX 1 INTO DATA(ls_c1).\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 'c1' act = ls_c1-id ... ).\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 'write_method' act = ls_c1-name ... ).\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals(\\n      exp = `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"METHOD m1. lv_open = '{'. ENDMETHOD.\\\"}`\\n      act = ls_c1-arguments\\n      msg = 'arguments must end at their own closing brace' ).\\n    READ TABLE lt_calls INDEX 2 INTO DATA(ls_c2).\\n    assert c2 / run_tests / {\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\"}.\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\n```\\n\\nWait: expected arguments after unescape: raw extracted `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"method\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"M1\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"METHOD m1. lv_open = '{'. ENDMETHOD.\\\\\\\"}` then unescape \u2192 `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"METHOD m1. lv_open = '{'. ENDMETHOD.\\\"}` \u2713.\\n\\nHmm \u2014 but unescape processes `\\\\u`? No `\\\\u` here. `\\\\\\\"`\u2192`\\\"` \u2713.\\n\\nChat test body:\\n\\n```\\n{\\\"tool_calls\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"METHOD m. lv_open = '{'. ENDMETHOD.\\\\\\\"}\\\"}},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"run_tests\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\"}\\\"}}]}\\n```\\n\\nparse_calls_chat: split_entries on tool_calls array; per entry: id via extract_str 'id' \u2192 c1; name via extract_str 'name' \u2192 write_method (first occurrence \u2014 entry contains `\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":...` \u2014 first `\\\"name\\\"` is the real one \u2713); arguments via extract_balanced key 'arguments' \u2192 after `\\\"arguments\\\"`: `:` then `\\\"` \u2192 inner mode \u2713.\\n\\nOld code on chat test: split_entries: entry1 braces: `{`1 ... `function` `{`2 ... arguments value `'{'`\u21923 ... `\\\\\\\"}`\u21922 ... `}`(function close)\u21921 ... `}`(entry close)\u21920? Let's count precisely. Entry1 = `{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"METHOD m. lv_open = '{'. ENDMETHOD.\\\\\\\"}\\\"}}`\\n\\nBraces: entry `{`(1), function `{`(2), arguments value `{`(3), `'{'`(4), inner close `}`(3), function `}`(2), entry `}`(1). Never 0 \u2192 runs into entry2: `,` `{`(2), function `{`(3), args `{`(4), inner `}`(3), function `}`(2), entry `}`(1). End: depth 1 \u2192 one mega entry. id=c1, name=write_method, arguments: first `{` after `\\\"arguments\\\"` = args obj (3); `'{'`\u21924; inner close\u21923; function close\u21922; entry1 close\u21921; entry2 stuff: args `{`\u21922, inner `}`\u21921... let me just say it ends at entry2's function `}`? depth after entry1 close = 1... entry2: `{`\u21922, function `{`\u21923, args `{`\u21924, `\\\\\\\"}`\u21923, function `}`\u21922, entry `}`\u21921, then `]}` of tool_calls/body: `]` nothing, `}`\u21920 \u2192 stop. arguments = everything from args `{` to that final `}` \u2014 garbage. And second call lost. RED \u2713.\\n\\nGood. Both tests red on old code.\\n\\nNow, also the user says ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR is already red \u2014 leave as is. When I run JSON_UTIL tests, that one will fail; expected. Also note `roundtrip_nonascii` \u2014 is it green now? It says \\\"test fails now\\\" in comments but current unescape handles `\\\\u` via uccp, so it should pass. Fine.\\n\\nLet me also double check `FIND lv_pat IN iv_json` \u2014 plain substring FIND (not REGEX). Pattern `\\\"arguments\\\"` could also match `\\\"arguments\\\"` occurring earlier inside another string... pre-existing.\\n\\nOne more thing in extract_balanced: the loop `WHILE lv_i \\u003c lv_len AND ( lv_rest+lv_i(1) = ` ` OR lv_rest+lv_i(1) = ':' )` \u2014 note lv_rest starts at the KEY match, so lv_rest+0 = `\\\"`. I start lv_i = strlen(lv_pat) (just after key). Then skip spaces/colons. What about tabs/newlines between? rare. OK.\\n\\nHmm \u2014 but there's a case: key matched, but the char right after key is not `:` (e.g. matched a substring inside a longer key like `\\\"argumentsx\\\"`? pattern includes closing quote so `\\\"arguments\\\"` can't match `\\\"argumentsx\\\"`). \u2713\\n\\nAlso what if value is `null`/number? no iv_open \u2192 empty return. Pre-existing.\\n\\nAlso potential issue: in inner mode, the first iv_open search starts at lv_i (the value's opening quote) and searches forward \u2014 could find `{` beyond the value end if value has no `{`... returns something odd; pre-existing-ish, acceptable.\\n\\nHmm, but actually \u2014 should the search for the first opening delimiter in inner mode stop at the value's closing bare quote? For robustness, yes ideally, but keep it simple.\\n\\nNow write everything. Start with the test include.\\n\\nAlso - think about whether `parse_calls_responses`'s `IF lv_e NS '\\\"function_call\\\"'` etc. still fine \u2014 yes.\\n\\nPotential gotcha: `DATA lv_in TYPE abap_bool VALUE abap_false.` declared before outer WHILE in split_entries but I set `lv_in = abap_false` at each entry start. In extract_balanced, lv_in declared once, initialized false \u2014 single pass, fine.\\n\\nAnother gotcha: in extract_balanced inner mode I use `CONTINUE` inside WHILE \u2014 allowed.\\n\\nAlso in inner mode when hitting bare `\\\"` I do `lv_pos = lv_pos - 1. EXIT.` so rv_ excludes the quote. Hmm, why back up? Because rv_ should end before the quote. Actually let me keep it simple: `EXIT` without backup would include the quote in rv_. Which is better? If braces unbalanced inside value (malformed), either is garbage. But consider a legit case: inner mode where iv_close found properly \u2014 normal path. The bare-quote case only happens on malformed input. Keep `lv_pos = lv_pos - 1` then EXIT \u2014 safe as long as lv_pos \u2265 lv_start (it is, since we advanced past start).\\n\\nHmm wait, actually there's a subtle problem: inner mode + the FIRST character being `{` \u2014 after this `{`, the very next chars `\\\\\\\"` toggle ON. What if arguments = `\\\"{}\\\"`? `{` depth1, `}` depth0 \u2192 rv_ = `{}`. \u2713\\n\\nWhat about arguments that legitimately contain a quote-escaped brace... covered.\\n\\nNow also think: does anything call extract_balanced with iv_open = '[' and a STRING value (inner mode)? E.g. key 'arguments' always object. 'output'/'tool_calls' direct arrays. So inner mode with '[' unlikely. Fine.\\n\\nLet me also consider: chat body `ev_assistant_raw` for the NEXT turn \u2014 executor stores `{\\\"tool_calls\\\":[...]}` and later build_input_items re-parses with extract_balanced 'tool_calls' + split_entries + extract_str 'arguments'. With escaped args (`escape( ls_c-arguments )` \u2014 braces not escaped, quotes escaped) \u2192 same scenario, now handled. \u2713\\n\\nWrite the test include now. I need to be careful with string literals containing backslashes in ABAP source within JSON tool-call arguments \u2014 my write_method source parameter is itself JSON, so backslashes must be doubled in the JSON I send. The tool layer handles it (arguments is a JSON string). I'll write `\\\\` as `\\\\\\\\` in the JSON args. Yes \u2014 in my function_calls arguments JSON, every literal backslash becomes `\\\\\\\\`.\\n\\nLet me compose the test include source carefully:\\n\\n```abap\\n\\\"* Unit tests for the tool-call parsing of zcl_pia_00_llm_http.\\n\\nCLASS ltcl_parse_calls DEFINITION FINAL FOR TESTING\\n  RISK LEVEL HARMLESS DURATION SHORT.\\n\\n  PRIVATE SECTION.\\n    \\\" regression: a single '{' inside a string value of the first call's\\n    \\\" arguments (JSON does not escape braces, e.g. an ABAP literal '{')\\n    \\\" made split_entries/extract_balanced run past the end of the value:\\n    \\\" the first call got the fields behind arguments as extra text and\\n    \\\" every following call was lost\\n    METHODS responses_two_calls_brace_in_string FOR TESTING.\\n    METHODS chat_two_calls_brace_in_string FOR TESTING.\\nENDCLASS.\\n\\nCLASS ltcl_parse_calls IMPLEMENTATION.\\n\\n  METHOD responses_two_calls_brace_in_string.\\n    DATA lv_body TYPE string.\\n    CONCATENATE\\n      `{\\\"output\\\":[`\\n      `{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\",`\\n      `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"method\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"M1\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":`\\n      `\\\\\\\"METHOD m1. lv_open = '{'. ENDMETHOD.\\\\\\\"}\\\",`\\n      `\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"},`\\n      `{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"run_tests\\\",`\\n      `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\"}\\\",`\\n      `\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`\\n      `]}`\\n      INTO lv_body.\\n\\n    DATA(lt_calls) = zcl_pia_00_llm_http=\\u003eparse_calls_responses( lv_body ).\\n\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals(\\n      exp = 2 act = lines( lt_calls )\\n      msg = 'both function calls must be found' ).\\n\\n    READ TABLE lt_calls INDEX 1 INTO DATA(ls_c1).\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_subrc( ).\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 'c1' act = ls_c1-id msg = 'id of call 1' ).\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 'write_method' act = ls_c1-name msg = 'name of call 1' ).\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals(\\n      exp = `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"METHOD m1. lv_open = '{'. ENDMETHOD.\\\"}`\\n      act = ls_c1-arguments\\n      msg = 'arguments of call 1 must end at their own closing brace' ).\\n\\n    READ TABLE lt_calls INDEX 2 INTO DATA(ls_c2).\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_subrc( ).\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 'c2' act = ls_c2-id msg = 'id of call 2' ).\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 'run_tests' act = ls_c2-name msg = 'name of call 2' ).\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals(\\n      exp = `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\"}`\\n      act = ls_c2-arguments\\n      msg = 'arguments of call 2' ).\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\n\\n  METHOD chat_two_calls_brace_in_string.\\n    DATA lv_body TYPE string.\\n    CONCATENATE\\n      `{\\\"tool_calls\\\":[`\\n      `{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\",`\\n      `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"METHOD m. lv_open = '{'. ENDMETHOD.\\\\\\\"}\\\"}},`\\n      `{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"run_tests\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\"}\\\"}}`\\n      `]}`\\n      INTO lv_body.\\n\\n    DATA(lt_calls) = zcl_pia_00_llm_http=\\u003eparse_calls_chat( lv_body ).\\n    ... same asserts\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\n\\nENDCLASS.\\n```\\n\\nCheck CONCATENATE: multiple sources into target \u2014 `CONCATENATE src1 src2 ... INTO lv_body.` allowed (up to ... many). Yes.\\n\\nCareful about CONCATENATE with backquote literals containing `\\\\` \u2014 fine.\\n\\nWait, one detail: in the chat entry, the guard `IF lv_e NS '\\\"function_call\\\"'...` \u2014 chat entries contain `\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\"` \u2014 fine.\\n\\nAlso note: chat parse: ls-name = extract_str 'name' \u2014 the entry's first `\\\"name\\\"` is inside function object \u2713.\\n\\nNow double-check my expected chat arguments after fix: raw extracted = `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"METHOD m. lv_open = '{'. ENDMETHOD.\\\\\\\"}` \u2192 unescape \u2192 `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"METHOD m. lv_open = '{'. ENDMETHOD.\\\"}` \u2713.\\n\\nHmm, wait \u2014 chat parse in OLD code: `ls-arguments = unescape(extract_balanced(...))`... in old code trace above arguments ends at body-final `}` \u2192 includes lots. Red \u2713.\\n\\nNow, is `assert_subrc` available? cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_subrc( exp = 0 ) \u2014 yes, exists. After READ TABLE sy-subrc 0. OK.\\n\\nAlso potential syntax nit: `DATA(ls_c1)` inline after READ \u2014 need `INTO DATA(ls_c1)` \u2014 yes that's valid.\\n\\nOne more consideration: the testclass include for LLM_HTTP is EMPTY (no content at all). write_source with include testclasses \u2014 full source. Also risk: the main class must have... test classes in testclasses include are local; fine.\\n\\nLet me also double check: does `parse_calls_responses` filter require `\\\"function_call\\\"` as substring: my entries have `\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\"` \u2713.\\n\\nNow the fix code. Let me write the two methods fully.\\n\\nsplit_entries new:\\n\\n```abap\\n  METHOD split_entries.\\n    \\\" top-level {...} entries of a JSON array string; string-aware: braces inside\\n    \\\" \\\"...\\\" values do not count (JSON does not escape them, so an ABAP literal\\n    \\\" like '{' in a tool-call source used to swallow the following entries) and\\n    \\\" \\\\\\\" is an escaped quote; every access is clamped\\n    DATA lv_len_arr TYPE i.\\n    lv_len_arr = strlen( iv_arr ).\\n    DATA lv_pos TYPE i VALUE 0.\\n    DATA lv_in TYPE abap_bool VALUE abap_false.\\n    WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len_arr.\\n      FIND FIRST OCCURRENCE OF '{' IN iv_arr+lv_pos MATCH OFFSET DATA(lv_off).\\n      IF sy-subrc \\u003c\\u003e 0. EXIT. ENDIF.\\n      DATA lv_start TYPE i.\\n      lv_start = lv_pos + lv_off.\\n      IF lv_start \\u003e= lv_len_arr. EXIT. ENDIF.\\n      DATA lv_depth TYPE i.\\n      lv_depth = 1.\\n      DATA lv_end TYPE i.\\n      lv_end = lv_start.\\n      lv_in = abap_false.\\n      WHILE lv_end \\u003c lv_len_arr - 1 AND lv_depth \\u003e 0.\\n        lv_end = lv_end + 1.\\n        DATA lv_ch TYPE string.\\n        lv_ch = iv_arr+lv_end(1).\\n        IF lv_ch = '\\\"'.\\n          lv_in = boolc( lv_in = abap_false ).\\n        ELSEIF lv_ch = `\\\\` AND lv_in = abap_true AND lv_end + 1 \\u003c lv_len_arr.\\n          lv_end = lv_end + 1.  \\\" escaped character (e.g. \\\\\\\" inside the string)\\n        ELSEIF lv_in = abap_false.\\n          IF lv_ch = '{'.\\n            lv_depth = lv_depth + 1.\\n          ELSEIF lv_ch = '}'.\\n            lv_depth = lv_depth - 1.\\n          ENDIF.\\n        ENDIF.\\n      ENDWHILE.\\n      IF lv_depth \\u003e 0.\\n        lv_end = lv_len_arr - 1.\\n      ENDIF.\\n      DATA lv_len TYPE i.\\n      lv_len = lv_end - lv_start + 1.\\n      IF lv_len \\u003c= 0. EXIT. ENDIF.\\n      IF lv_start + lv_len \\u003e lv_len_arr.\\n        lv_len = lv_len_arr - lv_start.\\n      ENDIF.\\n      IF lv_len \\u003e 0.\\n        APPEND iv_arr+lv_start(lv_len) TO rt_.\\n      ENDIF.\\n      lv_pos = lv_end + 1.\\n    ENDWHILE.\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\n```\\n\\nWait: `DATA lv_in ... VALUE abap_false` then `lv_in = abap_false` per entry \u2014 fine.\\n\\nHmm, careful: `boolc` returns abap_bool string 'X'/' '. Comparison `lv_in = abap_false` where lv_in is abap_bool \u2014 fine.\\n\\nOne check: escaped quote inside string: `\\\"a\\\\\\\"b\\\"` \u2192 at `\\\\` with lv_in true \u2192 skip next (`\\\"`) \u2014 good, doesn't toggle. And `\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"}\\\"` \u2014 same. \u2713\\n\\nBut: `\\\\` outside string (lv_in false) \u2014 e.g. malformed \u2014 no skip; then it's just a char, not counted. Fine.\\n\\nextract_balanced new:\\n\\n```abap\\n  METHOD extract_balanced.\\n    \\\" value of iv_key up to the matching closing delimiter; string-aware:\\n    \\\" - direct object/array value: \\\"...\\\" literals are skipped, \\\\\\\" is escaped\\n    \\\" - string value (JSON embedded in a string, e.g. arguments): the embedded\\n    \\\"   quotes appear as \\\\\\\" and toggle; a bare \\\" ends the value. Raw brace\\n    \\\"   counting ran past the end whenever a value contained an unbalanced\\n    \\\"   brace (an ABAP literal like '{'), swallowing the rest of the body\\n    DATA lv_off TYPE i.\\n    DATA(lv_pat) = '\\\"' \\u0026\\u0026 iv_key \\u0026\\u0026 '\\\"'.\\n    FIND lv_pat IN iv_json MATCH OFFSET lv_off.\\n    IF sy-subrc \\u003c\\u003e 0. RETURN. ENDIF.\\n    DATA lv_rest TYPE string.\\n    lv_rest = iv_json+lv_off.\\n    DATA lv_len TYPE i.\\n    lv_len = strlen( lv_rest ).\\n    \\\" skip the key itself, colon and blanks\\n    DATA lv_val TYPE i.\\n    lv_val = strlen( lv_pat ).\\n    WHILE lv_val \\u003c lv_len AND ( lv_rest+lv_val(1) = ':' OR lv_rest+lv_val(1) = ` ` ).\\n      lv_val = lv_val + 1.\\n    ENDWHILE.\\n    IF lv_val \\u003e= lv_len. RETURN. ENDIF.\\n    \\\" string value -\\u003e the JSON we want is embedded in it (quotes are \\\\\\\")\\n    DATA lv_inner TYPE abap_bool.\\n    lv_inner = boolc( lv_rest+lv_val(1) = '\\\"' ).\\n    \\\" first opening delimiter at/after the value start\\n    DATA lv_start TYPE i VALUE -1.\\n    WHILE lv_val \\u003c lv_len AND lv_start \\u003c 0.\\n      IF lv_rest+lv_val(1) = iv_open.\\n        lv_start = lv_val.\\n      ENDIF.\\n      lv_val = lv_val + 1.\\n    ENDWHILE.\\n    IF lv_start \\u003c 0. RETURN. ENDIF.\\n    DATA lv_depth TYPE i VALUE 1.\\n    DATA lv_pos TYPE i.\\n    lv_pos = lv_start.\\n    DATA lv_in TYPE abap_bool VALUE abap_false.\\n    DATA lv_next TYPE i.\\n    WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len - 1 AND lv_depth \\u003e 0.\\n      lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\n      DATA lv_ch TYPE string.\\n      lv_ch = lv_rest+lv_pos(1).\\n      IF lv_ch = `\\\\`.\\n        lv_next = lv_pos + 1.\\n        IF lv_next \\u003c lv_len.\\n          IF lv_inner = abap_true AND lv_rest+lv_next(1) = '\\\"'.\\n            lv_in = boolc( lv_in = abap_false ).  \\\" quote of the embedded JSON\\n          ENDIF.\\n          lv_pos = lv_next.  \\\" skip the escaped character\\n        ENDIF.\\n        CONTINUE.\\n      ENDIF.\\n      IF lv_ch = '\\\"'.\\n        IF lv_inner = abap_true.\\n          lv_pos = lv_pos - 1.  \\\" closing quote of the string value: it is over\\n          EXIT.\\n        ENDIF.\\n        lv_in = boolc( lv_in = abap_false ).\\n        CONTINUE.\\n      ENDIF.\\n      IF lv_in = abap_false.\\n        IF lv_ch = iv_open.\\n          lv_depth = lv_depth + 1.\\n        ELSEIF lv_ch = iv_close.\\n          lv_depth = lv_depth - 1.\\n        ENDIF.\\n      ENDIF.\\n    ENDWHILE.\\n    DATA(lv_l2) = lv_pos - lv_start + 1.\\n    IF lv_start + lv_l2 \\u003e lv_len.\\n      lv_l2 = lv_len - lv_start.\\n    ENDIF.\\n    IF lv_l2 \\u003e 0.\\n      rv_ = lv_rest+lv_start(lv_l2).\\n    ENDIF.\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\n```\\n\\nHold on \u2014 in OUTER mode, `\\\\` handling: my code treats `\\\\` as escape skip regardless of mode. In outer mode outside strings a `\\\\` is invalid JSON; skipping 2 harmless. Inside outer strings `\\\\` skips next \u2014 needed so `\\\\\\\"` doesn't toggle. But in outer mode, when lv_ch = `\\\\` and next is `\\\"`: my code does NOT toggle (only toggles if lv_inner) and skips 2 \u2192 correct for outer (escaped quote inside string). \u2713 And `\\\\n` inside outer string: skip 2 \u2713.\\n\\nBut WAIT \u2014 outer mode bug: `\\\\` skip happens even when NOT in string. Consider outer text like `\\\"a\\\" , \\\"b\\\"` \u2014 no backslashes outside strings. fine.\\n\\nNow verify outer mode with quotes: `\\\"output\\\":[{...}]`: at `\\\"` toggle ON... `[` in-string? NO wait: `\\\"[` \u2014 the `[` comes after `\\\"output\\\"` closed. Sequence: `\\\"` ON, output, `\\\"` OFF, `:`, `[` start... \u2713. Inside items: `\\\"type\\\"` ON/OFF; arguments value `\\\"` ON; `\\\\\\\"` \u2192 skip (no toggle since outer); `'{'` in-string not counted \u2713; value `\\\"` OFF; `}` depth 0... \\n\\nHmm wait \u2014 but this is extract_balanced('output') returning the whole ARRAY; depth counting for `[`/`]`: lv_ch `[` \u2014 not open/close unless iv_open='['. For output: iv_open='[', iv_close=']'. Strings skipped \u2713; nested `[`/`]` counted \u2713; `{`/`}` ignored \u2713.\\n\\nInner-mode check on arguments again with the code: lv_inner = true (value starts with `\\\"`). Find first `{` from lv_val \u2014 the value's opening quote is AT lv_val; we then search from lv_val forward for `{` \u2192 finds the `{` inside the string \u2713 (lv_val increments past the quote char).\\n\\nThen scan: at `{` (lv_start) depth 1. Continue: `\\\\` next `\\\"` \u2192 toggle ON, skip to after `\\\\\\\"`. `name` chars: none special. `\\\\``? etc. Then `,`... then `\\\\` `\\\"` toggle ON for source value... inside: `'`,`{` \u2014 in-string, not counted \u2713... `\\\\` `\\\"` toggle OFF, then `}` \u2192 depth 0 \u2192 exit. rv_ ends at that `}` \u2713.\\n\\nEdge: what about `\\\\\\\\` before a quote-end in inner mode, e.g. source ends with a backslash: `...lv_x = '\\\\\\\\'. ENDMETHOD.\\\\\\\"` \u2192 raw `\\\\\\\\` then `'` etc: `\\\\` next `\\\\` \u2192 not quote \u2192 skip 2 \u2713.\\n\\nNow check existing test `balanced_no_open_bracket`: `{\\\"o\\\":\\\"txt\\\"}` \u2192 lv_inner = true, search `{` from after `\\\"o\\\":` position \u2192 value `\\\"txt\\\"}` \u2014 chars `\\\"`,`t`,`x`,`t`,`}` \u2014 no `{` \u2192 lv_start=-1 \u2192 RETURN empty \u2713.\\n\\n`balanced_object` `{\\\"o\\\":{\\\"x\\\":1},\\\"y\\\":2}`: FIND `\\\"o\\\"` \u2192 offset 1. lv_rest = `\\\"o\\\":{\\\"x\\\":1},\\\"y\\\":2}`. lv_val=3 \u2192 skip `:` \u2192 lv_val=4 char `{` \u2192 lv_inner = false. find `[`? no\u2014iv_open='{': lv_start=4? wait: the WHILE for lv_start starts at lv_val=4: `lv_rest+4(1)`... lv_rest = `\\\"o\\\":{\\\"x\\\":1},\\\"y\\\":2}`: index 0=`\\\"`,1=`o`,2=`\\\"`,3=`:`,4=`{`,5=`\\\"`,6=`x`,7=`\\\"`,8=`:`,9=`1`,10=`}`,11=`,`,12=`\\\"`,13=`y`,14=`\\\"`,15=`:`,16=`2`,17=`}`. lv_start=4, depth1. pos5 `\\\"` \u2192 toggle ON. 6,7: `x`; pos7 `\\\"` toggle OFF. 8 `:`. 9 `1`. 10 `}` depth0 \u2192 exit. rv_ = rest+4(7) = `{\\\"x\\\":1}` \u2713.\\n\\n`balanced_nested_and_array` `[1,[2,3]]`: no quotes \u2713.\\n\\n`balanced_unclosed` `{\\\"o\\\":{\\\"x\\\":1`: rest same start; lv_start=4 (inner `{`)... wait key 'o', rest = `\\\"o\\\":{\\\"x\\\":1`, indices: 0`\\\"`,1`o`,2`\\\"`,3`:`,4`{`,5`\\\"`,6`x`,7`\\\"`,8`:`,9`1`. lv_val: skip key(3) + `:` \u2192 4 \u2192 `{` outer mode, lv_start=4, depth1, scan to end (len 10, while pos\\u003c9): pos5 `\\\"` ON, 6 x, 7 `\\\"` OFF, 8 `:`, 9 `1`. loop ends pos=9, depth\\u003e0 \u2192 lv_l2 = 9-4+1=6 \u2192 rv_ = `{\\\"x\\\":1` \u2713 matches expected.\\n\\nflatten_tools: extract_balanced key 'function' open '{' \u2014 entry `{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"x\\\",\\\"parameters\\\":{...}}}`. FIND `\\\"function\\\"` \u2014 hmm! Pattern `\\\"function\\\"` \u2014 the entry contains `\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{...`. FIND FIRST: `\\\"function\\\"` first occurrence is the VALUE of type at... `\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\"` \u2014 substring `\\\"function\\\"` occurs there! offset of that is earlier than the key `\\\"function\\\":`. So lv_rest starts at `\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\"...`. Then skip key `\\\"function\\\"` (10 chars) \u2192 next char `,` \u2014 not `:` or space \u2192 lv_val stays; value char = `,` \u2192 lv_inner = false; then search first `{` from there \u2192 the `{` of function object \u2713. Works (as before \u2014 old code also found that `{`). \u2713 Same behavior.\\n\\nWait, old code: FIND `\\\"function\\\"` \u2192 same offset \u2192 then first `{` after \u2192 same. \u2713 no regression.\\n\\nbuild_input_items extract_balanced 'tool_calls' on `{\\\"tool_calls\\\":[...]}` \u2192 outer mode \u2713.\\n\\nzif~chat: `extract_balanced(iv_json = lv_resp iv_key = 'output' iv_open = '[' iv_close = ']')` \u2014 responses body: `\\\"output\\\":[...]` \u2192 outer \u2713. Note: responses body also has `\\\"output_text\\\"` inside items \u2014 FIND `\\\"output\\\"` matches `\\\"output\\\"` \u2014 the first occurrence: could `\\\"output\\\"` appear inside... items contain `\\\"type\\\":\\\"output_text\\\"` \u2014 pattern `\\\"output\\\"` needs quote after t \u2014 `\\\"output_text\\\"` has `_` \u2192 no. But also function_call_output items have `\\\"output\\\"` \u2014 wait, that's in INPUT items (request), not response. Fine, and first match is the real output array anyway.\\n\\nAlso `zif_pia_00_llm~chat` chat branch: `ls-content CP '{\\\"tool_calls\\\"*'` then `ls-content+14` \u2014 unchanged.\\n\\nOK. One more potential regression: executor or other classes using split_entries/extract_balanced? Let me check other ZCL_PIA classes quickly for usage \u2014 session, executor... Probably only LLM_HTTP and JSON_UTIL tests. I could grep but tools don't offer search. The main consumers are the ones seen. There's also ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY maybe parsing tool JSON. Risk is low: string-awareness only changes behavior for inputs with braces inside strings (previously broken).\\n\\nActually, let me check ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY and executor for extract_balanced usage to be safe. I'll read them... that's 2 more reads; worth it for safety. Actually the fix only changes behavior where unbalanced braces appear inside strings \u2014 previously mis-parsed. Any caller with such data was already broken. I'll skip extra reads. Hmm, but one caution: `flatten_tools` uses split_entries on tools JSON: `[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"read_object\\\",\\\"description\\\":\\\"... '{' ...\\\"}}]` \u2014 descriptions could contain braces; now handled better.\\n\\nLet me now also think about `unescape` in the arguments path once more: unescape receives `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...}` and converts `\\\\\\\"`\u2192`\\\"`. Our rv_ from inner mode = `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",...}` \u2014 contains no bare `\\\"`. \u2713 expected result as asserted.\\n\\nAlright, and what about arguments containing `\\\\u007B` (escaped brace as unicode)? Some models escape `{` as `\\\\u0077`? If the model escaped the brace (`\\\\u007B`), raw has no bare `{` and old code worked. Our fix also works: `\\\\` next `u` \u2192 skip 2 (not quote) \u2014 hmm, `\\\\u` in inner mode: `\\\\` then `u` \u2192 skip 2 only, then `007B` chars \u2014 not counted (not braces) \u2713.\\n\\nEdge in inner mode: what if arguments contain `\\\\\\\\'` \u2026 covered.\\n\\nWhat about a bare `\\\"` INSIDE... can't happen in valid JSON string.\\n\\nOK now also think about inner-mode + value that contains the pattern `\\\\` at the very end \u2014 clamped.\\n\\nI think the fix is solid. Also: should I care that `FIND lv_pat IN iv_json` for key 'arguments' could match `\\\"arguments\\\"` INSIDE the source string of an earlier field (e.g. description text mentioning arguments)? Pre-existing; skip.\\n\\nNow execute plan:\\n\\nStep 1: write_source testclasses ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP, activate, then run_tests (if publish pending, next turn).\\n\\nLet me write final sources carefully. Note: in write_source JSON, the ABAP source with backslashes \u2014 every `\\\\` in ABAP source must be `\\\\\\\\` in the JSON string. My ABAP test code contains `\\\\\\\"` sequences etc. I must be meticulous.\\n\\nAlso the test include must not conflict: LLM_HTTP testclasses currently empty \u2014 writing a fresh one is fine.\\n\\nLet me draft the exact test include text:\\n\\n```\\n\\\"* Unit tests for the tool-call parsing of zcl_pia_00_llm_http.\\n\\nCLASS ltcl_parse_calls DEFINITION FINAL FOR TESTING\\n  RISK LEVEL HARMLESS DURATION SHORT.\\n\\n  PRIVATE SECTION.\\n    \\\" regression: a single opening brace inside a string value of the first\\n    \\\" call's arguments (JSON does not escape braces, e.g. an ABAP literal '{')\\n    \\\" used to make the brace counting run past the end of the value: the first\\n    \\\" call got the fields behind \\\"arguments\\\" as extra text and every following\\n    \\\" call of the same answer was lost\\n    METHODS responses_two_calls_brace_in_string FOR TESTING.\\n    METHODS chat_two_calls_brace_in_string FOR TESTING.\\nENDCLASS.\\n\\nCLASS ltcl_parse_calls IMPLEMENTATION.\\n\\n  METHOD responses_two_calls_brace_in_string.\\n    DATA lv_body TYPE string.\\n    DATA lt_calls TYPE zif_pia_00_tool=\\u003ett_calls.\\n    DATA ls_call LIKE LINE OF lt_calls.\\n\\n    CONCATENATE\\n      `{\\\"output\\\":[`\\n      `{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\",`\\n      `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"method\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"M1\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":`\\n      `\\\\\\\"METHOD m1. lv_open = '{'. ENDMETHOD.\\\\\\\"}\\\",`\\n      `\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"},`\\n      `{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"run_tests\\\",`\\n      `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\"}\\\",`\\n      `\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`\\n      `]}`\\n      INTO lv_body.\\n\\n    lt_calls = zcl_pia_00_llm_http=\\u003eparse_calls_responses( lv_body ).\\n\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals(\\n      exp = 2\\n      act = lines( lt_calls )\\n      msg = 'both function calls of the answer must be found' ).\\n\\n    READ TABLE lt_calls INDEX 1 INTO ls_call.\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_subrc( msg = 'call 1 exists' ).\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 'c1' act = ls_call-id msg = 'call 1: call_id' ).\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 'write_method' act = ls_call-name msg = 'call 1: name' ).\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals(\\n      exp = `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"METHOD m1. lv_open = '{'. ENDMETHOD.\\\"}`\\n      act = ls_call-arguments\\n      msg = 'call 1: arguments end at their own closing brace' ).\\n\\n    READ TABLE lt_calls INDEX 2 INTO ls_call.\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_subrc( msg = 'call 2 exists' ).\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 'c2' act = ls_call-id msg = 'call 2: call_id' ).\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 'run_tests' act = ls_call-name msg = 'call 2: name' ).\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals(\\n      exp = `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\"}`\\n      act = ls_call-arguments\\n      msg = 'call 2: arguments' ).\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\n\\n  METHOD chat_two_calls_brace_in_string.\\n    DATA lv_body TYPE string.\\n    DATA lt_calls TYPE zif_pia_00_tool=\\u003ett_calls.\\n    DATA ls_call LIKE LINE OF lt_calls.\\n\\n    CONCATENATE\\n      `{\\\"tool_calls\\\":[`\\n      `{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\",`\\n      `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":`\\n      `\\\\\\\"METHOD m. lv_open = '{'. ENDMETHOD.\\\\\\\"}\\\"}},`\\n      `{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"run_tests\\\",`\\n      `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\"}\\\"}}`\\n      `]}`\\n      INTO lv_body.\\n\\n    lt_calls = zcl_pia_00_llm_http=\\u003eparse_calls_chat( lv_body ).\\n\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals(\\n      exp = 2\\n      act = lines( lt_calls )\\n      msg = 'both tool calls of the answer must be found' ).\\n\\n    READ TABLE lt_calls INDEX 1 INTO ls_call.\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_subrc( msg = 'call 1 exists' ).\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 'c1' act = ls_call-id msg = 'call 1: id' ).\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 'write_method' act = ls_call-name msg = 'call 1: name' ).\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals(\\n      exp = `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"METHOD m. lv_open = '{'. ENDMETHOD.\\\"}`\\n      act = ls_call-arguments\\n      msg = 'call 1: arguments end at their own closing brace' ).\\n\\n    READ TABLE lt_calls INDEX 2 INTO ls_call.\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_subrc( msg = 'call 2 exists' ).\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 'c2' act = ls_call-id msg = 'call 2: id' ).\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 'run_tests' act = ls_call-name msg = 'call 2: name' ).\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals(\\n      exp = `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\"}`\\n      act = ls_call-arguments\\n      msg = 'call 2: arguments' ).\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\n\\nENDCLASS.\\n```\\n\\nDoes assert_subrc have a msg param? cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_subrc( exp = 0 msg = ... ) \u2014 yes it accepts msg. After READ TABLE with sy-subrc 0. Fine. Actually simpler to drop assert_subrc since lines=2 assert already covers; but keep.\\n\\nNote CONCATENATE line count limit? CONCATENATE accepts many sources; fine.\\n\\nAlso check: `zif_pia_00_tool=\\u003ett_calls` type exists (used in class). \u2713 ts_call has id, name, arguments \u2713.\\n\\nOne concern: parse_calls_responses with OLD split_entries on my test body \u2014 mega entry \u2192 first assert lines=2 fails \u2192 test RED \u2713. And with old extract_balanced, arguments garbage. Both asserts red.\\n\\nBut CAREFUL: with old code, could an ABAP runtime dump occur (substring out of range) making it \\\"red\\\" with error rather than assert failure? Either way red. But for the \\\"show red then green\\\" narrative it's fine as long as no dump \u2014 old split_entries is clamped; old extract_balanced: `rv_ = lv_rest+lv_start(lv_len)` \u2014 lv_pos bounded by loop \\u003c strlen-1... old loop: WHILE lv_pos \\u003c strlen(lv_rest) - 1 \u2192 lv_pos \u2264 len-1 \u2192 lv_len = lv_pos - lv_start + 1 \u2264 len - lv_start \u2192 substring valid. No dump. Good: clean assert failures.\\n\\nNow, also the user wants me to leave ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR alone \u2014 I'm not touching it.\\n\\nLet me also decide whether to add JSON_UTIL-level tests for the two helpers (split_entries with brace-in-string, extract_balanced inner mode). It strengthens the suite; the repro tests already cover via LLM_HTTP. I'll add two small tests to JSON_UTIL testclasses too? That requires write_source of the FULL testclasses include (it's long, ~500 lines). Risky but doable... write_method can add a method? \\\"Replace one existing METHOD block; a new method or changed signature needs write_source.\\\" So adding test methods to the existing test class requires full write_source of that big include. Meh \u2014 the LLM_HTTP tests cover the fix. The user asked for \\\"an ABAP Unit test that reproduces it\\\" \u2014 the two in LLM_HTTP suffice. Skip JSON_UTIL test edits.\\n\\nPlan execution now: \\n1) write_source testclasses LLM_HTTP + activate (same message? \\\"read before write\\\" \u2014 I read it already, empty). Then run_tests \u2014 but run_tests runs on PUBLISHED code; if activation reports publish pending, must wait. I'll do write_source first, then activate, then try run_tests; if told pending, wait.\\n\\nActually order: write_source and activate are dependent (must write before activate) \u2014 so separate blocks. The instructions: multiple independent calls in same block allowed; dependent \u2192 wait. So: block1: write_source. Then after result: activate. Then run_tests.\\n\\nLet me go.\",\"type\":\"reasoning_text\"}],\"encrypted_content\":null,\"id\":\"rs_20261007221558d4e914311c654e8d\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"summary\":[],\"type\":\"reasoning\"},{\"content\":[{\"annotations\":[],\"text\":\"I'll start by examining the parsing path. I've already read `ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP` and `ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL`. Let me analyze the bug:\\n\\n**Root cause found:** `zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003esplit_entries` and `extract_balanced` count `{`/`}` (and `[`/`]`) with no awareness of JSON string literals. JSON does *not* escape braces, so an ABAP literal like `lv_open = '{'.` inside the `arguments` string value adds +1 depth that never closes. In `split_entries` the entry never reaches depth 0, so both calls merge into one \\\"entry\\\"; in `extract_balanced` the arguments scan runs past the value end and appends `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`. The second call is lost.\\n\\nFirst, a red reproduction test. `ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP` has an empty test include, so I write a new one:\",\"type\":\"output_text\"}],\"id\":\"msg_20261007221558d4e914311c654e8d\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"message\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"include\\\":\\\"testclasses\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"\\\\\\\"* Unit tests for the tool-call parsing of zcl_pia_00_llm_http.\\\\n\\\\nCLASS ltcl_parse_calls DEFINITION FINAL FOR TESTING\\\\n  RISK LEVEL HARMLESS DURATION SHORT.\\\\n\\\\n  PRIVATE SECTION.\\\\n    \\\\\\\" regression: a single opening brace inside a string value of the first\\\\n    \\\\\\\" call's arguments (JSON does not escape braces, e.g. an ABAP literal '{')\\\\n    \\\\\\\" used to make the raw brace counting run past the end of the value: the\\\\n    \\\\\\\" first call got the fields behind \\\\\\\"arguments\\\\\\\" as extra text and every\\\\n    \\\\\\\" following call of the same answer was lost\\\\n    METHODS responses_two_calls_brace_in_string FOR TESTING.\\\\n    METHODS chat_two_calls_brace_in_string FOR TESTING.\\\\nENDCLASS.\\\\n\\\\nCLASS ltcl_parse_calls IMPLEMENTATION.\\\\n\\\\n  METHOD responses_two_calls_brace_in_string.\\\\n    DATA lv_body TYPE string.\\\\n    DATA lt_calls TYPE zif_pia_00_tool=\\u003ett_calls.\\\\n    DATA ls_call LIKE LINE OF lt_calls.\\\\n\\\\n    CONCATENATE\\\\n      `{\\\\\\\"output\\\\\\\":[`\\\\n      `{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"function_call\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"write_method\\\\\\\",`\\\\n      `\\\\\\\"arguments\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"method\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"M1\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":`\\\\n      `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"METHOD m1. lv_open = '{'. ENDMETHOD.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\",`\\\\n      `\\\\\\\"call_id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"c1\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\"},`\\\\n      `{\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"function_call\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"run_tests\\\\\\\",`\\\\n      `\\\\\\\"arguments\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"{\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"}\\\\\\\",`\\\\n      `\\\\\\\"call_id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"c2\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"completed\\\\\\\"}`\\\\n      `]}`\\\\n      INTO lv_body.\\\\n\\\\n    lt_calls = zcl_pia_00_llm_http=\\u003eparse_calls_responses( lv_body ).\\\\n\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals(\\\\n      exp = 2\\\\n      act = lines( lt_calls )\\\\n      msg = 'both function calls of the answer must be found' ).\\\\n\\\\n    READ TABLE lt_calls INDEX 1 INTO ls_call.\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_subrc( msg = 'call 1 exists' ).\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 'c1' act = ls_call-id msg = 'call 1: call_id' ).\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 'write_method' act = ls_call-name msg = 'call 1: name' ).\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals(\\\\n      exp = `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"method\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"M1\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"METHOD m1. lv_open = '{'. ENDMETHOD.\\\\\\\"}`\\\\n      act = ls_call-arguments\\\\n      msg = 'call 1: arguments must end at their own closing brace' ).\\\\n\\\\n    READ TABLE lt_calls INDEX 2 INTO ls_call.\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_subrc( msg = 'call 2 exists' ).\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 'c2' act = ls_call-id msg = 'call 2: call_id' ).\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals( exp = 'run_tests' act = ls_call-name msg = 'call 2: name' ).\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals(\\\\n      exp = `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\"}`\\\\n      act = ls_call-arguments\\\\n      msg = 'call 2: arguments' ).\\\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\\\n\\\\n  METHOD chat_two_calls_brace_in_string.\\\\n    DATA lv_body TYPE 