. With string-awareness, we need: after the key, find the opening delimiter \u2014 but the `{` we want is inside a string. Tricky.\\n\\nBetter approach for extract_balanced generally: the scanner should skip characters inside strings when counting depth, but the initial FIND of iv_open should also consider strings? For the responses case, the desired `{` is inside a string value. If we skip strings when finding the opening `{`, we'd land on the next real `{` (item2) \u2014 wrong.\\n\\nSo how to fix correctly? Options:\\n\\n1. For the responses case, don't use extract_balanced on 'arguments' with braces. Instead: find `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"` then take the string value until the closing unescaped quote (string-aware scan), then unescape it. That's the semantically correct parse: arguments is a JSON string whose content is the object text. Then the result is the inner text `{...}` which after unescape... wait, the string value is the escaped source; extract the string content (between quotes, honoring `\\\\\\\"`), then unescape \u2192 the source. \\n\\n2. Keep extract_balanced for the chat case where arguments is a real nested object.\\n\\nSo the right fix might be twofold:\\n- Add a string-aware helper: extract the JSON string value after a key (e.g., `extract_str_after` or reuse extract_str? extract_str uses PCRE `\\\"((?:[^\\\"\\\\\\\\]|\\\\\\\\.)*)\\\"` which is already string-aware and correct!). Indeed `extract_str( iv_json = lv_e iv_name = 'arguments' )` with the PCRE handles escaped quotes correctly and returns the raw escaped content; then unescape. That fixes the responses case cleanly, and it also fixes the chat case? In the chat case, arguments is a real object, not a string \u2014 extract_str would fail (no `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"` pattern with a quote right after the colon... actually `\\\"arguments\\\":{...}` \u2014 the regex requires `:` then `\\\"`; won't match). So chat keeps extract_balanced, but extract_balanced must become string-aware to not miscount braces inside string values (e.g., a source with unbalanced braces inside a string, like `'{'`).\\n\\nAnd split_entries must become string-aware so entries split at real top-level commas/braces.\\n\\nSo the plan:\\n1. `split_entries`: skip characters inside JSON strings (handling `\\\\\\\"`) when tracking brace depth. Also, entries are separated at depth 0; currently it scans from `{` to matching `}`. Make the inner WHILE string-aware.\\n2. `extract_balanced`: make the scanning loop string-aware (skip chars inside strings). Keep the initial FIND of the opening delimiter as-is (finds first occurrence, even inside a string \u2014 which is what the responses arguments case needs, since the `{` is inside the string value... wait, no: for the responses case I'm switching to extract_str. But other uses of extract_balanced: 'tool_calls' array in chat (opening `[` \u2014 first `[` after key, could that be inside a string? unlikely), 'output' array, 'function' object, 'arguments' object in chat. The opening-delimiter FIND could theoretically match a `{` inside a string earlier than the real one \u2014 e.g., chat item: `\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",...}}` \u2014 after key `function`, first `{` is the real one. Fine. For arguments (chat): after `\\\"arguments\\\"`, first `{` is the real object. Fine. But if `name` came after arguments and the name value contained `{`... no, the FIND happens after the key position, taking the FIRST `{` \u2014 for chat arguments the first `{` after the key is the object opener (correct) unless the JSON has `\\\"arguments\\\":` followed by something weird. OK.\\n\\n   But the depth scanning must skip strings so that `'{'` inside `\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"` doesn't bump depth, and a `}` inside a string doesn't drop it.\\n\\n3. `parse_calls_responses`: replace extract_balanced+unescape for arguments with extract_str+unescape. Wait \u2014 but there's a subtlety: extract_str finds the FIRST `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"...\\\"` \u2014 with the PCRE `[^\\\"\\\\\\\\]|\\\\\\\\.` it correctly skips escaped quotes. The source contains `\\\\'`? ABAP source with `'{'` \u2014 in JSON escaped form, single quotes aren't escaped. The source contains `\\\"` chars (escaped as `\\\\\\\"`) \u2014 handled by `\\\\\\\\.`. Good.\\n\\n   Hmm, but careful: extract_str on the item \u2014 the item also contains `\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\"` etc. The PCRE anchors on `\\\"arguments\\\"` specifically. Good.\\n\\n4. Also `parse_calls_chat` uses extract_balanced for arguments (object) \u2014 keep, but now string-aware. Test the chat scenario where source contains `'{'`.\\n\\nWait, but in the chat format the arguments object contains `\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"` \u2014 with string-aware scanning, extract_balanced returns exactly the object. Then unescape \u2014 the raw JSON contains `\\\\\\\"`? No \u2014 in my constructed chat body, the source value is `\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"` \u2014 no escaped quotes inside. unescape leaves it. Expected = `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}`.\\n\\nAlso `build_input_items` uses extract_str on 'arguments' for chat assistant messages \u2014 assistant raw is `{\\\"tool_calls\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":..,\\\"name\\\":..,\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"\\u003cescaped object\\u003e\\\"}]}`; extract_str returns the escaped object text; embedded into `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"...\\\"` \u2014 fine.\\n\\nNow also the executor: does it re-parse arguments? Let me check ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR quickly to see how calls are executed \u2014 the bug says \\\"PIA runs only the first call\\\". With split_entries fixed, both calls parse. The executor probably just loops over et_calls. Fine.\\n\\nNow, implementing string-aware scanning in split_entries and extract_balanced without regex (PCRE available though \u2014 extract_str uses PCRE, so PCRE is available in this system).\\n\\nFor split_entries: I could rewrite with a helper that scans char by char with an in_string flag:\\n\\n```\\nMETHOD split_entries.\\n  DATA lv_len_arr TYPE i.\\n  lv_len_arr = strlen( iv_arr ).\\n  DATA lv_pos TYPE i VALUE 0.\\n  WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len_arr.\\n    FIND FIRST OCCURRENCE OF '{' IN iv_arr+lv_pos MATCH OFFSET DATA(lv_off).\\n    IF sy-subrc \\u003c\\u003e 0. EXIT. ENDIF.\\n    DATA lv_start TYPE i.\\n    lv_start = lv_pos + lv_off.\\n    IF lv_start \\u003e= lv_len_arr. EXIT. ENDIF.\\n    \\\" walk to the matching closing brace, ignoring braces inside JSON strings\\n    DATA lv_depth TYPE i VALUE 1.\\n    DATA lv_end TYPE i.\\n    lv_end = lv_start.\\n    DATA lv_in_str TYPE abap_bool VALUE abap_false.\\n    WHILE lv_end \\u003c lv_len_arr - 1 AND lv_depth \\u003e 0.\\n      lv_end = lv_end + 1.\\n      IF lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n        IF iv_arr+lv_end(1) = `\\\\`.\\n          lv_end = lv_end + 1. \\\" skip escaped char\\n        ELSEIF iv_arr+lv_end(1) = `\\\"`.\\n          lv_in_str = abap_false.\\n        ENDIF.\\n      ELSEIF iv_arr+lv_end(1) = `\\\"`.\\n        lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n      ELSEIF iv_arr+lv_end(1) = '{'.\\n        lv_depth = lv_depth + 1.\\n      ELSEIF iv_arr+lv_end(1) = '}'.\\n        lv_depth = lv_depth - 1.\\n      ENDIF.\\n    ENDWHILE.\\n    ...\\n```\\n\\nCareful with the `\\\\` skip: if lv_end+1 exceeds array... The WHILE condition re-checks lv_end \\u003c lv_len_arr - 1. If the escaped char is the last char, lv_end+1 could exceed; but then loop condition fails next iteration anyway. But accessing iv_arr+lv_end(1) after increment \u2014 need lv_end \\u003c lv_len_arr guard. Let me guard: after `lv_end = lv_end + 1` for the escape skip, check `IF lv_end \\u003e= lv_len_arr. EXIT. ENDIF.`\\n\\nSame string-aware logic for extract_balanced's depth loop.\\n\\nNow for the responses arguments: switch to extract_str + unescape. But wait \u2014 there's a catch: what if the model sends arguments as a real object in responses API (some backends do)? The official API sends a string. The bug report shows a string. But to be robust, I could try extract_str first; if empty, fall back to extract_balanced. That's a safe superset. Let me do:\\n\\n```\\nls-arguments = zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eunescape( zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eextract_str(\\n                   iv_json = lv_e iv_name = 'arguments' ) ).\\nIF ls-arguments IS INITIAL.\\n  ls-arguments = zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eunescape( zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eextract_balanced(\\n                   iv_json = lv_e iv_key = 'arguments' iv_open = '{' iv_close = '}' ) ).\\nENDIF.\\n```\\n\\nHmm, but extract_str would also match an arguments value that is an empty string; fallback then. OK.\\n\\nWait \u2014 one more consideration for the responses case: extract_str with PCRE `\\\"arguments\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"((?:[^\\\"\\\\\\\\]|\\\\\\\\.)*)\\\"`. The escaped source contains `\\\\\\\\n` (backslash backslash n) and `\\\\\\\"`? Our source contains `'` and `{` `}` \u2014 no double quotes. In general it handles them. Good.\\n\\nNow, about the JSON_UTIL tests: the existing tests there describe current behavior. My changes to split_entries and extract_balanced could break existing tests in ltcl_json_util. Let me check which ones touch these:\\n\\n- balanced_object: `{\\\"o\\\":{\\\"x\\\":1},\\\"y\\\":2}` key o \u2192 `{\\\"x\\\":1}` \u2014 string-aware scanning unchanged. \u2713\\n- balanced_nested_and_array \u2713\\n- balanced_missing_key \u2713\\n- balanced_no_open_bracket: `{\\\"o\\\":\\\"txt\\\"}` \u2014 key 'o' found at... wait, FIND `\\\"o\\\"` in `{\\\"o\\\":\\\"txt\\\"}` matches at offset 1. lv_rest = `\\\"o\\\":\\\"txt\\\"}`. First `{` in lv_rest? None \u2192 return empty. With my change, unchanged (I keep the initial FIND non-string-aware? If I made the initial FIND string-aware it'd still find none). \u2713\\n- balanced_unclosed: `{\\\"o\\\":{\\\"x\\\":1` \u2192 tail `{\\\"x\\\":1` (depth never 0, loop ends at strlen-1). With string-aware: same. \u2713\\n- No split_entries tests exist in ltcl_json_util! Interesting \u2014 split_entries has no direct tests there. OK.\\n\\nAlso ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR is red (known) \u2014 leave.\\n\\nNow the executor: does it also parse? Let me look at ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR to see if tool arguments are re-parsed with json_util (e.g., extracting 'name'/'method'/'source' from arguments via extract_str). If it uses extract_str (PCRE, string-aware), fine. Let me read it to be sure there's no second occurrence of the bug.\\n\\nLet me now:\\n1. Read ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR (main) to check for related parsing.\\n2. Fix the test file (chat expected value via concatenation, responses item key order with call_id/status after arguments).\\n3. Activate, run tests \u2192 expect my 2 new tests red (and emoji red pre-existing).\\n4. Fix JSON_UTIL (split_entries + extract_balanced string-aware) and parse_calls_responses (extract_str first).\\n5. Activate, run tests \u2192 green except emoji.\\n\\nNote: test classes for ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP are new; run_tests(name=ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP) runs them. Also run ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL tests after the fix.\\n\\nLet me read the executor first.\",\"type\":\"reasoning_text\"}],\"encrypted_content\":null,\"id\":\"rs_20261007222259bdba390a56ee4ddc\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"summary\":[],\"type\":\"reasoning\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7228015271485628573\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7228015271485628573\",\"name\":\"read_object\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"}],\"parallel_tool_calls\":true,\"previous_response_id\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":13419,\"input_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":13248},\"output_tokens\":6423,\"output_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":6402},\"total_tokens\":19842}}
{\"conversation_id\":\"\",\"created_at\":1791383241,\"error\":null,\"id\":\"resp_2026100722243632ba4ceb11064a9c\",\"incomplete_details\":null,\"instructions\":\"You are PIA (Pi-ABAP Agent), written in ABAP, running on the model glm-5.3 (z.ai) through backend OSG-STORE. If asked which model you are, say exactly that; never claim another model or vendor. Tools: outline(name), read_method(name, method, class), write_method(name, method, source - one METHOD block, class), read_object(name, include main|testclasses), write_source(name, source - FULL source, include), activate(name), run_tests(name). Rules: prefer outline, read_method and write_method for changing existing methods; use write_source only for new methods, changed declarations or a new test include, and then write the FULL source; read before write; always activate after write; if activation says publish pending, finish the turn and run_tests in the next turn. Answer briefly in the user language. Your own code: ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR (agent loop), ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (LLM client: builds requests, parses responses and tool calls), ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL (JSON helpers), ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION and ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION_STORE (conversation), ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY (tools), ZCL_PIA_15_T_* (the tools), ZCL_PIA_20_B_* (backends), ZCL_PIA_30_* (terminal, turns). Unit tests live in the testclasses include of a class.\",\"metadata\":null,\"model\":\"glm-5.3\",\"object\":\"response\",\"output\":[{\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Line 80: the second `cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals(` in chat test \u2014 the `exp = |{\\\\n ...|` string template line? Line 80 col 5... let me count. The issue: inside the string template `|{\\\\n    \\\"name\\\"...` \u2014 hmm, in a string template, `{` starts an embedded expression! `\\\\n` inside a template literal: `|...|` \u2014 embedded expressions are `{ expr }`. So `|{\\\\n` \u2014 `{` opens an embedded expression, `\\\\n` then `    \\\"name\\\"` \u2014 that's invalid ABAP expression \u2192 parser error attributed to the next statement maybe.\\n\\nSo I cannot use string templates with literal `{`. Better build the expected value with concatenation of backquotes:\\n\\n```\\n    lv_exp = `{` \\u0026\\u0026 `\\\\n` \\u0026\\u0026 `    \\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",` \\u0026\\u0026 `\\\\n` \\u0026\\u0026 `    \\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"` \\u0026\\u0026 `\\\\n` \\u0026\\u0026 `  }`.\\n```\\n\\nWait \u2014 but what exactly does extract_balanced produce for arguments in the chat case? The entry:\\n\\n```\\n{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}}}},\\n```\\n\\nWith the fixed split (string-aware), entry1 = `{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}}}}`.\\n\\nextract_balanced on arguments with old depth logic: first `{` after `\\\"arguments\\\"` is the arguments object's. depth: at `{` of arguments \u21921. Then `{` inside string? No\u2014inside string `'{'` is a char... but scanning: after arguments `{`, next chars `\\\"name\\\":...`. The `'{'` inside the string increments depth wrongly \u21921... wait let's count: depth starts 1 at arguments `{`. Scanning forward: `\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}}}}` \u2014 the `{` inside the string makes depth 2, then each `}` decrements: `}}}}` \u2192 3,2,1,0. So it ends one `}` early... actually ends at the 4th `}` \u2014 wait, it consumed all four `}` and depth reached 0 at the 4th? depth: 1 \u2192 +1 (string `{`) = 2 \u2192 `}` =1 \u2192 `}`=0? No wait: after the string's `{`, depth=2. Then `}}}}`: first `}` \u21921, second \u21920. So extract_balanced returns `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}` + `}`? Hmm, position: returns from arguments `{` through the `}` where depth hits 0, which is the 2nd of the four `}`. The four `}` close: arguments-object, function-object, entry-object, plus one extra... entry is `{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",...,\\\"function\\\":{...}}` and then `}}` more close tool_calls array and choices... Actually the full structure: `...arguments\\\":{...}}}}` closes: arguments, function, entry, and tool_calls-array? No\u2014tool_calls array closes with `]`. In my body: `\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}}}},` \u2014 four `}`: close arguments, close function, close entry, close \\\"message\\\" object. Then `]` closes tool_calls, `}` closes choices-entry, `]` closes choices, `}` closes root. And the second call similar with `}}}}` closing message, then `]}}]}`.\\n\\nOK so exact expected string for chat arguments after fix: `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}` \u2014 the balanced object. Since I'd have to fix extract_balanced to be string-aware too for the chat path to work... wait does the chat path need extract_balanced fix? With the fixed split_entries (string-aware), entry1 is correct. Then extract_balanced(iv_key='arguments', '{', '}') with string-unaware depth: as computed, it returns arguments-object minus the final `}`? Let me recount precisely.\\n\\nSubstring from arguments: `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}}}}`,...\\n\\nlv_start at the `{` of arguments. depth=1. Scan:\\n- `{` (string) \u2192 depth 2\\n- `}` (1st of the four) \u2192 depth 1\\n- `}` (2nd) \u2192 depth 0 \u2192 stop.\\n\\nReturn = `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}` + the 2nd `}`? The return is lv_rest+lv_start(lv_len) where lv_len = lv_pos - lv_start + 1, lv_pos = position of the char that brought depth to 0, i.e. the 2nd `}`. So the result includes both `}`: `{\\\"name\\\":...,\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}}`.\\n\\nHmm wait: the string content is `\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"` \u2014 the `{` inside. depth after arguments-open = 1, then `{` in string \u2192 2, then `.` `\\\"` then `}}}}`. First `}` \u2192 1, second \u2192 0, stop at second `}`. Result: `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}}` \u2014 includes one extra `}` (the one that closes `function` in reality). So chat arguments would get `...}}` \u2014 wrong-ish but arguably harmless? It's `{\\\"name\\\":...}}` which as JSON has an extra closing brace. Then unescape... The expected value I wrote was `{\\\\n    \\\"name\\\"...` \u2014 wait, I wrote expected with `\\\\n`? That's wrong \u2014 the body I constructed has no newlines. Let me fix: expected = `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}}` per current behavior, or should I also make extract_balanced string-aware and expect `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}`?\\n\\nFor the bug report: the responses path is the broken one. The chat path with old split_entries: would `{` inside string break split too? Entry scanning in split_entries counts `{`/`}` only, so the `'{'` in string also confuses it: entry1 would end at... it scans from `{` of entry1: depth 1; `{` of function \u21922; `{` of arguments \u21923; `{` in string \u21924; `}` \u21923; `}` \u21922; `}` \u21921; `}` \u21920 \u2192 entry1 = correct full entry1! Because there are exactly 4 `{` (entry, function, arguments, string) and the string-brace is spurious but there's also an extra real closing... hmm actually entry1 has 3 real opens (entry, function, arguments) + 1 fake (string) = depth reaches 0 after 4 closes, but only 3 closes belong to entry1 (arguments, function, entry) \u2014 the 4th `}` closes `message`. So old split would make entry1 = `{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",...'{'.\\\",}}}}`?? No wait \u2014 entry1's text ends with `}}}},` \u2014 the four `}` are: arguments-close, function-close, entry-close, message-close. Depth needs 4 decrements; 3 real opens + 1 fake = 4. So entry1 = full entry1 including message's closing `}`. Then entry2 starts after. So chat happens to survive here (lucky), though it's fragile.\\n\\nFor the fix, the cleanest is to make both split_entries and extract_balanced string-aware (skip chars inside JSON strings, respecting escapes). That fixes both paths robustly. Then chat arguments = `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}` exactly.\\n\\nSo the plan:\\n1. Fix `split_entries`: when scanning, track in-string state (toggle on unescaped `\\\"`, skip escaped chars).\\n2. Fix `extract_balanced`: same string-awareness when counting depth.\\n3. Tests: two tests in ltcl_parse_calls of ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (responses + chat), plus maybe unit tests in JSON_UTIL testclasses for split_entries/extract_balanced with braces in strings. But careful: existing JSON_UTIL tests document SUSPECT behaviors like balanced_unclosed (returns tail) \u2014 keep that behavior. My fix must not break existing tests: balanced_object, balanced_nested_and_array, balanced_missing_key, balanced_no_open_bracket, balanced_unclosed.\\n\\nAlso existing test `roundtrip_nonascii` expects unescape(escape(`\u0416\u20AC`)) = `\u0416\u20AC` \u2014 that test exists and presumably passes now (unescape handles \\\\u). Fine.\\n\\nAlso must keep behavior: extract_balanced finds key `\\\"key\\\"` then first open char after. Note: the key search uses plain FIND of `\\\"output\\\"` \u2014 could match inside a string value; not our concern now.\\n\\nNow the current test file: fix the chat expected value (no string template with `{`). Replace with concatenation of backquotes. Let me rewrite the whole test include carefully avoiding:\\n- method names \u2264 30 chars: `responses_two_calls_one_brace` = 29 \u2713, `chat_two_calls_one_brace` = 24 \u2713.\\n- No inline `DATA lv_body TYPE string.` with concatenation on same statement \u2014 actually the earlier parser error \\\"Statement does not exist ... lv_body\\\" at line with `DATA lv_body TYPE string.` followed by chained `\\u0026\\u0026`? Hmm, the error was at LINE 20 COL 5 `lv_body` \u2014 that was probably the multi-line concatenation where the chain started... Actually I suspect the issue was `DATA lv_body TYPE string.` then `lv_body = `{` \\u0026\\u0026 ... `.` \u2014 no wait. Possibly the parser complained because a backquote string containing `{` inside \\u0026\\u0026 chain is fine... The responses test in the current file compiled past line 20 (error was at line 80 only). So current form is OK. Only line 80's string template `|{\\\\n...|` is the problem \u2014 string template with `{` starts embedded expression. Fix that.\\n\\nAlso note: expected for chat after fix = `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}`. Build with backquotes:\\n\\n```\\n    DATA lv_exp1 TYPE string.\\n    lv_exp1 = `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}`.\\n```\\n\\nWait \u2014 can a backquote literal contain `{`? Yes, backticks are raw strings, no embedded expressions. The responses test already uses `` `{` `` and compiled. Good.\\n\\nAlso for the responses test expected lv_a1: escape(`{` + `\\\\n`...) \u2014 hold on: escape() of the literal backslash-n characters. lv_a1 = `{` \\u0026\\u0026 `\\\\n` \\u0026\\u0026 ... \u2014 in backquotes, `\\\\n` is two chars backslash+n. escape turns `\\\\` into `\\\\\\\\` \u2192 `\\\\\\\\n`. So arguments value in JSON = `{\\\\\\\\n    lv_open = '{'.\\\\\\\\n  }`. unescape gives back `{` `\\\\n`(2 chars) etc. = lv_a1. \u2713.\\n\\nNow, does the JSON `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\\n    lv_open = '{'.\\\\\\\\n  }\\\"` parse correctly with the fixed split? The value contains `{` (from `lv_open = '{'.`) inside a JSON string \u2192 skipped when in-string. \u2713.\\n\\nAlso need to double check the responses body's first entry: `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"...\\\"` \u2014 the value has escaped backslashes `\\\\\\\\` \u2014 my string-aware scanner must handle `\\\\\\\"` and `\\\\\\\\` escapes: when in string and char is `\\\\`, skip next char.\\n\\nNow write the fix in zcl_pia_00_json_util:\\n\\nsplit_entries new implementation:\\n\\n```\\nMETHOD split_entries.\\n  DATA lv_len_arr TYPE i.\\n  lv_len_arr = strlen( iv_arr ).\\n  DATA lv_pos TYPE i VALUE 0.\\n  WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len_arr.\\n    FIND FIRST OCCURRENCE OF '{' IN iv_arr+lv_pos MATCH OFFSET DATA(lv_off).\\n    IF sy-subrc \\u003c\\u003e 0. EXIT. ENDIF.\\n    DATA lv_start TYPE i.\\n    lv_start = lv_pos + lv_off.\\n    IF lv_start \\u003e= lv_len_arr. EXIT. ENDIF.\\n    DATA lv_depth TYPE i VALUE 1.\\n    DATA lv_end TYPE i.\\n    lv_end = lv_start.\\n    DATA lv_in_str TYPE abap_bool VALUE abap_false.\\n    WHILE lv_end \\u003c lv_len_arr - 1 AND lv_depth \\u003e 0.\\n      lv_end = lv_end + 1.\\n      IF lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n        IF iv_arr+lv_end(1) = `\\\\`.\\n          lv_end = lv_end + 1.  \\\" skip escaped char\\n        ELSEIF iv_arr+lv_end(1) = `\\\"`.\\n          lv_in_str = abap_false.\\n        ENDIF.\\n      ELSEIF iv_arr+lv_end(1) = `\\\"`.\\n        lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n      ELSEIF iv_arr+lv_end(1) = '{'.\\n        lv_depth = lv_depth + 1.\\n      ELSEIF iv_arr+lv_end(1) = '}'.\\n        lv_depth = lv_depth - 1.\\n      ENDIF.\\n    ENDWHILE.\\n    ...\\n```\\n\\nCareful: skipping escaped char with lv_end+1 could run past bounds; the WHILE condition checks lv_end \\u003c lv_len_arr - 1 before increment. If `\\\\` is last char, lv_end+1 = lv_len_arr-? Access iv_arr+lv_end(1) with lv_end = lv_len_arr would dump. Guard: IF lv_end + 1 \\u003c lv_len_arr. Let me write:\\n\\n```\\n      IF lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n        IF iv_arr+lv_end(1) = `\\\\`.\\n          IF lv_end + 1 \\u003c lv_len_arr.\\n            lv_end = lv_end + 1.\\n          ENDIF.\\n        ELSEIF iv_arr+lv_end(1) = `\\\"`.\\n          lv_in_str = abap_false.\\n        ENDIF.\\n```\\n\\nIf depth never reaches 0 (unclosed), old code sets lv_end = lv_len_arr - 1 \u2192 keep.\\n\\nextract_balanced similarly:\\n\\n```\\nMETHOD extract_balanced.\\n  DATA lv_off TYPE i.\\n  DATA(lv_pat) = '\\\"' \\u0026\\u0026 iv_key \\u0026\\u0026 '\\\"'.\\n  FIND lv_pat IN iv_json MATCH OFFSET lv_off.\\n  IF sy-subrc \\u003c\\u003e 0. RETURN. ENDIF.\\n  DATA lv_rest TYPE string.\\n  lv_rest = iv_json+lv_off.\\n  DATA lv_start TYPE i.\\n  FIND FIRST OCCURRENCE OF iv_open IN lv_rest MATCH OFFSET lv_start.\\n  IF sy-subrc \\u003c\\u003e 0. RETURN. ENDIF.\\n  DATA lv_depth TYPE i VALUE 1.\\n  DATA lv_pos TYPE i.\\n  lv_pos = lv_start.\\n  DATA lv_in_str TYPE abap_bool VALUE abap_false.\\n  DATA lv_len TYPE i.\\n  lv_len = strlen( lv_rest ).\\n  WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len - 1 AND lv_depth \\u003e 0.\\n    lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\n    IF lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n      IF lv_rest+lv_pos(1) = `\\\\`.\\n        IF lv_pos + 1 \\u003c lv_len.\\n          lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\n        ENDIF.\\n      ELSEIF lv_rest+lv_pos(1) = `\\\"`.\\n        lv_in_str = abap_false.\\n      ENDIF.\\n    ELSEIF lv_rest+lv_pos(1) = `\\\"`.\\n      lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n    ELSEIF lv_rest+lv_pos(1) = iv_open.\\n      lv_depth = lv_depth + 1.\\n    ELSEIF lv_rest+lv_pos(1) = iv_close.\\n      lv_depth = lv_depth - 1.\\n    ENDIF.\\n  ENDWHILE.\\n  DATA(lv_l) = lv_pos - lv_start + 1.\\n  rv_ = lv_rest+lv_start(lv_l).\\nENDMETHOD.\\n```\\n\\nHmm one subtlety: FIND FIRST OCCURRENCE OF iv_open \u2014 the open char could be inside a string before the real open. E.g. `{\\\"o\\\":\\\"[\\\",\\\"a\\\":[1]}` with key `a`, open `[` \u2192 finds the `[` inside the string value. Edge case; not required for the bug. But also for key `arguments` where earlier fields contain the open char in strings? For robustness could scan for first open not-in-string, but that complicates. The bug scenario: `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\\\\\"}\\\"` (responses). Key find: `\\\"arguments\\\"` then first `{` after \u2014 the value starts with `{` (the arguments JSON object) since the `{` in `lv_open` comes later. Fine.\\n\\nBut wait \u2014 for the responses path, before reaching arguments there's `\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\"` \u2014 no braces. OK.\\n\\nHowever, consider chat entry: `\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":...,\\\"arguments\\\":{...` \u2014 after key `\\\"arguments\\\"` the first `{` is the arguments object. \u2713.\\n\\nOne more: FIND lv_pat IN iv_json \u2014 plain substring, not PCRE; `\\\"arguments\\\"` could match `\\\"arguments\\\"` inside a string value too. Ignore.\\n\\nAlso should I make the initial FIND of iv_open string-aware? Consider responses first entry where `\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\"` precedes; fine. But imagine arguments value string containing `{` before... the value IS after the key. The first `{` after the key position: in responses, arguments value = `{\\\\\\\\n    lv_open = '{'.\\\\\\\\n  }` \u2014 starts with `{`. In string-aware depth scan, that opening `{` is inside a JSON string! Uh oh. For the responses format, arguments is a STRING containing JSON, not a nested object. extract_balanced extracts the balanced region starting at the first `{` after the key \u2014 which is inside the string value \u2014 and then counts braces without string awareness relative to the *extracted* content... Hmm wait.\\n\\nLet me re-examine: responses entry:\\n\\n`{\\\"id\\\":\\\"a\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\n    lv_open = '{'.\\\\n  }\\\"}`\\n\\n(after escape, arguments value chars: `{`, `\\\\`(2 chars: backslash n)... The extract_balanced(iv_key='arguments', '{', '}'): lv_rest starts at `\\\"arguments\\\"`. FIND first `{` in lv_rest \u2192 the `{` at the start of the value string (it's inside a JSON string but FIND doesn't know). lv_start = that `{`. Then depth scan over remaining: with my string-aware fix, the scanner starts lv_in_str = false \u2014 but we're actually inside a string! The chars after `{` are `\\\\` `n` \u2014 scanner sees `\\\\` while not in-string \u2192 it's not `\\\"` or `{`/`}` \u2192 ignored. Then `    lv_open = ` then `{` \u2192 depth 2! Then `'` `.` `\\\\` `n` `  ` then `}` \u2192 depth 1, then `\\\"` then `}` \u2192 depth 0. Result: `{` ... up to that final `}` = `{\\\\n    lv_open = '{'.\\\\n  }\\\"` \u2014 includes the closing quote! Hmm. That gives arguments = `{\\\\\\\\n    lv_open = '{'.\\\\\\\\n  }\\\"` and then unescape \u2192 lv_a1 + `\\\"`. Extra quote. Hmm.\\n\\nWait \u2014 but does the scanner see the `\\\"` before the last `}`? Sequence at end: `...  }\\\"}`. Chars: `}`, `\\\"`, `}`. Scanner: `{`(start, depth1) ... `}` \u2192 depth 0? Let me redo: value = `{` `\\\\` `n` ` ` ` ` ` ` ` ` `l`...`=` ` ` `'` `{` `'` `.` `\\\\` `n` ` ` ` ` `}` `\\\"` `}`.\\n\\ndepth=1 at start `{`. Scan:\\n- `\\\\` not in string \u2192 ignore (it's not `\\\"`).\\n- `n`, spaces, `lv_open = ` ignore.\\n- `{` \u2192 depth 2.\\n- `'`, `.`, `\\\\`, `n`, spaces ignore.\\n- `}` \u2192 depth 1.\\n- `\\\"` \u2192 in-string = true (wrongly, we're leaving the string).\\n- `}` \u2192 in string \u2192 ignored! depth stays 1.\\n- ...continues to next entry: `,` `\\\"` (already in string, not `\\\"`... wait `\\\"` while in_str \u2192 toggles off? My code: in_str and char = `\\\"` \u2192 in_str = false. The `,` then `\\\"` \u2192 in_str=false. Then `{`? Next entry starts `{\\\"id\\\":\\\"b\\\"...` \u2192 `{` \u2192 depth 2... \\n\\nUgh. Messy. The old (non-string-aware) code for extract_balanced on this input: start `{` depth1, `{` in string \u2192 2, `}` \u2192 1, `}` \u2192 0 \u2192 returns `{...}\\\"}`? Old: chars after the value's inner `{`: `}` \u21921, `\\\"` ignored, `}` \u2192 0. Return includes final `}`: arguments = `{\\\\\\\\n    lv_open = '{'.\\\\\\\\n  }\\\"}` \u2014 wait that includes `\\\"}`. Hmm, actually old behavior gave the reported bug differently: the reported symptom is arguments arrive with extra text `,\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`. That comes from the FIRST `}` closing early: In the real transcript the arguments value is `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\\n  }\\\"}` maybe? The first `}` encountered after start is the one closing the arguments JSON object (depth 1\u21920)?? No wait, that would be correct...\\n\\nLet me recompute the reported symptom: arguments arrive with `,\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"})` appended. That means extract_balanced ran past the arguments value's closing `\\\"` and `}` and through the rest of the entry until some later `}`. For that, depth must have been \\u003e1 at the arguments object's real close. Why? Because the `{` inside `lv_open = '{'.` incremented depth: depth 1 \u2192 2 at the string `{`; then `}` (arguments close... wait the arguments value `{...}` then `\\\"` then `}` closes the entry object.\\n\\nResponses entry: `...\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"method\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"M1\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\\\\\"}\\\"}`. Raw chars after `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"`: `{` (depth1, start), `\\\\`,`\\\"`,... value contains `'{'.` \u2192 `{` depth2, then value ends `\\\\\\\"` `}` \u2014 the `}` closes the arguments-JSON (as a character inside the string) \u2192 depth 1, then `\\\"` (end of string), then `}` closes entry \u2192 depth 0. Return = `{...\\\\n  }\\\"}`?? That includes `\\\"` and `}`. Hmm that ends at the entry's closing brace \u2014 result: `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",...,\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}` + ... \\n\\nHold on. The value string (unescaped form) is `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}` \u2014 wait in the actual scenario from my earlier test I had value `{` `\\\\n` `    lv_open = '{'.` `\\\\n` `  }`. After escape in the wire JSON: `{\\\\\\\\n    lv_open = '{'.\\\\\\\\n  }` \u2014 no escaped quotes needed since I used single quotes in the ABAP source. The wire chars: `{` `\\\\` `n` ` ` ... `'` `{` `'` `.` `\\\\` `n` ` ` ` ` `}` `\\\"` `}` (last two: close of arguments-string, close of entry).\\n\\nOld extract_balanced: start at first `{` (value start), depth 1. Scan: `\\\\`n etc ignored; `{` \u2192 2; `}` \u2192 1; `\\\"` ignored; `}` \u2192 0. End. Return = value + `\\\"}`. unescape \u2192 `{` `\\\\n` `    lv_open = '{'.` `\\\\n` `  }\\\"` + `}`... i.e. lv_a1 + `\\\"}`. Extra text = `\\\"}`. The bug report says extra text `,\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` \u2014 that's a different shape (fields AFTER arguments in the entry, e.g. arguments comes before call_id/status in the real transcript: `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"...\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`). So in the real body, entry = `{\\\"id\\\":\\\"a\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"...{...}...\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`. extract_balanced: value starts `{` depth1... but the value `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"...\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"... '{' ...\\\"}` \u2014 inner `{` in string bumps depth; the value's real closing `}` brings depth 1, then `\\\"` ignored, `,` `\\\"call_id\\\"...` ignored, and the entry's final `}` brings depth 0. Result = everything from value start to entry end including `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`. And split_entries similarly mis-splits: entry1 swallows beyond, second call lost. Exactly the reported symptom. \\n\\nSo for the responses path, the right fix must handle: arguments is a JSON string whose CONTENT is JSON with braces. The old code \\\"works\\\" when content has balanced braces. The robust fix for parse of the arguments in responses format: extract the string value of `\\\"arguments\\\"` (extract_str + unescape) rather than extract_balanced! Look at parse_calls_responses:\\n\\n```\\nls-arguments = unescape( extract_balanced( iv_key='arguments' '{' '}' ) ).\\n```\\n\\nIf instead: `ls-arguments = unescape( extract_str( iv_json = lv_e iv_name = 'arguments' ) )` \u2014 extract_str is a regex `'\\\"arguments\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"((?:[^\\\"\\\\\\\\]|\\\\\\\\.)*)\\\"'` which correctly handles escaped quotes and stops at the real end of the string. That's the clean fix for responses. For the chat format, arguments is a real nested object, so extract_balanced is appropriate (with string-awareness for `{` inside string values like `'{'.`).\\n\\nHmm, but extract_str uses PCRE `((?:[^\\\"\\\\\\\\]|\\\\\\\\.)*)` \u2014 fine in OSG/SAP? It's already used elsewhere (id, name, content). Yes.\\n\\nSo minimal, targeted fixes:\\n1. `parse_calls_responses`: use extract_str + unescape for arguments. (Fixes: correct arguments, no swallowing of following fields.)\\n2. `split_entries`: string-aware brace counting. (Fixes: second call not lost.)\\n3. `extract_balanced`: string-aware depth for chat arguments (`'{'` inside string values). With string-awareness, chat entry `\\\"arguments\\\":{\\\"name\\\":...,\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}` \u2192 correct balanced extraction.\\n\\nNow for my responses test: with fix 1, arguments = unescape(extract_str) = lv_a1 exactly \u2713.\\n\\nFor chat test: split (fix 2) gives correct entries; extract_balanced (fix 3) gives `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}` \u2713.\\n\\nNow check existing JSON_UTIL tests still pass with fixes:\\n- split_entries has no direct unit tests in ltcl_json_util (tests are for escape/unescape/extract_str/extract_balanced/to_int...). Right \u2014 no split_entries tests. Good.\\n- extract_balanced tests: balanced_object `{\\\"o\\\":{\\\"x\\\":1},\\\"y\\\":2}` \u2192 `{\\\"x\\\":1}` \u2713 (no strings with braces). balanced_nested_and_array \u2713. missing \u2192 '' \u2713. no_open_bracket \u2713. unclosed `{\\\"o\\\":{\\\"x\\\":1` \u2192 tail `{\\\"x\\\":1` \u2713 (depth never 0, lv_pos ends at len-1, returns tail). My fix keeps that.\\n\\nNow also verify extract_balanced change doesn't break when value strings contain `\\\"` escaped... fine.\\n\\nOne more check on split_entries fix with the responses array: entries separated by `,` at top level; string-aware counting: entry1 contains `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\\n    lv_open = '{'.\\\\\\\\n  }\\\"` \u2014 scanner enters string at each `\\\"`, and the `{` inside the arguments-value string is skipped because we're in-string. Wait \u2014 careful: the arguments value is itself inside a JSON string: `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\n    lv_open = '{'.\\\\n  }\\\"`. Between the quotes of the arguments value, ALL content (including `{` and `}`) is in-string \u2192 skipped. Entry1 closes at its real `}`. \u2713 Then entry2 parsed. \u2713\\n\\nAnd the escaped-backslash handling: value contains `\\\\\\\\` (escaped backslash) then `n`. Scanner in string sees `\\\\` \u2192 skip next char (`n`). \u2713. Also `\\\\\\\"` inside strings \u2192 skipped. \u2713\\n\\nNow also the chat body: tool_calls array entries contain nested strings with `'{'` \u2014 `'` isn't a JSON string delimiter, but the `{` is inside `\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"` \u2192 in-string \u2192 skipped \u2713.\\n\\nNow, about parse_calls_responses using extract_str: the entry also has `\\\"call_id\\\"`, `\\\"name\\\"`, `\\\"id\\\"`. extract_str(iv_name='name') \u2014 careful: `\\\"name\\\"` also appears... entry fields: id, type, status, call_id, name, arguments. extract_str 'name' regex `\\\"name\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"...\\\"` \u2014 matches the entry's name field \u2713 (could also match `\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\"` inside the arguments string! The regex FIND FIRST occurrence \u2014 `\\\"name\\\"` first occurrence in the entry is the entry's own name field (before arguments). In my test entry, order: id, type, status, call_id, name, arguments \u2014 name comes before arguments \u2713. But in general, if arguments came before name, `\\\"name\\\"` inside arguments string would match first. Existing code already has this issue for 'name' via extract_str? No \u2014 currently name uses extract_str too (`ls-name = extract_str( iv_json = lv_e iv_name = 'name' )`). Yes already extract_str. So same behavior. Fine.\\n\\nWait, actually currently: `ls-name = zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eextract_str( iv_json = lv_e iv_name = 'name' )` \u2014 yes. OK.\\n\\nBut hmm, one risk with extract_str for arguments: if the arguments string value contains the substring `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"` ... no.\\n\\nAlso \u2014 `zif_pia_00_llm~chat` for responses api also extracts output_text via extract_str 'text' \u2014 untouched.\\n\\nNow the executor: does it use parse_calls or its own? Let me check ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR quickly for how it dispatches multiple calls, to be sure nothing else loses the second call (e.g., session handling of multiple calls). Also build_input_items / ev_assistant_raw re-escape: `escape( ls_c-arguments )` \u2014 arguments containing `{` fine.\\n\\nAlso check: `ev_assistant_raw` construction escapes arguments \u2014 with correct arguments now, replay of multi-turn works.\\n\\nLet me also look at the executor to see if it executes all calls in et_calls. Quick read of executor.\\n\\nThen implement:\\n1. write_method for split_entries (full new METHOD block).\\n2. write_method for extract_balanced.\\n3. write_method for parse_calls_responses (change arguments line to extract_str+unescape).\\n4. Add tests: fix the chat expected (remove string template) in testclasses; maybe also add JSON_UTIL tests for split_entries with brace-in-string? The task says \\\"write an ABAP Unit test that reproduces it and is red\\\". The two tests in ltcl_parse_calls reproduce it. I could also add extract_balanced/split_entries tests in JSON_UTIL testclasses \u2014 nice but optional; I'll add two focused ones (split_brace_in_string, balanced_brace_in_string) to lock the helper behavior. Method names \u226430: `split_entries_brace_in_string` = 27 \u2713, `balanced_brace_in_string` = 25 \u2713.\\n\\nBut wait: test must be RED before fix. Order: first write tests, activate, run \u2192 red (only new ones fail, ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR already red). Then fix, activate, run \u2192 green (except emoji). The activation of testclasses alone: allowed (testclasses belong to class ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP; activation publishes both includes). run_tests runs on published code. Good \u2014 do tests first, run, show red. Then fix JSON_UTIL + parse method, activate, run both classes' tests.\\n\\nCurrent testclasses file needs fixing at line ~80 (string template). Rewrite full include with corrected chat expected. Expected chat arguments after fix: `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}`. As backquote literal \u2014 contains `'` and `{` \u2014 fine in backquotes.\\n\\nAlso assert entry count 2 and ids \u2014 the core regression.\\n\\nAlso the responses test asserts arguments = lv_a1 exactly (no extra `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\"...`).\\n\\nLet me also double check the red state before fix for responses test:\\n- Old parse: split_entries on output array with entries containing `{`/`}` inside strings \u2192 entry boundaries wrong. Entry1: scan from first `{`: depth 1; strings skipped? NO \u2014 old code doesn't skip strings: `{` of value \u2192 2, `}` \u2192 1, `}` \u2192 0 \u2192 entry1 ends at the `}` closing the entry (which is the 2nd `}` after value close)... \\n\\nwait, entry1 = `{\\\"id\\\":\\\"a\\\",...,\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\\n    lv_open = '{'.\\\\\\\\n  }\\\"}`. Old scan: `{`(