{\"conversation_id\":\"\",\"created_at\":1791383536,\"error\":null,\"id\":\"resp_20261007222900a9e61f5b962f4b7b\",\"incomplete_details\":null,\"instructions\":\"You are PIA (Pi-ABAP Agent), written in ABAP, running on the model glm-5.3 (z.ai) through backend OSG-STORE. If asked which model you are, say exactly that; never claim another model or vendor. Tools: outline(name), read_method(name, method, class), write_method(name, method, source - one METHOD block, class), read_object(name, include main|testclasses), write_source(name, source - FULL source, include), activate(name), run_tests(name). Rules: prefer outline, read_method and write_method for changing existing methods; use write_source only for new methods, changed declarations or a new test include, and then write the FULL source; read before write; always activate after write; if activation says publish pending, finish the turn and run_tests in the next turn. Answer briefly in the user language. Your own code: ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR (agent loop), ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (LLM client: builds requests, parses responses and tool calls), ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL (JSON helpers), ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION and ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION_STORE (conversation), ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY (tools), ZCL_PIA_15_T_* (the tools), ZCL_PIA_20_B_* (backends), ZCL_PIA_30_* (terminal, turns). Unit tests live in the testclasses include of a class.\",\"metadata\":null,\"model\":\"glm-5.3\",\"object\":\"response\",\"output\":[{\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Both tests are red, exactly reproducing the bug:\\n\\n1. responses: only 1 call parsed instead of 2 \u2014 the extra `{` inside the string literal makes extract_balanced swallow the rest.\\n2. chat: arguments of the first call contains trailing `}}` garbage \u2014 again from the unbalanced brace.\\n\\nNow fix the root cause: `extract_balanced` and `split_entries` in ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL must ignore braces that are inside JSON string literals.\\n\\nFix for `extract_balanced`: when scanning, skip over string literals \u2014 when a `\\\"` is encountered and not escaped, skip to the closing unescaped `\\\"`.\\n\\nFix for `split_entries`: same \u2014 track in_string and escaping.\\n\\nBut careful: existing tests in ltcl_json_util expect certain SUSPECT behaviors:\\n- balanced_unclosed: input `{\\\"o\\\":{\\\"x\\\":1` \u2192 returns tail `{\\\"x\\\":1`. My new implementation must preserve that: with string-awareness, `{\\\"o\\\":{\\\"x\\\":1` \u2014 scan from the `{` after `\\\"o\\\":`: depth 1, no close found, so lv_end = lv_len-1, returns tail. Keep that behavior.\\n- balanced_no_open_bracket: `{\\\"o\\\":\\\"txt\\\"}` \u2192 key 'o' found, no opening bracket in rest \u2192 ''. With string awareness: after the key match, lv_rest = `\\\":\\\"txt\\\"}`. The first `{` search \u2014 none, returns ''. Still fine. Wait \u2014 but there's a subtlety: the `{` search in lv_rest happens on the rest AFTER the key. In `{\\\"o\\\":{\\\"x\\\":1},...}`, lv_rest = `\\\":{\\\"x\\\":1}...`, first `{` is the one after the colon. Good.\\n\\n  However, one caveat with string awareness in finding the opening: if the value is a string like `\\\"{ hello }\\\"` \u2014 the first `{` inside a string would be wrongly taken as the opening. To be safe, the search for iv_open should also skip strings. Let me implement a helper scan that walks the string once, tracking whether inside a string literal.\\n\\nLet me write the new extract_balanced:\\n\\n```abap\\nMETHOD extract_balanced.\\n  DATA lv_len TYPE i.\\n  DATA lv_pos TYPE i.\\n  DATA lv_in_str TYPE abap_bool.\\n  lv_len = strlen( iv_json ).\\n  \\\" 1) find the key\\n  FIND FIRST OCCURRENCE OF PCRE |\\\"{ iv_key }\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*| IN iv_json MATCH OFFSET DATA(lv_key_off) MATCH LENGTH DATA(lv_key_len).\\n  ...\\n```\\n\\nHmm, careful with PCRE availability \u2014 the existing code uses PCRE in extract_str, so PCRE is available. But simpler: keep the original FIND of `\\\"key\\\"` then skip whitespace/colon manually with string awareness.\\n\\nImplementation plan:\\n\\n```abap\\nMETHOD extract_balanced.\\n  DATA(lv_len) = strlen( iv_json ).\\n  FIND '\\\"' \\u0026\\u0026 iv_key \\u0026\\u0026 '\\\"' IN iv_json MATCH OFFSET DATA(lv_off).\\n  IF sy-subrc \\u003c\\u003e 0. RETURN. ENDIF.\\n  \\\" walk from lv_off + key length, skipping strings, to the first iv_open at top level\\n  DATA lv_pos TYPE i.\\n  lv_pos = lv_off + strlen( iv_key ) + 2.\\n  DATA lv_start TYPE i VALUE -1.\\n  DATA lv_in_str TYPE abap_bool VALUE abap_false.\\n  WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len.\\n    DATA(lv_ch) = iv_json+lv_pos(1).\\n    IF lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n      IF lv_ch = `\\\\`.\\n        lv_pos = lv_pos + 2. \\\" skip escaped char\\n        CONTINUE.\\n      ELSEIF lv_ch = `\\\"`.\\n        lv_in_str = abap_false.\\n      ENDIF.\\n    ELSE.\\n      IF lv_ch = `\\\"`.\\n        lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n      ELSEIF lv_ch = iv_open.\\n        lv_start = lv_pos.\\n        EXIT.\\n      ENDIF.\\n    ENDIF.\\n    lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\n  ENDWHILE.\\n  IF lv_start \\u003c 0. RETURN. ENDIF.\\n  \\\" 2) balanced scan from lv_start, ignoring braces inside strings\\n  DATA lv_depth TYPE i VALUE 1.\\n  lv_pos = lv_start.\\n  lv_in_str = abap_false.\\n  WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len - 1 AND lv_depth \\u003e 0.\\n    lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\n    lv_ch = iv_json+lv_pos(1).\\n    IF lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n      IF lv_ch = `\\\\`.\\n        lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\n      ELSEIF lv_ch = `\\\"`.\\n        lv_in_str = abap_false.\\n      ENDIF.\\n    ELSE.\\n      IF lv_ch = `\\\"`.\\n        lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n      ELSEIF lv_ch = iv_open.\\n        lv_depth = lv_depth + 1.\\n      ELSEIF lv_ch = iv_close.\\n        lv_depth = lv_depth - 1.\\n      ENDIF.\\n    ENDIF.\\n  ENDWHILE.\\n  IF lv_depth \\u003e 0.\\n    lv_pos = lv_len - 1.\\n  ENDIF.\\n  DATA(lv_out_len) = lv_pos - lv_start + 1.\\n  IF lv_start + lv_out_len \\u003e lv_len.\\n    lv_out_len = lv_len - lv_start.\\n  ENDIF.\\n  IF lv_out_len \\u003e 0.\\n    rv_ = iv_json+lv_start(lv_out_len).\\n  ENDIF.\\nENDMETHOD.\\n```\\n\\nWait, careful about `lv_pos = lv_pos + 2` in the escaped-char skip inside the first loop when using CONTINUE with the WHILE: original increments at bottom; with `lv_pos = lv_pos + 2. CONTINUE.` fine.\\n\\nAlso careful: in the escaped skip branch when lv_pos+1 might be \\u003e= lv_len: `iv_json+lv_pos(1)` access of lv_pos+1 beyond length would be a substring access error. In ABAP, `iv_json+lv_pos(1)` where lv_pos \\u003c lv_len is safe. After `\\\\` at last position: we set lv_pos = lv_pos + 2 \u2192 loop ends. Fine since we only access iv_json+lv_pos(1) at loop top when lv_pos \\u003c lv_len.\\n\\nHmm, but there's a subtle issue with the original semantics: the original returns tail on unclosed input (`balanced_unclosed` test expects `{\\\"x\\\":1` tail). My version: depth stays 1, while ends at lv_len-1, then lv_depth \\u003e 0 \u2192 lv_pos = lv_len - 1, out_len = lv_len - lv_start \u2192 whole tail. Same. \\n\\nBut wait \u2014 there's a behavioral difference for `balanced_no_open_bracket` test: `{\\\"o\\\":\\\"txt\\\"}` with key 'o': lv_off = 1 (position of `\\\"o\\\"`), lv_pos starts at 1 + 1 + 2 = 4. The rest is `:\\\"txt\\\"}`. Scanning: `:` not open; `\\\"` \u2192 in_str = true; t,x,t; `\\\"` \u2192 in_str false; `}` not open; end. lv_start stays -1 \u2192 return ''. Test passes.\\n\\nHmm, but one important consideration: the original code took the FIRST `{` after the key match, even if the value was, e.g., a string containing `{`. My version skips strings \u2014 better.\\n\\nBut caution: in `parse_calls_responses`, `extract_balanced( iv_json = iv_body iv_key = 'output' ... )`. Fine.\\n\\nAnother subtle case: `extract_balanced` where key appears inside a string value earlier in the JSON. Pre-existing limitation, not our concern.\\n\\nNow also: what if the open char is found but then a `\\\"` appears \u2014 starting a string \u2014 handled.\\n\\nNow `split_entries` fix: it splits the array content by `{`...`}` at top level. It must ignore braces inside strings AND also should split on top-level objects; braces inside strings must not count toward depth. New implementation:\\n\\n```abap\\nMETHOD split_entries.\\n  DATA(lv_len) = strlen( iv_arr ).\\n  DATA lv_pos TYPE i VALUE 0.\\n  WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len.\\n    \\\" find next top-level '{' (skipping string literals)\\n    DATA lv_start TYPE i VALUE -1.\\n    DATA lv_in_str TYPE abap_bool VALUE abap_false.\\n    DATA lv_i TYPE i.\\n    lv_i = lv_pos.\\n    WHILE lv_i \\u003c lv_len.\\n      DATA(lv_ch) = iv_arr+lv_i(1).\\n      IF lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n        IF lv_ch = `\\\\`.\\n          lv_i = lv_i + 2.\\n          CONTINUE.\\n        ELSEIF lv_ch = `\\\"`.\\n          lv_in_str = abap_false.\\n        ENDIF.\\n      ELSE.\\n        IF lv_ch = `\\\"`.\\n          lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n        ELSEIF lv_ch = '{'.\\n          lv_start = lv_i.\\n          EXIT.\\n        ENDIF.\\n      ENDIF.\\n      lv_i = lv_i + 1.\\n    ENDWHILE.\\n    IF lv_start \\u003c 0. EXIT. ENDIF.\\n    \\\" balanced scan to the matching '}' at top level\\n    ...\\n```\\n\\nActually, I can restructure split_entries as a single pass state machine that records entry boundaries. That's cleaner:\\n\\n```abap\\nMETHOD split_entries.\\n  DATA(lv_len) = strlen( iv_arr ).\\n  DATA lv_pos TYPE i.\\n  DATA lv_start TYPE i VALUE -1.\\n  DATA lv_depth TYPE i VALUE 0.\\n  DATA lv_in_str TYPE abap_bool VALUE abap_false.\\n  WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len.\\n    DATA(lv_ch) = iv_arr+lv_pos(1).\\n    IF lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n      IF lv_ch = `\\\\`.\\n        lv_pos = lv_pos + 2.\\n        CONTINUE.\\n      ELSEIF lv_ch = `\\\"`.\\n        lv_in_str = abap_false.\\n      ENDIF.\\n    ELSE.\\n      IF lv_ch = `\\\"`.\\n        lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n      ELSEIF lv_ch = '{'.\\n        IF lv_depth = 0.\\n          lv_start = lv_pos.\\n        ENDIF.\\n        lv_depth = lv_depth + 1.\\n      ELSEIF lv_ch = '}'.\\n        IF lv_depth \\u003e 0.\\n          lv_depth = lv_depth - 1.\\n          IF lv_depth = 0 AND lv_start \\u003e= 0.\\n            APPEND iv_arr+lv_start(lv_pos - lv_start + 1) TO rt_.\\n            lv_start = -1.\\n          ENDIF.\\n        ENDIF.\\n      ENDIF.\\n    ENDIF.\\n    lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\n  ENDWHILE.\\n  \\\" unclosed last entry: keep old defensive behavior -\\u003e append the tail\\n  IF lv_depth \\u003e 0 AND lv_start \\u003e= 0.\\n    APPEND iv_arr+lv_start(lv_len - lv_start) TO rt_.\\n  ENDIF.\\nENDMETHOD.\\n```\\n\\nHmm \u2014 but the old defensive behavior for unclosed entry appended the tail from lv_start to end. Keep that for compatibility.\\n\\nOne concern: in ABAP, declaring `DATA lv_start TYPE i VALUE -1.` \u2014 VALUE with a literal constant -1 is fine (constant). The original code even had a comment \\\"VALUE takes only a constant on SAP\\\" \u2014 -1 is a constant literal, OK.\\n\\nAlso `DATA(lv_ch)` inline inside a WHILE loop: declared once per method in ABAP (inline declarations are method-scoped), fine.\\n\\nNow \u2014 will these changes break existing tests in ltcl_json_util? Let's check the split_entries users: flatten_tools, build_input_items, parse loops. There are no direct split_entries tests in ltcl_json_util. extract_balanced tests: balanced_object, balanced_nested_and_array, balanced_missing_key, balanced_no_open_bracket, balanced_unclosed \u2014 all preserved by my analysis.\\n\\nOne more important consideration: `escaped \\\\` skip `lv_pos = lv_pos + 2` \u2014 in escape branch. In JSON, `\\\\\\\\` is an escaped backslash, `\\\\\\\"` escaped quote \u2014 skipping 2 is right.\\n\\nBut hmm: in the arguments value, `\\\\\\\\n` (escaped backslash + n)? e.g. ABAP source containing backslash. Fine.\\n\\nNow also \u2014 is there an issue with extract_str for the responses case? After the fix, entry 1 is `{\\\"id\\\":\\\"a\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\n    lv_open = '{'.\\\\n  }\\\"}`. extract_str( 'call_id' ) \u2192 c1. extract_str( 'name' ) \u2192 hmm! \\\"name\\\" appears in arguments? arguments contains `\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\"`... in my responses test lv_a1 contains only ABAP source without `\\\"name\\\":`. But in real traffic, arguments contain {\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":...}. extract_str finds FIRST occurrence of `\\\"name\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"...\\\"`. In the entry, the order is id, type, status, call_id, name, arguments \u2014 so \\\"name\\\" of the entry comes before arguments. First occurrence = the real name. OK, matches how it currently works in production (the escape test shows extract_str returns raw escaped value, and then parse_calls_responses calls unescape on extract_balanced result, not extract_str... wait it calls unescape(extract_balanced(arguments)) for responses \u2014 since arguments is a string containing escaped JSON. Fine.)\\n\\nNow write the fix in zcl_pia_00_json_util. I'll use write_method twice: once for extract_balanced, once for split_entries.\\n\\nCareful about ABAP syntax details:\\n- `FIND ... MATCH OFFSET DATA(lv_off)` \u2014 inline declaration in FIND statement is allowed in newer ABAP; original code used `FIND lv_pat IN iv_json MATCH OFFSET lv_off.` with predeclared `DATA lv_off TYPE i.`. Keep that style to be safe.\\n\\nLet me also double check the first-loop key offset: `FIND lv_pat IN iv_json MATCH OFFSET lv_off.` (first occurrence). lv_pat = `\\\"output\\\"`. Then start scanning at lv_off + strlen( iv_key ) + 2. Note: FIND ... IN ... MATCH OFFSET \u2014 does it find first occurrence? Yes, default is first occurrence.\\n\\nEdge: the original then did `lv_rest = iv_json+lv_off.` and searched for open in lv_rest, returning substring of lv_rest. My version works with absolute offsets into iv_json \u2014 equivalent.\\n\\nAnother subtlety: the `WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len - 1 AND lv_depth \\u003e 0` \u2014 same as original (original: `WHILE lv_pos \\u003c strlen( lv_rest ) - 1`). I compute lv_len once.\\n\\nAlso, for the open-scan loop: what if there's whitespace/colon before the open? Handled since we just scan for the open char ignoring strings.\\n\\nNow, method signature stays the same. Let me write the two methods.\\n\\nAlso, must the escaped-char skip worry about `\\\\` as the very last char? `lv_pos = lv_pos + 2` then loop condition checks \u2014 safe.\\n\\nLet me write the code carefully:\\n\\n```abap\\nMETHOD extract_balanced.\\n  \\\" braces/brackets inside JSON string literals must not count toward the depth:\\n  \\\" an escaped source like \\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\" would otherwise break the balance\\n  DATA lv_len TYPE i.\\n  DATA lv_pos TYPE i.\\n  DATA lv_start TYPE i.\\n  DATA lv_depth TYPE i.\\n  DATA lv_ch TYPE string.\\n  DATA lv_in_str TYPE abap_bool VALUE abap_false.\\n  lv_len = strlen( iv_json ).\\n\\n  FIND '\\\"' \\u0026\\u0026 iv_key \\u0026\\u0026 '\\\"' IN iv_json MATCH OFFSET DATA(lv_off).\\n  IF sy-subrc \\u003c\\u003e 0. RETURN. ENDIF.\\n  lv_pos = lv_off + strlen( iv_key ) + 2.\\n\\n  \\\" first opening bracket at top level (not inside a string literal)\\n  lv_start = -1.\\n  WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len.\\n    lv_ch = iv_json+lv_pos(1).\\n    IF lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n      IF lv_ch = `\\\\`.\\n        lv_pos = lv_pos + 2.\\n        CONTINUE.\\n      ELSEIF lv_ch = `\\\"`.\\n        lv_in_str = abap_false.\\n      ENDIF.\\n    ELSE.\\n      IF lv_ch = `\\\"`.\\n        lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n      ELSEIF lv_ch = iv_open.\\n        lv_start = lv_pos.\\n        EXIT.\\n      ENDIF.\\n    ENDIF.\\n    lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\n  ENDWHILE.\\n  IF lv_start \\u003c 0. RETURN. ENDIF.\\n\\n  \\\" balanced scan to the matching closing bracket\\n  lv_depth = 1.\\n  lv_pos = lv_start.\\n  lv_in_str = abap_false.\\n  WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len - 1 AND lv_depth \\u003e 0.\\n    lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\n    lv_ch = iv_json+lv_pos(1).\\n    IF lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n      IF lv_ch = `\\\\`.\\n        lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\n      ELSEIF lv_ch = `\\\"`.\\n        lv_in_str = abap_false.\\n      ENDIF.\\n    ELSE.\\n      IF lv_ch = `\\\"`.\\n        lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n      ELSEIF lv_ch = iv_open.\\n        lv_depth = lv_depth + 1.\\n      ELSEIF lv_ch = iv_close.\\n        lv_depth = lv_depth - 1.\\n      ENDIF.\\n    ENDIF.\\n  ENDWHILE.\\n  IF lv_depth \\u003e 0.\\n    lv_pos = lv_len - 1. \\\" unclosed: return the tail (previous behavior)\\n  ENDIF.\\n  DATA(lv_out) = lv_pos - lv_start + 1.\\n  IF lv_start + lv_out \\u003e lv_len.\\n    lv_out = lv_len - lv_start.\\n  ENDIF.\\n  IF lv_out \\u003e 0.\\n    rv_ = iv_json+lv_start(lv_out).\\n  ENDIF.\\nENDMETHOD.\\n```\\n\\nWait: `FIND ... MATCH OFFSET DATA(lv_off).` \u2014 inline DATA in FIND is supported (ABAP 7.02+? MATCH OFFSET with inline declaration requires 7.40?). To be safe, declare `DATA lv_off TYPE i.` at top like the original. The original had `DATA lv_off TYPE i.` \u2014 yes, do that.\\n\\nAlso note: `WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len - 1 AND lv_depth \\u003e 0` \u2014 when lv_len is 0... `iv_json` empty \u2192 key not found \u2192 return. OK.\\n\\nEdge: in the balanced scan, when `\\\\` at position lv_len-1: `lv_pos = lv_pos + 1` inside \u2192 may make lv_pos = lv_len \u2192 while condition lv_pos \\u003c lv_len - 1 false \u2192 exit. depth\\u003e0 \u2192 lv_pos = lv_len - 1 \u2192 tail. Fine.\\n\\nNow the second escaped-skip in the balanced loop uses `lv_pos = lv_pos + 1` (because the loop itself increments at top). In the first loop I used `lv_pos = lv_pos + 2` with CONTINUE because increment is at bottom. Let me double-check first loop: positions: `\\\\` at p, escaped char at p+1. Set lv_pos = p + 2, CONTINUE \u2192 while condition checks p+2 \\u003c lv_len. Right.\\n\\nBut hold on \u2014 in the first loop with CONTINUE, ABAP WHILE loops don't auto-increment, correct.\\n\\nFor split_entries single-pass version:\\n\\n```abap\\nMETHOD split_entries.\\n  \\\" top-level {...} entries of a JSON array string; braces inside string\\n  \\\" literals (escaped source code!) do not count toward the depth\\n  DATA lv_len TYPE i.\\n  DATA lv_pos TYPE i.\\n  DATA lv_start TYPE i VALUE -1.\\n  DATA lv_depth TYPE i VALUE 0.\\n  DATA lv_ch TYPE string.\\n  DATA lv_in_str TYPE abap_bool VALUE abap_false.\\n  lv_len = strlen( iv_arr ).\\n  WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len.\\n    lv_ch = iv_arr+lv_pos(1).\\n    IF lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n      IF lv_ch = `\\\\`.\\n        lv_pos = lv_pos + 2.\\n        CONTINUE.\\n      ELSEIF lv_ch = `\\\"`.\\n        lv_in_str = abap_false.\\n      ENDIF.\\n    ELSE.\\n      IF lv_ch = `\\\"`.\\n        lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n      ELSEIF lv_ch = '{'.\\n        IF lv_depth = 0.\\n          lv_start = lv_pos.\\n        ENDIF.\\n        lv_depth = lv_depth + 1.\\n      ELSEIF lv_ch = '}'.\\n        IF lv_depth \\u003e 0.\\n          lv_depth = lv_depth - 1.\\n          IF lv_depth = 0 AND lv_start \\u003e= 0.\\n            DATA(lv_n) = lv_pos - lv_start + 1.\\n            APPEND iv_arr+lv_start(lv_n) TO rt_.\\n            lv_start = -1.\\n          ENDIF.\\n        ENDIF.\\n      ENDIF.\\n    ENDIF.\\n    lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\n  ENDWHILE.\\n  IF lv_depth \\u003e 0 AND lv_start \\u003e= 0.\\n    DATA(lv_tail) = lv_len - lv_start.  \\\" unclosed entry: keep the defensive tail\\n    APPEND iv_arr+lv_start(lv_tail) TO rt_.\\n  ENDIF.\\nENDMETHOD.\\n```\\n\\n`DATA lv_start TYPE i VALUE -1.` \u2014 VALUE with constant -1 allowed (the SAP restriction mentioned in a comment was about variables). OK.\\n\\nAlso note `DATA(lv_n)` inside loop \u2014 inline declaration inside a loop: allowed only once (compiler treats as declaration at first pass; ABAP allows inline declarations in loops? Yes \u2014 inline DATA in loops is permitted; it's initialized each pass? Actually inline DATA in a loop is allowed and retains value across passes. Fine.)\\n\\nHmm, one thing: `APPEND iv_arr+lv_start(lv_n) TO rt_.` \u2014 substring access with variables lengths: allowed with offset/length in parentheses where both are variables? `iv_arr+lv_start(lv_n)` \u2014 offset and length can be variables in modern ABAP. The original code used exactly that pattern: `APPEND iv_arr+lv_start(lv_len) TO rt_.` Yes.\\n\\nNow: will the executor or anything else depend on old buggy behavior? No.\\n\\nAlso there might be other consumers of extract_balanced with keys whose values contain braces in strings \u2014 this only improves.\\n\\nAdditionally, should I check `zcl_pia_00_llm_http=\\u003ezif_pia_00_llm~chat` responses answer extraction uses extract_balanced on output again \u2014 fine.\\n\\nNow let me also think: with the fix, does the responses case now parse both calls? Entry 1 = correct object with escaped arguments. extract_balanced('arguments') on entry: key found at `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"`, first `{` at top level skipping strings: the value is a string `\\\"{\\\\n    lv_open = '{'.\\\\n  }\\\"` \u2014 WAIT. The arguments value is a STRING starting with `{`. My open-scan skips string literals: scanning entry: `\\\"id\\\":\\\"a\\\"`... `\\\"arguments\\\"` then `:` then `\\\"` \u2192 in_str = true; the `{` inside the string is skipped; `\\\"` at end \u2192 in_str=false; then `}` ... no `{` found at top level \u2192 lv_start = -1 \u2192 return ''!! That breaks parsing completely!\\n\\nHmm. This is important. The original code searched for the first `{` after the key regardless of strings \u2014 for the responses format, arguments is a string-encoded JSON object, so the first `{` after `\\\"arguments\\\"` IS inside the string, and that's what we want to extract (the balanced content within the string value), then unescape it.\\n\\nSo skipping strings in the open-search would break the responses arguments extraction. I need: open-search NOT string-aware (first `{` after key), but the balanced scan string-aware. But then in the balanced scan starting inside the string `{\\\\n lv_open = '{'.\\\\n }` \u2014 the scan starts at the `{` that begins the arguments JSON; but the string-content braces: the scan would treat the `'` chars \u2014 no `\\\"` inside the arguments value until... the arguments string value itself is escaped, so inner quotes are `\\\\\\\"`. E.g. arguments value: `\\\"{\\\\n    lv_open = '{'.\\\\n  }\\\"`. After the opening `{` (first char of the string content), scanning: `{`? none until end `}`. Wait the content is `{`, `\\\\n` (2 chars backslash-n), spaces, `lv_open = '{'.`, `\\\\n`, spaces, `}`. The balanced scan from the first `{`: encounters `\\\\` (of `\\\\n`) \u2192 my string-aware scanner would treat it as escape-skip only when in_str... but we're not in_str here (we started outside a string). Hmm \u2014 `\\\\` outside a string: my scanner ignores it (no branch). Then `'` chars ignored. Then `}` \u2192 depth 0 \u2192 end. The result: `{\\\\n    lv_open = '{'.\\\\n  }` \u2014 correct!\\n\\nBut wait \u2014 what if the escaped content contains `\\\\\\\"` sequences (like a source with quotes: `WRITE 'hello \\\"x\\\"'.`)? Then within the arguments string value, `\\\\\\\"` appears. My scanner, starting outside a string (since it begins at the `{` inside the string value, in_str=false), would see `\\\\` \u2192 not handled outside in_str \u2192 then `\\\"` \u2192 in_str = true! Then it would skip subsequent braces until the next unescaped `\\\"`. That could break balance counting for content with quotes and braces.\\n\\nHmm. Example arguments escaped: `{\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_x = '{'. \\\\\\\"q\\\\\\\"\\\"}`... Let's think: value string: `{\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}\\\"`. Wait let me construct: source ABAP = `    lv_open = '{'.` and also a comment `\\\"`. Ugh.\\n\\nActually more common: arguments = `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}`. As escaped string in JSON: `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"method\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"M1\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\\\\\"}\\\"`. \\n\\nOriginal behavior: extract_balanced from first `{`: scan for `{`/`}` balance ignoring strings: content = `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":...}` \u2014 chars: `{` depth1, `\\\\` ignored, `\\\"` \u2014 if string-aware, enters string mode; `\\\\\\\"` \u2014 inside string mode, `\\\\` escape skip... hmm let's trace: after `{`, next chars: `\\\\`, `\\\"`, `n`, `a`, `m`, `e`, `\\\\`, `\\\"`, `:`, ... \\n\\nWith my string-aware scanner (starting in_str=false at the `{`): sees `\\\\` \u2192 ignore (outside string, no branch handles it), then `\\\"` \u2192 in_str = true. Then `n a m e` inside string... then `\\\\` \u2192 in_str escape: skip next char (`\\\"`) \u2192 pos+1 extra. Then `:` ... wait we're still in_str. Then `\\\\` skip `\\\"`. Then... hmm after `\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"` we have `:` `Z`... it stays in string mode until an unescaped `\\\"` \u2014 which never comes until the very end `\\\\\\\"}\\\"` \u2014 the final `\\\\\\\"` is escaped-skip, then `}` (in string), then `\\\"` ends string. So the whole content was treated as one string and the final `}` before the closing `\\\"` closed... no wait, the `}` inside the string doesn't count. Result: the scan would run to the end and the tail would be returned. That breaks extraction of arguments for the common case with escaped quotes!\\n\\nSo a naive string-aware balanced scan fails for escaped-string-encoded JSON (the responses format), because the content itself contains `\\\\\\\"` everywhere and the outer quoting context is unknown to the scanner.\\n\\nThe original (string-unaware) scanner works for the responses arguments because escaped quotes `\\\\\\\"` never produce a bare `\\\"` ... hmm wait, the original scanner counts `{` and `}` only; quotes are ignored entirely. For the responses arguments `{\\\"name\\\":...,\\\"source\\\":\\\"... '{' ...\\\"}`: the `{` in `'{'.` increments depth wrongly \u2014 THAT's the original bug. So original: depth = 1 (outer) + 1 (inner '{' in string) = 2; the `}` closing arguments reduces to 1; then continues to `,\\\"status\\\"...`... then entry's closing `}` \u2192 0 \u2192 returns arguments + `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\"...}`. Matches the reported symptom.\\n\\nSo what's the right approach for a scanner over escaped-string content? The proper way: when scanning inside a string-encoded JSON, treat `\\\\\\\"` as a quote char (not string delimiter). The rule: a `\\\"` preceded by an odd number of backslashes is not a delimiter.\\n\\nAlternative simpler approach: for the balanced scan, be string-aware but with the twist that an escaped quote `\\\\\\\"` toggles... no.\\n\\nCleaner: change the approach at the parser level rather than making one universal scanner:\\n\\nOption A: In parse_calls_responses, for `arguments`, extract the string value via extract_str (which correctly handles `((?:[^\\\"\\\\\\\\]|\\\\\\\\.)*)` \u2014 escaped-aware!) and then unescape it. That's much more robust: extract_str with regex handles escaped quotes properly and returns the raw string content; unescape converts to real JSON text.\\n\\nBut careful: extract_str finds the FIRST `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"...\\\"` in the entry \u2014 good. The regex `((?:[^\\\"\\\\\\\\]|\\\\\\\\.)*)` correctly stops at the unescaped closing quote. This solves the responses case entirely without touching extract_balanced.\\n\\nFor parse_calls_chat: arguments is a real nested object (not a string): `extract_balanced( iv_key = 'arguments' iv_open = '{' iv_close = '}' )`. Here a string-aware scanner is correct (real JSON object). The bug there: braces inside string values of the arguments object, e.g. `\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"` \u2192 the `'{'` inside the string value breaks balance. A string-aware scanner on real JSON works: `\\\"source\\\":\\\"...\\\"` \u2014 the `\\\"` delimiters are real. Trace with my scanner: start at `{` of arguments (in_str=false). `\\\"name\\\"` \u2192 in_str toggles on/off properly (no escapes inside). `:` etc. `\\\"source\\\"` toggles. `:` `\\\"` \u2192 in_str=true \u2192 content `    lv_open = '{'.` \u2014 `{` skipped since in_str \u2192 `\\\"` \u2192 in_str=false. `}` \u2192 depth 0. \\n\\nAnd for the chat-format arguments the open-search: first `{` after `\\\"arguments\\\"` key \u2014 in chat JSON, `\\\"arguments\\\":{...}` \u2014 the first `{` is the object. Fine, no string-skipping needed for open-search. But consider responses: `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"...\\\\\\\"}\\\"` \u2014 first `{` after key is inside the string \u2014 that's what we want (if we were to use extract_balanced). So keeping open-search non-string-aware preserves the responses usage.\\n\\nSo the minimal correct fix:\\n1. split_entries: string-aware (real JSON array of objects) \u2014 fixes losing the second call in BOTH formats. In the responses format, the entries array contains real objects; braces inside string values must not count. \u2713\\n2. parse_calls_responses: use extract_str + unescape for arguments instead of extract_balanced+unescape. \u2713\\n3. extract_balanced: make the balanced scan string-aware BUT that would break responses-style usage... but after fix 2, extract_balanced's remaining uses for `arguments` is only chat (real object). Other uses: 'tool_calls' array with `[`/`]`, 'output' array, 'function' object in flatten_tools, 'text'/'arguments' in build_input_items (extract_str)... Let me check build_input_items: `extract_str( iv_json = lv_e iv_name = 'arguments' )` \u2014 chat entries have arguments as object; extract_str on `\\\"arguments\\\":{\\\"name\\\":...}` \u2014 regex `\\\"arguments\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"((?:[^\\\"\\\\\\\\]|\\\\\\\\.)*)\\\"` requires a `\\\"` right after colon (with optional whitespace) \u2014 `\\\"name\\\"` follows, so it matches `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"` then `name\\\\\\\"...` hmm: `{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",...,\\\"arguments\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\"...}}` \u2014 regex looks for `\\\"arguments\\\"` then `:` then optional ws then `\\\"`. After `\\\"arguments\\\":` comes `{` \u2014 no match at that position? The regex engine will try: `\\\"arguments\\\"` matched, `\\\\s*:\\\\s*` matches `:`, then needs `\\\"` but finds `{` \u2192 fail \u2192 backtrack, search other positions for `\\\"arguments\\\"`... only one. So no match \u2192 empty. Hmm, so build_input_items extracts arguments from chat-format content via extract_str \u2014 that yields empty?? Wait, in build_input_items the source is ls-content which is the raw assistant content stored as `{\\\"tool_calls\\\":[...]}` \u2014 created in ev_assistant_raw as `{\\\"id\\\":..,\\\"name\\\":..,\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"\\u003cescaped\\u003e\\\"}` \u2014 a STRING. So extract_str works there. OK.\\n\\nSo making extract_balanced string-aware: does any current usage pass escaped-string content? parse_calls_responses arguments (to be replaced by extract_str), parse_calls_chat arguments (real object \u2713), output/tool_calls arrays (real \u2713), flatten_tools 'function' (real \u2713). Also executor/session code? Let me grep other classes for extract_balanced usage later. But since only json_util defines it, other classes may use it. Let me check the other classes quickly (executor, session, tools). I should search for usages to avoid breaking behavior. Let me read zcl_pia_00_executor and maybe others.\\n\\nActually, a safer approach for extract_balanced: keep it string-aware in the balanced scan (correct for real JSON), and in parse_calls_responses switch to extract_str+unescape. Then also double-check other callers.\\n\\nThere's a subtlety with string-aware balanced scan and escaped strings (responses arguments) \u2014 no longer used there. But wait \u2014 what about `extract_balanced( iv_json = lv_resp iv_key = 'output' iv_open = '[' iv_close = ']' )` in the answer extraction of responses: entries contain string-encoded arguments with `\\\\\\\"` \u2014 scanning with string-awareness: `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...\\\"` \u2014 `\\\"` starts string; inside, `\\\\` escape-skips `\\\"`; ... `\\\"` at end closes. The braces inside are properly skipped. But here's the catch I identified: within the string, sequences like `\\\\\\\"` are handled; but what about `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"` (escaped backslash then quote)? `\\\\` skips next char (`\\\\`), then `\\\"` closes string \u2014 correct! Because `\\\\\\\\` is an escaped backslash and the following `\\\"` is a real delimiter. My skip-2 handles that. What about `\\\\\\\\\\\"` (in escaped JSON content, a literal backslash at end of source line is encoded `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\`, and quote `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`)... generic rule: in_str + `\\\\` \u2192 skip exactly 2 chars. That's the standard correct JSON string scanner. \u2713 The problematic case earlier was scanning escaped-string CONTENT as if top-level (starting mid-string), which we no longer do.\\n\\nSo plan:\\n1. json_util: make extract_balanced balanced-scan string-aware (open-search: keep first-occurrence semantics, not string-aware \u2014 needed for chat arguments? no wait, chat arguments has `{` directly. For responses format... after fix, nobody extracts string-content braces via extract_balanced. Hmm, but keep open-search non-string-aware = current behavior, less risk).\\n\\nHmm wait, actually there's a subtle issue with open-search not string-aware for extract_balanced with key 'arguments' in the chat format: `\\\"arguments\\\":{` \u2192 first `{` is right. But what if an earlier field's string value contains a `{` before... e.g. entry `{\\\"id\\\":\\\"a{b\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":{...}}` \u2014 the open-search finds the `{` inside `\\\"a{b\\\"` string \u2192 wrong start. Edge case; pre-existing. With string-aware open-search, chat arguments still found correctly (the `{` after the colon). But responses-format usage (if any remained) would break. Since I'm replacing the responses usage with extract_str, making open-search string-aware is strictly better for the remaining real-JSON usages... but wait: open-search string-aware starting from after the key: in chat entry, after `\\\"arguments\\\"` comes `:{` \u2014 no strings between \u2192 fine.\\n\\nBut hold on \u2014 extract_balanced is also used with the FULL body for 'output'/'tool_calls' \u2014 string-aware open-search: scanning from after `\\\"output\\\"`: `:[` \u2192 fine.\\n\\nHmm, but there's one more usage pattern I should verify \u2014 session/executor. Let me grep. I'll read the other classes to check for extract_balanced usage. Let me do that now.\\n\\nAlso 2. parse_calls_responses: replace extract_balanced+unescape with unescape(extract_str(...)).\\n\\n3. split_entries: string-aware.\\n\\nThen tests: my chat test expects arguments to include the full object `{...}` including the source with `'{'`. With fix: entry 1 of tool_calls array = `{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}}}` (split_entries string-aware \u2192 correct entry boundaries \u2713). extract_balanced('arguments') with string-aware scan \u2192 `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}` \u2713. unescape of that: no backslash sequences \u2192 unchanged \u2713. My expected value in the test: |{\\\\n    \\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\\n    \\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"\\\\n  }| \u2014 hmm, that has pretty-printed JSON with real newlines?! I wrote the chat body inline (not pretty-printed) but the expected with \\\\n... That test expectation is wrong \u2014 the actual arguments string from the inline body would be `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}` (single line, no newlines). I must fix the expectation to match the inline body. Wait \u2014 but the test is currently RED for the right reason? The chat test failed on arguments with actual `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}}` vs my expected multi-line \u2014 my expected was wrong (I copied from the red-run message: expected shown as `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}`... hmm the failure message showed expected as single-line `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}` and actual with trailing `}}`. Interesting \u2014 so the expected in the run was already the single-line version? The failure output shows: Expected [{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}] \u2014 yes single line. But the current file on disk (which I read after) shows expected with |{\\\\n ... | multi-line. So the file I last wrote (3580 chars) has the multi-line expectation. The red run used an earlier version. Either way, I need to update the chat test expectation to the single-line value: `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}`.\\n\\nHmm wait, but actually let me reconsider: maybe keep the pretty-printed form to be more realistic? The chat arguments in real traffic from glm: `\\\"arguments\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"...\\\"}` typically compact. I'll keep the body as-is and fix the expectation.\\n\\nAlso for the responses test: arguments expected lv_a1 = multi-line with REAL newlines? I built lv_a1 with `\\\\n` as literal backslash-n two-char sequences (since I used backquotes: `\\\\n` in backticks is backslash+n, not newline). Then escape( lv_a1 ) converts real newlines... wait lv_a1 contains literal `\\\\n` sequences (2 chars: backslash, n), not newlines. escape() leaves backslash \u2192 `\\\\\\\\` and `n` stays. So the JSON body has `\\\\\\\\n` (escaped backslash + n). Then parsing: extract_str returns raw `{\\\\n    lv_open = '{'.\\\\n  }`... hold on: the raw string value in JSON after escape() is `{\\\\\\\\n    lv_open = '{'.\\\\\\\\n  }`. extract_str returns `{\\\\\\\\n    lv_open = '{'.\\\\\\\\n  }`, then unescape \u2192 `{\\\\n...` \u2192 lv_a1 back (with literal backslash-n). And my expected is lv_a1 \u2192 \u2713. Good, that's consistent. And that matches real PIA behavior (session files contain \\\\n escaped sequences).\\n\\nWait, one problem: in the responses test the escaped arguments also contains `'{'` \u2192 with extract_str-based extraction, fine. \u2713 And split_entries on the output array must ignore the `{` inside the arguments string \u2192 string-aware \u2713.\\n\\nLet me also double check the responses test's second assertion about `status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"` \u2014 entry includes `\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"` \u2014 extract_str('call_id') \u2192 c1 \u2713.\\n\\nNow, additionally, should I also make extract_str handle the case where `\\\"arguments\\\"` key appears inside... fine.\\n\\nAlso `flatten_tools` uses split_entries + extract_balanced('function') \u2014 string-aware improves.\\n\\nOne more potential consumer: zcl_pia_00_executor, session store, terminal... Let me check quickly for extract_balanced/split_entries usage in other classes to make sure string-awareness doesn't break them. I'll outline/read a few.\\n\\nPlan of writes:\\n1. write_method extract_balanced on ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL.\\n2. write_method split_entries on ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL.\\n3. write_method parse_calls_responses on ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (use extract_str + unescape).\\n4. Fix chat test expectation.\\n5. Activate both classes, run tests for both classes.\\n\\nAlso maybe add unit tests in ltcl_json_util for split_entries with brace-in-string? Not strictly required; the llm_http tests cover it. But adding one focused test in json_util would be good practice. The task says: write an ABAP Unit test that reproduces it and is red, then fix and show green. My two red tests in llm_http cover both parsers. That's sufficient. I could add split_entries tests to json_util testclasses too \u2014 but modifying that test include risks touching the already-red ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR (must leave as is \u2014 I can add tests without touching it, but the run would show that failure; the task says leave it red, fine). I'll keep it simple and not touch json_util tests.\\n\\nWait \u2014 but first, let me check other users of extract_balanced/split_entries to avoid regressions. Let me outline executor and check. Actually let me just read the executor class and search. Classes: ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR, ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION, ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION_STORE, ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY, ZCL_PIA_15_T_*, ZCL_PIA_20_B_*, ZCL_PIA_30_*. Reading all is heavy; the risk is low since string-awareness only changes behavior for JSON containing braces/quotes inside strings (previously mis-parsed anyway). extract_str unchanged. I'll do a quick check of executor only.\\n\\nLet me now perform the writes. For write_method I need the exact method names.\\n\\nFor parse_calls_responses new source:\\n\\n```abap\\n  METHOD parse_calls_responses.\\n    DATA(lv_arr) = zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eextract_balanced(\\n      iv_json = iv_body iv_key = 'output' iv_open = '[' iv_close = ']' ).\\n    LOOP AT zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003esplit_entries( lv_arr ) INTO DATA(lv_e).\\n      IF lv_e NS '\\\"function_call\\\"' AND lv_e NS '\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\"'.\\n        CONTINUE.\\n      ENDIF.\\n      DATA ls TYPE zif_pia_00_tool=\\u003ets_call.\\n      ls-id = zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eextract_str( iv_json = lv_e iv_name = 'call_id' ).\\n      ls-name = zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eextract_str( iv_json = lv_e iv_name = 'name' ).\\n      \\\" arguments is a STRING containing JSON: take it with the escaped-aware regex\\n      \\\" and unescape it (extract_balanced would run past the closing quote whenever\\n      \\\" the source contains an unbalanced brace such as lv_open = '{'.)\\n      ls-arguments = zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eunescape(\\n        zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eextract_str( iv_json = lv_e iv_name = 'arguments' ) ).\\n      IF ls-name IS NOT INITIAL.\\n        APPEND ls TO rt_.\\n      ENDIF.\\n    ENDLOOP.\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\n```\\n\\nWait: careful \u2014 extract_str on entry for 'name': entry is `{\\\"id\\\":\\\"a\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"...\\\"}`. `\\\"name\\\"` first occurrence \u2014 could `\\\"name\\\"` appear inside arguments before the entry's own name field? arguments comes after name in typical order. But in the chat-format... this is responses. In responses output items, order can vary; if arguments precedes name and contains `\\\"name\\\":`, extract_str would grab the wrong one (raw escaped `\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...`). Hmm \u2014 extract_str returns raw (escaped) content: `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\"...` \u2192 unescaped would be wrong. But note the OLD code had the same ordering issue for extract_str('name') \u2014 it used extract_str for name already. So no regression. Actually wait, old code used extract_str for name and extract_balanced for arguments. With extract_str for arguments there's a new subtlety: if `\\\"arguments\\\"` appears... only once per entry. Fine.\\n\\nAnother subtlety: extract_str regex `\\\"arguments\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"((?:[^\\\"\\\\\\\\]|\\\\\\\\.)*)\\\"` \u2014 for the responses entry the value is a string \u2713. \\n\\nEdge: What if arguments value is a real object (some providers)? Then extract_str fails \u2192 empty. Old code handled objects. To be safe, fall back: if extract_str is initial, try extract_balanced. I'll add that fallback:\\n\\n```abap\\n      ls-arguments = zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eunescape(\\n        zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eextract_str( iv_json = lv_e iv_name = 'arguments' ) ).\\n      IF ls-arguments IS INITIAL.\\n        ls-arguments = zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eunescape( zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eextract_balanced(\\n                         iv_json = lv_e iv_key = 'arguments' iv_open = '{' iv_close = '}' ) ).\\n      ENDIF.\\n```\\n\\nOK. Now the chat fix is purely in json_util (extract_balanced string-aware + split_entries string-aware).\\n\\nHmm, wait \u2014 one more check on chat: `extract_balanced( iv_key = 'arguments' )` with string-aware open-search. Entry: `{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":{...}}}`. Open-search after `\\\"arguments\\\"`: `:{` \u2192 found. But with string-aware open-search... I decided open-search stays non-string-aware (first occurrence) to preserve any string-content usage. For chat that's the `{` right after colon \u2713.\\n\\nHmm, but hold on: should the open-search be string-aware for the balanced scan to be consistent? Consider extract_balanced('tool_calls') on chat body: body = `{\\\"choices\\\":[{\\\"message\\\":{...,\\\"tool_calls\\\":[...]}}]}`. FIND `\\\"tool_calls\\\"` \u2192 first occurrence. lv_rest after: `:[...]`. First `[` \u2192 ok.\\n\\nNow `FIND '\\\"' \\u0026\\u0026 iv_key \\u0026\\u0026 '\\\"' IN iv_json MATCH OFFSET lv_off.` \u2014 plain FIND (not regex), fine.\\n\\nNow, is there any caller that relies on extract_balanced finding an opening bracket INSIDE a string? That was only the responses arguments case. parse_calls_responses now uses extract_str first. Any other? Let me check other classes before finalizing. I'll read the executor to be safe.\\n\\nLet me now also double check my string-aware split_entries against the chat body test: tool_calls array content:\\n`{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}}},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",...}}}`\\n\\nScan: `{` depth1 start0; `\\\"id\\\"` string toggles; ... `\\\"source\\\"` string: content `    lv_open = '{'.` \u2014 the `{` is in_str \u2192 skipped \u2713; `\\\"` closes; `}` depth0 \u2192 entry 1 appended \u2713; then `,`; `{` \u2192 entry 2 start... `}` closes \u2713. Two entries \u2713.\\n\\nAnd extract_balanced on entry 1 with key 'arguments': open-search finds first `{` after `\\\"arguments\\\"` = the arguments object start. Balanced scan string-aware: `\\\"name\\\"` etc. toggles; source string `{` skipped; final `}` closes \u2713. Returns `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}` \u2713. unescape: contains no `\\\\` \u2192 unchanged \u2713.\\n\\nResponses flow with fixes: extract_balanced('output') string-aware on body: the `'{'.` inside arguments string skipped \u2713 \u2192 array `[...]`. split_entries \u2192 2 entries \u2713. extract_str('arguments') \u2192 `{\\\\n    lv_open = '{'.\\\\n  }` raw (with `\\\\\\\\n`? wait)...\\n\\nHold on, recheck the responses test data: lv_a1 = `{` \\u0026\\u0026 `\\\\n` \\u0026\\u0026 `    lv_open = '{'.` \\u0026\\u0026 `\\\\n` \\u0026\\u0026 `  }` \u2014 in ABAP backquotes, `\\\\n` is two chars (backslash, n)?? Yes, backquoted strings are literal: `\\\\n` = backslash + n. So lv_a1 = `{\\\\n    lv_open = '{'.\\\\n  }` where \\\\n is literal 2 chars. escape( lv_a1 ): `{` \u2192 not special (cp 123 \u226532 \\u003c128) \u2192 `{`; `\\\\` \u2192 `\\\\\\\\`; `n` \u2192 n; spaces; `'`; `{`... wait escape processes `{` fine. So escaped = `{\\\\\\\\n    lv_open = '{'.\\\\\\\\n  }`. In the JSON body: `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\\n    lv_open = '{'.\\\\\\\\n  }\\\"`.\\n\\nParsing: split_entries on output array \u2014 the arguments string contains `{` and `'` and `.` \u2014 in_str covers it \u2713. Entry 1 ends at the right `}` \u2713. extract_str('arguments') regex: value chars `{\\\\n...` wait raw content between quotes: `{\\\\\\\\n    lv_open = '{'.\\\\\\\\n  }` \u2014 regex `((?:[^\\\"\\\\\\\\]|\\\\\\\\.)*)`: `{` ok, `\\\\\\\\` matches `\\\\\\\\.` (backslash + any) \u2713 consuming both backslashes... wait the raw content has `\\\\\\\\` (two backslash chars) then `n`. The regex `[^\\\"\\\\\\\\]` matches any char except quote/backslash; `\\\\\\\\.` matches backslash followed by any char. Sequence `\\\\`,`\\\\`,`n`: first `\\\\\\\\.` consumes the two backslashes? `\\\\\\\\.` in the pattern means literal backslash then any char \u2192 consumes `\\\\` + `\\\\`. Then `n` matches `[^\\\"\\\\\\\\]`. Good. Then `'`,`{`,`'`,`.` fine. Then `\\\\`,`\\\\` again consumed by `\\\\\\\\.`. Then `n`, spaces, `}`. Then the closing `\\\"` unescaped ends the group \u2713. Submatch = raw `{\\\\\\\\n    lv_open = '{'.\\\\\\\\n  }` \u2192 unescape \u2192 `{\\\\n    lv_open = '{'.\\\\n  }` = lv_a1 \u2713.\\n\\nThe escape function on `'`? cp 39 \u2192 \u226532 \\u003c128 \u2192 kept \u2713.\\n\\nNow \u2014 the second responses entry parse: extract_str('call_id') \u2192 'c2' \u2713, name \u2713, arguments \u2713.\\n\\nGreat. Also check `IF lv_e NS '\\\"function_call\\\"'` still matches entries \u2713.\\n\\nNow the executor check. Let me read zcl_pia_00_executor main to see if it uses extract_balanced anywhere.\\n\\nActually \u2014 also zcl_pia_15_t_* tools might use json_util for parsing tool args (e.g., extracting 'source'). They use extract_str mostly. The risk is low; but a quick outline + grep-ish read of one or two would cost tokens. I'll read executor main only.\\n\\nActually, since json_util changes only affect strings-with-braces cases (previously broken), regression risk is minimal. But the extract_str\u2192parse_calls_responses change is in llm_http only. I'll skip reading everything; maybe just quickly read executor to be thorough since it's the agent loop. Let me do it in parallel with nothing else... I'll just do the writes; a quick read of executor is cheap insurance. Let me batch: read_object executor (main) \u2014 one call, then writes.\\n\\nActually to keep turns efficient: this turn, do the three write_method calls + the test fix write_method... wait the test fix is in testclasses include of llm_http (local class ltcl_parse_calls, method chat_two_calls_one_brace) \u2014 write_method with class='LTCL_PARSE_CALLS' works? The tool says: \\\"Owner class (a local test class name) when the method name is not unique\\\". So write_method( name='ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP' method='chat_two_calls_one_brace' class='LTCL_PARSE_CALLS' ). \\n\\nAll four writes are independent \u2192 batch them in one block. Then activate both classes in next turn (activate after write; the two activations are independent \u2192 batch). Then run tests.\\n\\nLet me prepare the chat test method fix: expected arguments single-line:\\n\\n```abap\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals(\\n      exp = `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}`\\n      act = ls_1-arguments\\n      msg = 'chat: arguments of first call' ).\\n```\\n\\nNow write extract_balanced. One concern: `DATA(lv_out)` inline \u2014 fine. Let me also keep variable naming style consistent.\\n\\nAlso: `FIND ... MATCH OFFSET lv_off.` needs `DATA lv_off TYPE i.` declared.\\n\\nsplit_entries: note `DATA(lv_n) = lv_pos - lv_start + 1.` inside loop then `APPEND iv_arr+lv_start(lv_n)`. Inline DATA in loop is OK.\\n\\nLet me write everything now.\",\"type\":\"reasoning_text\"}],\"encrypted_content\":null,\"id\":\"rs_20261007222900a9e61f5b962f4b7b\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"summary\":[],\"type\":\"reasoning\"},{\"content\":[{\"annotations\":[],\"text\":\"The tests are red for exactly the reported bug (only 1 of 2 calls parsed; first arguments swallow the rest). Root cause confirmed in `zcl_pia_00_json_util`: `split_entries` and `extract_balanced` count braces inside JSON string literals, so an escaped source like `lv_open = '{'.` breaks the balance. Fix: make both scanners string-literal aware, and in `parse_calls_responses` take `arguments` with the escaped-aware `extract_str` (it is a string value, not an object).\",\"type\":\"output_text\"}],\"id\":\"msg_20261007222900a9e61f5b962f4b7b\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"message\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"extract_balanced\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"  METHOD extract_balanced.\\\\n    \\\\\\\" braces inside JSON string literals must not count toward the depth:\\\\n    \\\\\\\" an escaped source like \\\\\\\"lv_open = '{'.\\\\\\\" would otherwise let the scan run\\\\n    \\\\\\\" past the closing bracket and eat the rest of the response\\\\n    DATA lv_len TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_pos TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_off TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_start TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_depth TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_out TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_ch TYPE string.\\\\n    DATA lv_in_str TYPE abap_bool VALUE abap_false.\\\\n    lv_len = strlen( iv_json ).\\\\n\\\\n    FIND '\\\\\\\"' \\u0026\\u0026 iv_key \\u0026\\u0026 '\\\\\\\"' IN iv_json MATCH OFFSET lv_off.\\\\n    IF sy-subrc \\u003c\\u003e 0. RETURN. ENDIF.\\\\n    lv_pos = lv_off + strlen( iv_key ) + 2.\\\\n\\\\n    \\\\\\\" first opening bracket after the key\\\\n    lv_start = -1.\\\\n    WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len.\\\\n      lv_ch = iv_json+lv_pos(1).\\\\n      IF lv_in_str = abap_true.\\\\n        IF lv_ch = `\\\\\\\\`.\\\\n          lv_pos = lv_pos + 2. \\\\\\\" escaped char (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\n ...) belongs to the string\\\\n          CONTINUE.\\\\n        ELSEIF lv_ch = `\\\\\\\"`.\\\\n          lv_in_str = abap_false.\\\\n        ENDIF.\\\\n      ELSE.\\\\n        IF lv_ch = `\\\\\\\"`.\\\\n          lv_in_str = abap_true.\\\\n        ELSEIF lv_ch = iv_open.\\\\n          lv_start = lv_pos.\\\\n          EXIT.\\\\n        ENDIF.\\\\n      ENDIF.\\\\n      lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\\\n    ENDWHILE.\\\\n    IF lv_start \\u003c 0. RETURN. ENDIF.\\\\n\\\\n    \\\\\\\" balanced scan to the matching closing bracket, skipping string literals\\\\n    lv_depth = 1.\\\\n    lv_pos = lv_start.\\\\n    lv_in_str = abap_false.\\\\n    WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len - 1 AND lv_depth \\u003e 0.\\\\n      lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\\\n      lv_ch = iv_json+lv_pos(1).\\\\n      IF lv_in_str = abap_true.\\\\n        IF lv_ch = `\\\\\\\\`.\\\\n          lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\\\n        ELSEIF lv_ch = `\\\\\\\"`.\\\\n          lv_in_str = abap_false.\\\\n        ENDIF.\\\\n      ELSE.\\\\n        IF lv_ch = `\\\\\\\"`.\\\\n          lv_in_str = abap_true.\\\\n        ELSEIF lv_ch = iv_open.\\\\n          lv_depth = lv_depth + 1.\\\\n        ELSEIF lv_ch = iv_close.\\\\n          lv_depth = lv_depth - 1.\\\\n        ENDIF.\\\\n      ENDIF.\\\\n    ENDWHILE.\\\\n    IF lv_depth \\u003e 0.\\\\n      lv_pos = lv_len - 1. \\\\\\\" unclosed: return the tail (previous behavior)\\\\n    ENDIF.\\\\n    lv_out = lv_pos - lv_start + 1.\\\\n    IF lv_start + lv_out \\u003e lv_len.\\\\n      lv_out = lv_len - lv_start.\\\\n    ENDIF.\\\\n    IF lv_out \\u003e 0.\\\\n      rv_ = iv_json+lv_start(lv_out).\\\\n    ENDIF.\\\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7228020597245076136\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7228020597245076136\",\"name\":\"write_method\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"split_entries\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"  METHOD split_entries.\\\\n    \\\\\\\" top-level {...} entries of a JSON array string; braces and quotes inside\\\\n    \\\\\\\" string literals (escaped source code with '{' for example) do not count,\\\\n    \\\\\\\" every access stays clamped\\\\n    DATA lv_len TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_pos TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_start TYPE i VALUE -1.\\\\n    DATA lv_depth TYPE i VALUE 0.\\\\n    DATA lv_n TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_ch TYPE string.\\\\n    DATA lv_in_str TYPE abap_bool VALUE abap_false.\\\\n    lv_len = strlen( iv_arr ).\\\\n    WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len.\\\\n      lv_ch = iv_arr+lv_pos(1).\\\\n      IF lv_in_str = abap_true.\\\\n        IF lv_ch = `\\\\\\\\`.\\\\n          lv_pos = lv_pos + 2. \\\\\\\" escaped char belongs to the string\\\\n          CONTINUE.\\\\n        ELSEIF lv_ch = `\\\\\\\"`.\\\\n          lv_in_str = abap_false.\\\\n        ENDIF.\\\\n      ELSE.\\\\n        IF lv_ch = `\\\\\\\"`.\\\\n          lv_in_str = abap_true.\\\\n        ELSEIF lv_ch = '{'.\\\\n          IF lv_depth = 0.\\\\n            lv_start = lv_pos.\\\\n          ENDIF.\\\\n          lv_depth = lv_depth + 1.\\\\n        ELSEIF lv_ch = '}'.\\\\n          IF lv_depth \\u003e 0.\\\\n            lv_depth = lv_depth - 1.\\\\n            IF lv_depth = 0 AND lv_start \\u003e= 0.\\\\n              lv_n = lv_pos - lv_start + 1.\\\\n              IF lv_start + lv_n \\u003e lv_len.\\\\n                lv_n = lv_len - lv_start.\\\\n              ENDIF.\\\\n              IF lv_n \\u003e 0.\\\\n                APPEND iv_arr+lv_start(lv_n) TO rt_.\\\\n              ENDIF.\\\\n              lv_start = -1.\\\\n            ENDIF.\\\\n          ENDIF.\\\\n        ENDIF.\\\\n      ENDIF.\\\\n      lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\\\n    ENDWHILE.\\\\n    IF lv_depth \\u003e 0 AND lv_start \\u003e= 0 AND lv_start \\u003c lv_len.\\\\n      \\\\\\\" unclosed last entry: keep the defensive tail\\\\n      lv_n = lv_len - lv_start.\\\\n      APPEND iv_arr+lv_start(lv_n) TO rt_.\\\\n    ENDIF.\\\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7228020597245076135\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7228020597245076135\",\"name\":\"write_method\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"parse_calls_responses\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"  METHOD parse_calls_responses.\\\\n    DATA(lv_arr) = zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eextract_balanced(\\\\n      iv_json = iv_body iv_key = 'output' iv_open = '[' iv_close = ']' ).\\\\n    LOOP AT zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003esplit_entries( lv_arr ) INTO DATA(lv_e).\\\\n      IF lv_e NS '\\\\\\\"function_call\\\\\\\"' AND lv_e NS '\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"function_call\\\\\\\"'.\\\\n        CONTINUE.\\\\n      ENDIF.\\\\n      DATA ls TYPE zif_pia_00_tool=\\u003ets_call.\\\\n      ls-id = zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eextract_str( iv_json = lv_e iv_name = 'call_id' ).\\\\n      ls-name = zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eextract_str( iv_json = lv_e iv_name = 'name' ).\\\\n      \\\\\\\" arguments is a STRING containing JSON: take it with the escaped-aware\\\\n      \\\\\\\" extract_str and unescape it (extract_balanced would run past the closing\\\\n      \\\\\\\" quote whenever the source holds an unbalanced brace like lv_open = '{'.)\\\\n      ls-arguments = zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eunescape(\\\\n        zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eextract_str( iv_json = lv_e iv_name = 'arguments' ) ).\\\\n      IF ls-arguments IS INITIAL.\\\\n        ls-arguments = zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eunescape( zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eextract_balanced(\\\\n                         iv_json = lv_e iv_key = 'arguments' iv_open = '{' iv_close = '}' ) ).\\\\n      ENDIF.\\\\n      IF ls-name IS NOT INITIAL.\\\\n        APPEND ls TO rt_.\\\\n      ENDIF.\\\\n    ENDLOOP.\\\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7228020597245076134\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7228020597245076134\",\"name\":\"write_method\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"class\\\":\\\"LTCL_PARSE_CALLS\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"chat_two_calls_one_brace\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"  METHOD chat_two_calls_one_brace.\\\\n    \\\\\\\" same scenario in the chat format (arguments is a nested object)\\\\n    DATA lv_body TYPE string.\\\\n    lv_body = `{` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n              `\\\\\\\"choices\\\\\\\":[{\\\\\\\"message\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"role\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"assistant\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"tool_calls\\\\\\\":[` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n                `{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"c1\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"function\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"function\\\\\\\":{` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n                  `\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"write_method\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"arguments\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"method\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"M1\\\\\\\",` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n                  `\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\\\\\"}}}},` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n                `{\\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"c2\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"function\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"function\\\\\\\":{` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n                  `\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"write_method\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"arguments\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"method\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"M2\\\\\\\",` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n                  `\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"    DATA lv_two TYPE i VALUE 2.\\\\\\\"}}}}` \\u0026\\u0026\\\\n              `]}}]}`.\\\\n\\\\n    DATA(lt_calls) = zcl_pia_00_llm_http=\\u003eparse_calls_chat( lv_body ).\\\\n\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals(\\\\n      exp = 2\\\\n      act = lines( lt_calls )\\\\n      msg = 'chat: both tool_calls entries must be parsed' ).\\\\n\\\\n    READ TABLE lt_calls INDEX 1 INTO DATA(ls_1).\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals(\\\\n      exp = 'c1'\\\\n      act = ls_1-id\\\\n      msg = 'chat: id of first call' ).\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals(\\\\n      exp = `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"method\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"M1\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\\\\\"}`\\\\n      act = ls_1-arguments\\\\n      msg = 'chat: arguments of first call' ).\\\\n\\\\n    READ TABLE lt_calls INDEX 2 INTO DATA(ls_2).\\\\n    cl_abap_unit_assert=\\u003eassert_equals(\\\\n      exp = 'c2'\\\\n      act = ls_2-id\\\\n      msg = 'chat: id of second call' ).\\\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7228020597245076133\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7228020597245076133\",\"name\":\"write_method\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"}],\"parallel_tool_calls\":true,\"previous_response_id\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":14092,\"input_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":