{\"conversation_id\":\"\",\"created_at\":1791383679,\"error\":null,\"id\":\"resp_202610072232403352e885d36641ef\",\"incomplete_details\":null,\"instructions\":\"You are PIA (Pi-ABAP Agent), written in ABAP, running on the model glm-5.3 (z.ai) through backend OSG-STORE. If asked which model you are, say exactly that; never claim another model or vendor. Tools: outline(name), read_method(name, method, class), write_method(name, method, source - one METHOD block, class), read_object(name, include main|testclasses), write_source(name, source - FULL source, include), activate(name), run_tests(name). Rules: prefer outline, read_method and write_method for changing existing methods; use write_source only for new methods, changed declarations or a new test include, and then write the FULL source; read before write; always activate after write; if activation says publish pending, finish the turn and run_tests in the next turn. Answer briefly in the user language. Your own code: ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR (agent loop), ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (LLM client: builds requests, parses responses and tool calls), ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL (JSON helpers), ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION and ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION_STORE (conversation), ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY (tools), ZCL_PIA_15_T_* (the tools), ZCL_PIA_20_B_* (backends), ZCL_PIA_30_* (terminal, turns). Unit tests live in the testclasses include of a class.\",\"metadata\":null,\"model\":\"glm-5.3\",\"object\":\"response\",\"output\":[{\"content\":[{\"text\":\"The write_method left an empty `METHOD extract_balanced.` + ENDMETHOD? Actually the file ends with:\\n\\n```\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\n  METHOD extract_balanced.\\n\\nENDCLASS.\\n```\\n\\nSo there's a dangling `METHOD extract_balanced.` without ENDMETHOD. I need to fix: remove the trailing empty method declaration. I'll use write_method to replace that broken block? The block \\\"METHOD extract_balanced.\\\" ... there's no ENDMETHOD. Easier: rewrite the whole main via write_source with the full corrected source. Let me do that: take the source above, remove the trailing `METHOD extract_balanced.` line.\\n\\nAlso check the balanced test expectations: `balanced_unclosed` expects tail `{\\\"x\\\":1` for `{\\\"o\\\":{\\\"x\\\":1`. New logic: key `\\\"o\\\"` found at offset 1; lv_pos = 1+1+2=4. chars: `:` then `{` \u2192 lv_start=5. depth=1, pos=5; loop: pos=6 `{` depth2; 7 `x`; 8 `\\\"`; 9 `:`; 10 `1`; pos \\u003c lv_len-1 (len=11, so pos\\u003c10): pos=9 then exits at pos=10? len = strlen(`{\\\"o\\\":{\\\"x\\\":1`) = 11. Loop while lv_pos \\u003c 10: pos goes 6..9, ends at 9 with depth 2. depth\\u003e0 \u2192 lv_pos = 10. out = 10-5+1=6 \u2192 `{\\\"x\\\":1` \u2713.\\n\\n`balanced_no_open_bracket`: `{\\\"o\\\":\\\"txt\\\"}` key at 1, pos=4: `:`, then `\\\"` \u2192 in_str true, chars t,x,t, then `\\\"` closes, then `}` \u2014 no open bracket found \u2192 lv_start=-1 \u2192 RETURN, rv_ initial. Test expects `` \u2713. Note previous behavior also empty. Good.\\n\\n`balanced_object`: `{\\\"o\\\":{\\\"x\\\":1},\\\"y\\\":2}` \u2192 start at 5, scan: 6 `{` d2, `\\\"x\\\"` string, `:`,`1`, `}` d1, `,`, `\\\"y\\\"` string, `:` ,`2`, `}` d0 \u2192 pos at that `}` = index? Result `{\\\"x\\\":1}` \u2713.\\n\\n`balanced_nested_and_array` with `[`/`]`: `{\\\"a\\\":[1,[2,3]]}` key `a` at 1, pos=4: `:`, `[` start=5. scan: `1`, `,`, `[` d2, `2`, `,`, `3`, `]` d1, `]` d0 \u2192 `[1,[2,3]]` \u2713.\\n\\nAlso `lv_pos = lv_pos + 2` when backslash in string during bracket search \u2014 could skip past closing quote if string ends with `\\\\\\\"`? If string is `\\\"abc\\\\\\\"` \u2026 that's an escaped quote, still inside string. Fine. Edge: backslash as last char of the whole string \u2192 lv_pos could exceed lv_len; loop condition handles.\\n\\nIn the depth loop, when lv_ch = `\\\\` I do lv_pos = lv_pos + 1 and then the loop's lv_pos = lv_pos + 1 advances 2 total. OK.\\n\\nOne more subtlety in the depth loop: `WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len - 1 AND lv_depth \\u003e 0` \u2014 same as before.\\n\\nPotential infinite loop: in the first WHILE, if lv_ch = `\\\\` and lv_pos+2 \\u003e lv_len, loop exits since lv_pos \\u003e= lv_len. fine.\\n\\nNow also split_entries has the same string-blindness problem for the chat format: entries of tool_calls array. In the chat test, the array `[{...},{...}]` \u2014 each entry's braces: does the `'{'.` inside a string break split_entries? For chat, the `'{'.` is inside arguments nested object but NOT inside a JSON string (chat arguments is a real object, the source string is a JSON string `\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"`). Wait in my chat test the source value contains `'{'.` \u2014 that's inside a JSON string `\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"`. The `'` are literal characters in the JSON string. So braces inside JSON strings. split_entries counts `{`/`}` blindly \u2192 for the first entry: depth counting would include the `{` inside the string \u2192 one extra `{`, so entry 1 wouldn't close at its real `}`... it would close later. Hmm, that means chat parse also broken via split_entries.\\n\\nThe red chat test failure was in arguments (extract_balanced), but after fixing extract_balanced, split_entries may still mis-split for braces inside strings. I should fix split_entries too, making it string-aware. Note the existing comment says \\\"defensive: braces inside string values may be unbalanced, so every access is clamped\\\". Making it string-aware is the proper fix.\\n\\nLet me check: with the chat test body, tool_calls array content:\\n```\\n{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}}},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",...}\\n```\\nBlind depth: entry1 opens `{`(1) ... `\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"` \u2014 the `{` inside string \u2192 depth +1 \u2192 then `}}}` closes 3 \u2192 depth reaches 0 exactly at the third `}`. Wait count: `{` id-level(1), `function` `{`(2), `arguments` `{`(3), string `{`(4), then `}}}}`? The text after source value: `\\\"}}}},` \u2014 hmm let me count the closers. Structure: arguments object closes `}`, function object closes `}`, tool_call object closes `}`, i.e. `}}}`  after the string value. Plus the string itself contains `{` but no `}`. So depth after string `{` = 4, then `}` \u21923, `}`\u21922, `}`\u21921 \u2014 not closed! The blind split continues past the `,` into entry 2 and closes at entry 2's first `}`. So entry 1 = both calls merged-ish. Actually the red chat test showed arguments extracted as `{...source...\\\".\\\\\\\"}}` \u2014 includes trailing `}}` \u2014 consistent with extract_balanced quirk, and the second entry lost. Indeed the red result showed only... the chat test asserted lines=2 first? The failure list showed only the arguments assertion failed at line 84, meaning lines( lt_calls ) = 2 passed?? Hmm, wait: assertions run in order: lines=2 (line ~69), then id c1, then arguments (line 84 failed). So split_entries DID return 2 entries. Let me recount.\\n\\nBody tool_calls array (from my test):\\n```\\n{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}}}},\\n{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M2\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    DATA lv_two TYPE i VALUE 2.\\\"}}}}\\n```\\nIn my test source I wrote `\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}}}},` \u2014 the closers: arguments `}`, function `}`, tool_call `}`, then `,`. That's `}}},`. I wrote 4 `}`? Let me look: `\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}}}},` \u2014 in the testclasses I wrote:\\n\\n`\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}}}},` \u2014 hmm from the file above: `\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",` \\u0026\\u0026 `\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}}}},`. Count braces in `}}}}`: 4. Openers in entry1: `{`(tool_call) + `{`(function) + `{`(arguments) + `{`(in string) = 4. So blind depth closes exactly at the 4th `}` \u2014 entry 1 = the full first object, and entry 2 parsed fine. That's why lines=2 passed by luck (the in-string `{` offset by... no wait, blind count: opener count 4 includes the string one, closer count 4 \u2192 balanced at end of entry 1. Lucky!)\\n\\nBut if the string contained `}` instead, or unbalanced differently, it would break. The robust fix: make split_entries string-aware too. The reported bug is fixed by extract_balanced alone (responses path: split_entries splits output items; the `'{'.` lives inside the JSON string of arguments; blind count: entry has `{` openers: item `{`(1), and the in-string `{`(2)... closers: `}}` at end (close item) \u2192 wait the responses entry: `{\\\"id\\\":\\\"a\\\",...,\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\n    lv_open = '{'.\\\\n  }\\\"}` \u2014 openers: item `{`(1), in-string `{` from `lv_open = '{'.`(2), and the arguments value's `{` (the first char of the string value content!) \u2014 the arguments string starts with `{`. So openers: 1 (item) + 2 (inside string: the leading `{` of the source and the one in `'{'.`) = 3. Closers inside string: the source's final `}` (1) + item's `}` (1) = 2. So blind depth never reaches 0 within entry 1 \u2192 entry 1 swallowed everything until entry 2's first `}`. Hence only 1 entry \u2192 the reported symptom!\\n\\nSo split_entries MUST also be string-aware for the responses case. Let me verify: with string-aware split, entry 1 closes at its real `}`. \\n\\nSo fix both methods. split_entries new version: track in_str similarly.\\n\\nNow write full main source for json_util with both methods fixed and remove the dangling METHOD.\\n\\nsplit_entries string-aware:\\n\\n```abap\\n  METHOD split_entries.\\n    \\\" top-level {...} entries of a JSON array string; braces inside JSON string\\n    \\\" literals do not count toward the depth (escaped source like \\\"lv_open = '{'.\\\")\\n    DATA lv_len_arr TYPE i.\\n    DATA lv_pos TYPE i VALUE 0.\\n    DATA lv_start TYPE i.\\n    DATA lv_end TYPE i.\\n    DATA lv_depth TYPE i.\\n    DATA lv_len TYPE i.\\n    DATA lv_ch TYPE string.\\n    DATA lv_in_str TYPE abap_bool VALUE abap_false.\\n    lv_len_arr = strlen( iv_arr ).\\n    WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len_arr.\\n      FIND FIRST OCCURRENCE OF '{' IN iv_arr+lv_pos MATCH OFFSET DATA(lv_off).\\n      IF sy-subrc \\u003c\\u003e 0. EXIT. ENDIF.\\n      lv_start = lv_pos + lv_off.\\n      IF lv_start \\u003e= lv_len_arr. EXIT. ENDIF.\\n      lv_depth = 1.\\n      lv_end = lv_start.\\n      lv_in_str = abap_false.\\n      WHILE lv_end \\u003c lv_len_arr - 1 AND lv_depth \\u003e 0.\\n        lv_end = lv_end + 1.\\n        lv_ch = iv_arr+lv_end(1).\\n        IF lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n          IF lv_ch = `\\\\`.\\n            lv_end = lv_end + 1.\\n          ELSEIF lv_ch = `\\\"`.\\n            lv_in_str = abap_false.\\n          ENDIF.\\n        ELSE.\\n          IF lv_ch = `\\\"`.\\n            lv_in_str = abap_true.\\n          ELSEIF lv_ch = '{'.\\n            lv_depth = lv_depth + 1.\\n          ELSEIF lv_ch = '}'.\\n            lv_depth = lv_depth - 1.\\n          ENDIF.\\n        ENDIF.\\n      ENDWHILE.\\n      ...\\n```\\n\\nWait: the first `{` found by FIND could itself be inside a string (e.g. array starts with a string containing `{` before any object). Edge case; for our tool-call arrays the first `{` is the object. Also after finishing an entry, lv_pos = lv_end + 1, next FIND searches from there \u2014 the next char is `,` then `{`. Fine.\\n\\nBut careful: after an entry, the closing `}` of the entry \u2014 when loop exits because lv_depth = 0, lv_end points at that `}`. Good. If lv_end was advanced past a backslash escape and exceeds bound... clamp exists.\\n\\nAlso one subtlety: when the scan starts, we're at `{` which opens the object; strings inside are tracked. What if `{` from FIND is inside a string that started before it? Then lv_in_str=false at start would misinterpret. Acceptable for our use; the first `{` in tool_calls/output arrays is an object opener.\\n\\nNote existing tests for split_entries? There are none directly in ltcl_json_util (no split tests listed... the private section lists balanced_* but no split tests). Fine.\\n\\nNow also keep the trailing clamp code. Also the dangling `METHOD extract_balanced.` must go \u2014 I'll rewrite full main.\\n\\nAlso check `unescape` behavior on arguments for responses: arguments value = escape(lv_a1) where lv_a1 = `{\\\\n    lv_open = '{'.\\\\n  }` \u2014 note in my test lv_a1 is built with `\\\\n` as literal backslash-n two chars (I used backquoted `\\\\n` which is 2 chars: backslash + n). Wait: in ABAP `\\\\n` in backquotes is literal backslash+n \u2014 2 chars. And escape() of backslash \u2192 `\\\\\\\\`, of n \u2192 n. Hmm, I built lv_a1 = `{` \\u0026\\u0026 `\\\\n` \\u0026\\u0026 ... \u2014 that's literal `\\\\n` 2-char sequence, representing the JSON-escaped newline. Then escape(lv_a1) doubles the backslash \u2192 `\\\\\\\\n`. That's realistic (a recorded body would have `\\\\\\\\n` inside the arguments string). unescape turns `\\\\\\\\n` \u2192 `\\\\n` (backslash + n)? unescape of `\\\\\\\\` \u2192 `\\\\`, then `n` copied \u2192 `\\\\n` literal 2 chars = lv_a1 \u2713. Good: assert equals lv_a1 works.\\n\\nNow the expected arguments after fix: extract_balanced with key 'arguments' open `{` close `}` on entry1: key found, first `{` after key... careful: after `\\\"arguments\\\":` comes `\\\"` (start of string) \u2192 in_str tracking in the \\\"find opening bracket\\\" loop: `\\\"` sets in_str=true, then chars of the string are skipped until closing `\\\"`, then `}` is... iv_open = `{` \u2014 but the opening `{` is INSIDE the string! The find-loop skips strings, so it won't find the `{` inside the string value; after the string closes, next non-string char is `}` (closing the item) \u2014 no `{` found \u2192 returns initial ''. That breaks the responses parsing!\\n\\nHmm. The old code found the first `{` after the key \u2014 which is the one inside the string value (the arguments JSON-as-string starts with `{`). With string-aware skipping we lose it. So for the responses case (arguments as JSON string), extract_balanced must find the opening brace inside the string?? No \u2014 the correct approach: arguments is a STRING containing JSON. The old code accidentally worked by grabbing from the first `{` (inside the string) and balancing blindly to the first `}`... which with the source `'{'.` ran past. \\n\\nBetter fix for parse_calls_responses: extract the arguments STRING (extract_str + unescape), then take the balanced object from that unescaped JSON. But extract_balanced searches a key in json; for the unescaped arguments string like `{\\\\n \\\"name\\\":...}`, we need the whole outer object. Could call extract_balanced with key... hmm, the unescaped string starts with `{`. extract_balanced requires a key first.\\n\\nAlternative: keep extract_balanced's \\\"first opening bracket after key\\\" but make the depth scan string-aware AND, for the arguments-as-string case, let the opening bracket be found inside a string? That's contradictory.\\n\\nCleaner: in parse_calls_responses, change to:\\n```abap\\nls-arguments = zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eunescape( zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eextract_str( iv_json = lv_e iv_name = 'arguments' ) ).\\n```\\nextract_str returns the raw escaped string value; unescape gives the JSON object text `{\\\"name\\\":...,\\\"source\\\":\\\"...'{'....\\\"}`. That's the arguments JSON as a string \u2014 exactly what should go into ls-arguments (executor presumably parses it or passes as string). What does the executor do with arguments? For responses, arguments is a JSON string per spec, so ls-arguments should be the unescaped JSON text. The old code produced balanced-substring then unescape \u2014 roughly same for well-formed input.\\n\\nCheck red expectations: test expects ls_1-arguments = lv_a1 (the raw source text with literal `\\\\n` sequences) \u2014 since unescape(extract_str) gives back lv_a1 \u2713.\\n\\nFor chat: arguments is a nested real object \u2192 extract_balanced(key='arguments', '{','}') still right, now string-aware. Expected in test: the object text with real newlines? My chat test expects `|{\\\\n    \\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\"...|` \u2014 with |...| and literal \\\\n inside... I wrote exp = `|{\\\\n    \\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\\n    \\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"\\\\n  }|` \u2014 inside | |, `\\\\n` is... in string templates `\\\\n` isn't special; it's literal backslash-n? Actually in ABAP string templates, `\\\\` must be escaped as `\\\\\\\\`. `\\\\n` in a template is invalid? Hmm \u2014 in | | templates, backslash is an escape char; `\\\\n` would be a parser error (\\\"invalid escape\\\")? Actually ABAP string templates: escape sequences are \\\\\\\\, \\\\', \\\\\\\", \\\\n(? no). Let me recall: In string templates, the escape character is `\\\\` and valid escapes: `\\\\\\\\`, `\\\\``... The rule: within | |, special chars are {, }, \\\\, '. `\\\\n`? I believe `\\\\` followed by anything other than \\\\\\\\, \\\\{, \\\\}, \\\\' is a syntax error... Hmm, but the testclasses compiled and RAN (red) \u2014 the chat test ran and failed only on arguments comparison, showing expected `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}` \u2014 wait the expected in the failure output shows real newlines? The JSON shows \\\"Expected [{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"ZCL_X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"method\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"M1\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\\\\\"}]\\\" \u2014 no visible \\\\n. The actual was `...'{'.\\\\\\\"}}`. Hmm so my expected used |\\\\n...| \u2014 since it compiled and the diff printed, \\\\n inside template likely produced a real newline. In ABAP string templates, `\\\\n` is NOT an escape \u2014 I think unknown escapes are errors. But it compiled... In newer ABAP, string templates don't support \\\\n; you'd use { ... }. But the error output shows expected without literal backslash-n, suggesting real newline. OK whatever \u2014 it compiled and ran; the expected value evidently contains real newlines (or literal \\\\n; the failure print may normalize).\\n\\nSimpler: rewrite the chat test expectation to build expected from the known object text without \\\\n ambiguity: just compare to the substring I control. Let me set chat expected as:\\n\\n```\\nDATA lv_exp1 TYPE string.\\nlv_exp1 = '{' \\u0026\\u0026 |\\\\n| ... \\n```\\nHmm messy. Alternative: after fix, chat arguments = extract_balanced of entry \u2192 `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}` (no newlines, exactly the body text). I'll change the expectation to that literal (in backticks, no newlines) \u2014 cleaner and matches the actual JSON in the test body. Let me update the chat test accordingly (write_source of testclasses again), plus keep both tests.\\n\\nAlso verify the chat entry post-fix via split_entries string-aware: entry1 = `{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_method\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}}}` \u2014 the string `\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"` : the `{` inside is skipped (in_str) \u2713, closes at the 3rd `}` \u2713. extract_balanced on arguments: key found, first non-string `{` after it = arguments object \u2713, depth scan: `\\\"source\\\"` value string skipped, then `}}}` \u2192 closes at first `}` \u2192 `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_X\\\",\\\"method\\\":\\\"M1\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"    lv_open = '{'.\\\"}` \u2713. id via extract_str 'id' \u2192 c1 \u2713. name: extract_str 'name' \u2192 first \\\"name\\\" in entry is inside function object \u2192 \\\"write_method\\\" \u2713 (as before).\\n\\nResponses path post-fix: output array split string-aware: entry1 closes at its real `}` (the `}` inside arguments string and the source's `}` are inside the string value \u2014 wait the arguments string value is `\\\"{\\\\n    lv_open = '{'.\\\\n  }\\\"` \u2014 hold on, in the body the arguments string starts with `{` (escaped source begins with `{`). During split scan of entry1: at `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"` the `\\\"` opens a string; inside, everything until the closing `\\\"` is skipped, including `{` and `}` chars \u2713; then `\\\"` closes string, then `}` closes item \u2713. Entry2 similarly \u2713. Then filter function_call \u2713. id: extract_str 'call_id' \u2192 c1 \u2713. arguments: NEW code = unescape(extract_str 'arguments') = lv_a1 \u2713. name = write_method \u2713.\\n\\nNow, will other callers of extract_balanced break? Callers: parse_calls_chat (tool_calls array), parse_calls_responses (output array), zif~chat (output array for text), build_input_items (tool_calls array), and my new responses arguments change. Array extraction with key tool_calls/output: key then `:` then `[` \u2014 string-aware find still finds `[` \u2713.\\n\\nBut careful in build_input_items/zif~chat the content may be replay-raw `{\\\"tool_calls\\\":[...]}` \u2014 fine.\\n\\nOne more: in extract_balanced responses usage for 'output' \u2014 the body contains \\\"output\\\":[ ... ] \u2014 with my string-aware search, ok.\\n\\nAlso zif_pia_00_llm~chat for responses extracts 'text' via extract_str \u2014 unaffected.\\n\\nNow what about places where the whole response 'content' string may include quotes \u2014 extract_str regex handles escapes \u2713.\\n\\nPlan:\\n1. write_source full main for ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL with fixed split_entries + extract_balanced (drop dangling method).\\n2. Change parse_calls_responses in ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP to use extract_str+unescape for arguments.\\n3. Update chat test expectation to the literal object text.\\n4. Activate both, run tests for both classes (json_util tests must stay green except pre-existing reds \u2014 note: ltcl_json_util has no split/balanced tests that conflict? balanced_unclosed expects tail \u2014 my impl returns tail \u2713; balanced_no_open_bracket expects '' \u2713).\\n\\nAlso run executor tests? The task: show tests green. I'll run both classes' tests.\\n\\nWrite full JSON_UTIL main now. Careful to keep everything else identical.\",\"type\":\"reasoning_text\"}],\"encrypted_content\":null,\"id\":\"rs_202610072232403352e885d36641ef\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"summary\":[],\"type\":\"reasoning\"},{\"content\":[{\"annotations\":[],\"text\":\"The dangling `METHOD extract_balanced.` from my write must go, and the same string-blindness also affects `split_entries` (that's what merges the two responses items into one). I'll rewrite the full main include with both methods string-aware:\",\"type\":\"output_text\"}],\"id\":\"msg_202610072232403352e885d36641ef\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"message\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"CLASS zcl_pia_00_json_util DEFINITION PUBLIC FINAL CREATE PUBLIC.\\\\n\\\\n  PUBLIC SECTION.\\\\n    CLASS-METHODS escape\\\\n      IMPORTING iv_        TYPE string\\\\n      RETURNING VALUE(rv_) TYPE string.\\\\n\\\\n    CLASS-METHODS codepoint\\\\n      IMPORTING iv_        TYPE string\\\\n      RETURNING VALUE(rv_) TYPE i.\\\\n\\\\n    CLASS-METHODS to_int\\\\n      IMPORTING iv_        TYPE string\\\\n      RETURNING VALUE(rv_) TYPE i.\\\\n\\\\n    CLASS-METHODS to_hex4\\\\n      IMPORTING iv_        TYPE i\\\\n      RETURNING VALUE(rv_) TYPE string.\\\\n\\\\n    CLASS-METHODS hexval\\\\n      IMPORTING iv_        TYPE string\\\\n      RETURNING VALUE(rv_) TYPE i.\\\\n\\\\n    CLASS-METHODS extract_str\\\\n      IMPORTING iv_json     TYPE string\\\\n                iv_name     TYPE string\\\\n      RETURNING VALUE(rv_)  TYPE string.\\\\n\\\\n    CLASS-METHODS unescape\\\\n      IMPORTING iv_        TYPE string\\\\n      RETURNING VALUE(rv_) TYPE string.\\\\n\\\\n    \\\\\\\" top-level {...} entries of a JSON array string\\\\n    CLASS-METHODS split_entries\\\\n      IMPORTING iv_arr      TYPE string\\\\n      RETURNING VALUE(rt_)  TYPE string_table.\\\\n\\\\n    CLASS-METHODS extract_balanced\\\\n      IMPORTING iv_json     TYPE string\\\\n                iv_key      TYPE string\\\\n                iv_open     TYPE string\\\\n                iv_close    TYPE string\\\\n      RETURNING VALUE(rv_)  TYPE string.\\\\n\\\\nENDCLASS.\\\\n\\\\nCLASS zcl_pia_00_json_util IMPLEMENTATION.\\\\n\\\\n  METHOD escape.\\\\n    DATA lv_i TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_len TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_ch TYPE string.\\\\n    DATA lv_cp TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_from TYPE i.\\\\n    lv_len = strlen( iv_ ).\\\\n    WHILE lv_i \\u003c lv_len.\\\\n      lv_ch = iv_+lv_i(1).\\\\n      CASE lv_ch.\\\\n        WHEN `\\\\\\\\`. rv_ = rv_ \\u0026\\u0026 `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\`.\\\\n        WHEN `\\\\\\\"`. rv_ = rv_ \\u0026\\u0026 `\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"`.\\\\n        WHEN OTHERS.\\\\n          IF lv_ch = |\\\\\\\\n|.\\\\n            rv_ = rv_ \\u0026\\u0026 `\\\\\\\\n`.\\\\n          ELSEIF lv_ch = |\\\\\\\\r|.\\\\n            rv_ = rv_ \\u0026\\u0026 `\\\\\\\\r`.\\\\n          ELSEIF lv_ch = |\\\\\\\\t|.\\\\n            rv_ = rv_ \\u0026\\u0026 `\\\\\\\\t`.\\\\n          ELSE.\\\\n            \\\\\\\" codepoint from the UTF-8 bytes, read with xstrlen and x(1) offsets: portable to SAP\\\\n            \\\\\\\" (the old strlen/to_upper on an xstring did not compile there) and independent of\\\\n            \\\\\\\" cl_abap_conv_out_ce=\\u003euccpi, which is wrong in open-abap-core (high byte * 255)\\\\n            \\\\\\\" a character outside the BMP (emoji) is two UTF-16 units: alone, a unit fails to convert\\\\n            \\\\\\\" on SAP (CX_SY_CONVERSION_CODEPAGE) and becomes U+FFFD in OSG; take the pair together\\\\n            lv_cp = codepoint( lv_ch ).\\\\n            IF lv_cp = 65533 AND lv_i + 1 \\u003c lv_len.\\\\n              lv_from = lv_i.\\\\n              lv_cp = codepoint( substring( val = iv_ off = lv_from len = 2 ) ).\\\\n              IF lv_cp \\u003e= 65536.\\\\n                lv_cp = lv_cp - 65536.\\\\n                rv_ = rv_ \\u0026\\u0026 `\\\\\\\\u` \\u0026\\u0026 to_hex4( 55296 + lv_cp DIV 1024 ) \\u0026\\u0026 `\\\\\\\\u` \\u0026\\u0026 to_hex4( 56320 + lv_cp MOD 1024 ).\\\\n                lv_i = lv_i + 2.\\\\n                CONTINUE.\\\\n              ENDIF.\\\\n              lv_cp = 65533.\\\\n            ENDIF.\\\\n            IF lv_cp \\u003e= 32 AND lv_cp \\u003c 128.\\\\n              rv_ = rv_ \\u0026\\u0026 lv_ch.\\\\n            ELSE.\\\\n              rv_ = rv_ \\u0026\\u0026 `\\\\\\\\u` \\u0026\\u0026 to_hex4( lv_cp ).\\\\n            ENDIF.\\\\n          ENDIF.\\\\n      ENDCASE.\\\\n      lv_i = lv_i + 1.\\\\n    ENDWHILE.\\\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\\\n\\\\n  METHOD codepoint.\\\\n    DATA lv_x TYPE xstring.\\\\n    DATA lv_b TYPE x LENGTH 1.\\\\n    DATA lv_b0 TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_b1 TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_b2 TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_b3 TYPE i.\\\\n    \\\\\\\" a lone UTF-16 surrogate does not convert on SAP; caught here because the method declares\\\\n    \\\\\\\" no RAISING (outside it the exception would arrive as CX_SY_NO_HANDLER)\\\\n    TRY.\\\\n        lv_x = cl_abap_conv_codepage=\\u003ecreate_out( )-\\u003econvert( iv_ ).\\\\n      CATCH cx_root.\\\\n        rv_ = 65533.\\\\n        RETURN.\\\\n    ENDTRY.\\\\n    lv_b = lv_x+0(1).\\\\n    lv_b0 = lv_b.\\\\n    CASE xstrlen( lv_x ).\\\\n      WHEN 1.\\\\n        rv_ = lv_b0.\\\\n      WHEN 2.\\\\n        lv_b = lv_x+1(1).\\\\n        lv_b1 = lv_b.\\\\n        rv_ = ( lv_b0 - 192 ) * 64 + ( lv_b1 - 128 ).\\\\n      WHEN 3.\\\\n        lv_b = lv_x+1(1).\\\\n        lv_b1 = lv_b.\\\\n        lv_b = lv_x+2(1).\\\\n        lv_b2 = lv_b.\\\\n        rv_ = ( lv_b0 - 224 ) * 4096 + ( lv_b1 - 128 ) * 64 + ( lv_b2 - 128 ).\\\\n      WHEN 4.\\\\n        lv_b = lv_x+1(1).\\\\n        lv_b1 = lv_b.\\\\n        lv_b = lv_x+2(1).\\\\n        lv_b2 = lv_b.\\\\n        lv_b = lv_x+3(1).\\\\n        lv_b3 = lv_b.\\\\n        rv_ = ( lv_b0 - 240 ) * 262144 + ( lv_b1 - 128 ) * 4096 + ( lv_b2 - 128 ) * 64 + ( lv_b3 - 128 ).\\\\n      WHEN OTHERS.\\\\n        rv_ = 65533.\\\\n    ENDCASE.\\\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\\\n\\\\n  METHOD to_int.\\\\n    \\\\\\\" hex pair -\\u003e int\\\\n    rv_ = hexval( substring( val = iv_ off = 0 len = 1 ) ) * 16\\\\n          + hexval( substring( val = iv_ off = 1 len = 1 ) ).\\\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\\\n\\\\n  METHOD to_hex4.\\\\n    \\\\\\\" 4-digit uppercase hex (was decimal: 1046 -\\u003e '1046' instead of '0416')\\\\n    CONSTANTS c_hex TYPE string VALUE `0123456789ABCDEF`.\\\\n    DATA lv_n TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_d TYPE i.\\\\n    lv_n = iv_.\\\\n    DO 4 TIMES.\\\\n      lv_d = lv_n MOD 16.\\\\n      rv_ = substring( val = c_hex off = lv_d len = 1 ) \\u0026\\u0026 rv_.\\\\n      lv_n = lv_n DIV 16.\\\\n    ENDDO.\\\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\\\n\\\\n  METHOD hexval.\\\\n    CASE to_upper( substring( val = iv_ off = 0 len = 1 ) ).\\\\n      WHEN '0'. rv_ = 0. WHEN '1'. rv_ = 1. WHEN '2'. rv_ = 2. WHEN '3'. rv_ = 3.\\\\n      WHEN '4'. rv_ = 4. WHEN '5'. rv_ = 5. WHEN '6'. rv_ = 6. WHEN '7'. rv_ = 7.\\\\n      WHEN '8'. rv_ = 8. WHEN '9'. rv_ = 9. WHEN 'A'. rv_ = 10. WHEN 'B'. rv_ = 11.\\\\n      WHEN 'C'. rv_ = 12. WHEN 'D'. rv_ = 13. WHEN 'E'. rv_ = 14. WHEN 'F'. rv_ = 15.\\\\n    ENDCASE.\\\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\\\n\\\\n  METHOD extract_str.\\\\n    DATA(lv_re) = '\\\\\\\"' \\u0026\\u0026 iv_name \\u0026\\u0026 '\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\s*:\\\\\\\\s*\\\\\\\"((?:[^\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\]|\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\.)*)\\\\\\\"'.\\\\n    FIND FIRST OCCURRENCE OF PCRE lv_re IN iv_json SUBMATCHES rv_.\\\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\\\n\\\\n  METHOD unescape.\\\\n    DATA lv_i TYPE i VALUE 0.\\\\n    DATA lv_len TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_j TYPE i.\\\\n    lv_len = strlen( iv_ ).\\\\n    WHILE lv_i \\u003c lv_len.\\\\n      IF iv_+lv_i(1) = `\\\\\\\\` AND lv_i + 1 \\u003c lv_len.\\\\n        lv_j = lv_i + 1.\\\\n        CASE iv_+lv_j(1).\\\\n          WHEN `n`. rv_ = rv_ \\u0026\\u0026 |\\\\\\\\n|.\\\\n          WHEN `r`. rv_ = rv_ \\u0026\\u0026 |\\\\\\\\r|.\\\\n          WHEN `t`. rv_ = rv_ \\u0026\\u0026 |\\\\\\\\t|.\\\\n          WHEN `u`.\\\\n            \\\\\\\" \\\\\\\\uXXXX -\\u003e character (was: only 5 ASCII codepoints, Cyrillic stayed escaped)\\\\n            DATA lv_hex TYPE string.\\\\n            DATA lv_char TYPE string.\\\\n            IF lv_j + 4 \\u003c lv_len.\\\\n              lv_hex = iv_+lv_j(5).\\\\n              lv_hex = lv_hex+1(4).\\\\n              TRY.\\\\n                  lv_char = cl_abap_conv_in_ce=\\u003euccp( to_upper( lv_hex ) ).\\\\n                  rv_ = rv_ \\u0026\\u0026 lv_char.\\\\n                CATCH cx_root.\\\\n                  rv_ = rv_ \\u0026\\u0026 `\\\\\\\\u` \\u0026\\u0026 lv_hex.\\\\n              ENDTRY.\\\\n              lv_i = lv_i + 4.\\\\n            ENDIF.\\\\n          WHEN OTHERS. rv_ = rv_ \\u0026\\u0026 iv_+lv_j(1).\\\\n        ENDCASE.\\\\n        lv_i = lv_i + 2.\\\\n      ELSE.\\\\n        rv_ = rv_ \\u0026\\u0026 iv_+lv_i(1).\\\\n        lv_i = lv_i + 1.\\\\n      ENDIF.\\\\n    ENDWHILE.\\\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\\\n\\\\n  METHOD split_entries.\\\\n    \\\\\\\" top-level {...} entries of a JSON array string; braces inside JSON string\\\\n    \\\\\\\" literals do not count toward the depth: a source like \\\\\\\"lv_open = '{'.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    \\\\\\\" inside an arguments value would otherwise let an entry swallow the\\\\n    \\\\\\\" following entries; every access stays clamped for malformed input\\\\n    DATA lv_len_arr TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_pos TYPE i VALUE 0.\\\\n    DATA lv_start TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_end TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_depth TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_len TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_ch TYPE string.\\\\n    DATA lv_in_str TYPE abap_bool.\\\\n    lv_len_arr = strlen( iv_arr ).\\\\n    WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len_arr.\\\\n      FIND FIRST OCCURRENCE OF '{' IN iv_arr+lv_pos MATCH OFFSET DATA(lv_off).\\\\n      IF sy-subrc \\u003c\\u003e 0. EXIT. ENDIF.\\\\n      lv_start = lv_pos + lv_off.\\\\n      IF lv_start \\u003e= lv_len_arr. EXIT. ENDIF.\\\\n      lv_depth = 1.\\\\n      lv_end = lv_start.\\\\n      lv_in_str = abap_false.\\\\n      WHILE lv_end \\u003c lv_len_arr - 1 AND lv_depth \\u003e 0.\\\\n        lv_end = lv_end + 1.\\\\n        lv_ch = iv_arr+lv_end(1).\\\\n        IF lv_in_str = abap_true.\\\\n          IF lv_ch = `\\\\\\\\`.\\\\n            lv_end = lv_end + 1. \\\\\\\" escaped char (\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ ...) belongs to the string\\\\n          ELSEIF lv_ch = `\\\\\\\"`.\\\\n            lv_in_str = abap_false.\\\\n          ENDIF.\\\\n        ELSE.\\\\n          IF lv_ch = `\\\\\\\"`.\\\\n            lv_in_str = abap_true.\\\\n          ELSEIF lv_ch = '{'.\\\\n            lv_depth = lv_depth + 1.\\\\n          ELSEIF lv_ch = '}'.\\\\n            lv_depth = lv_depth - 1.\\\\n          ENDIF.\\\\n        ENDIF.\\\\n      ENDWHILE.\\\\n      IF lv_depth \\u003e 0.\\\\n        lv_end = lv_len_arr - 1.\\\\n      ENDIF.\\\\n      lv_len = lv_end - lv_start + 1.\\\\n      IF lv_len \\u003c= 0. EXIT. ENDIF.\\\\n      IF lv_start + lv_len \\u003e lv_len_arr.\\\\n        lv_len = lv_len_arr - lv_start.\\\\n      ENDIF.\\\\n      IF lv_len \\u003e 0.\\\\n        APPEND iv_arr+lv_start(lv_len) TO rt_.\\\\n      ENDIF.\\\\n      lv_pos = lv_end + 1.\\\\n    ENDWHILE.\\\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\\\n\\\\n  METHOD extract_balanced.\\\\n    \\\\\\\" braces/brackets inside JSON string literals must not count toward the\\\\n    \\\\\\\" depth: an escaped source like \\\\\\\"lv_open = '{'.\\\\\\\" would otherwise let the\\\\n    \\\\\\\" scan run past the closing bracket and eat the rest of the response\\\\n    DATA lv_len TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_pos TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_off TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_start TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_depth TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_out TYPE i.\\\\n    DATA lv_ch TYPE string.\\\\n    DATA lv_in_str TYPE abap_bool VALUE abap_false.\\\\n    lv_len = strlen( iv_json ).\\\\n\\\\n    FIND '\\\\\\\"' \\u0026\\u0026 iv_key \\u0026\\u0026 '\\\\\\\"' IN iv_json MATCH OFFSET lv_off.\\\\n    IF sy-subrc \\u003c\\u003e 0. RETURN. ENDIF.\\\\n    lv_pos = lv_off + strlen( iv_key ) + 2.\\\\n\\\\n    \\\\\\\" first opening bracket after the key (outside string literals)\\\\n    lv_start = -1.\\\\n    WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len.\\\\n      lv_ch = iv_json+lv_pos(1).\\\\n      IF lv_in_str = abap_true.\\\\n        IF lv_ch = `\\\\\\\\`.\\\\n          lv_pos = lv_pos + 2.\\\\n          CONTINUE.\\\\n        ELSEIF lv_ch = `\\\\\\\"`.\\\\n          lv_in_str = abap_false.\\\\n        ENDIF.\\\\n      ELSE.\\\\n        IF lv_ch = `\\\\\\\"`.\\\\n          lv_in_str = abap_true.\\\\n        ELSEIF lv_ch = iv_open.\\\\n          lv_start = lv_pos.\\\\n          EXIT.\\\\n        ENDIF.\\\\n      ENDIF.\\\\n      lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\\\n    ENDWHILE.\\\\n    IF lv_start \\u003c 0. RETURN. ENDIF.\\\\n\\\\n    \\\\\\\" balanced scan to the matching closing bracket, skipping string literals\\\\n    lv_depth = 1.\\\\n    lv_pos = lv_start.\\\\n    lv_in_str = abap_false.\\\\n    WHILE lv_pos \\u003c lv_len - 1 AND lv_depth \\u003e 0.\\\\n      lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\\\n      lv_ch = iv_json+lv_pos(1).\\\\n      IF lv_in_str = abap_true.\\\\n        IF lv_ch = `\\\\\\\\`.\\\\n          lv_pos = lv_pos + 1.\\\\n        ELSEIF lv_ch = `\\\\\\\"`.\\\\n          lv_in_str = abap_false.\\\\n        ENDIF.\\\\n      ELSE.\\\\n        IF lv_ch = `\\\\\\\"`.\\\\n          lv_in_str = abap_true.\\\\n        ELSEIF lv_ch = iv_open.\\\\n          lv_depth = lv_depth + 1.\\\\n        ELSEIF lv_ch = iv_close.\\\\n          lv_depth = lv_depth - 1.\\\\n        ENDIF.\\\\n      ENDIF.\\\\n    ENDWHILE.\\\\n    IF lv_depth \\u003e 0.\\\\n      lv_pos = lv_len - 1. \\\\\\\" unclosed: return the tail (previous behavior)\\\\n    ENDIF.\\\\n    lv_out = lv_pos - lv_start + 1.\\\\n    IF lv_start + lv_out \\u003e lv_len.\\\\n      lv_out = lv_len - lv_start.\\\\n    ENDIF.\\\\n    IF lv_out \\u003e 0.\\\\n      rv_ = iv_json+lv_start(lv_out).\\\\n    ENDIF.\\\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\\\n\\\\nENDCLASS.\\\\n\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7228010392402788103\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7228010392402788103\",\"name\":\"write_source\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"}],\"parallel_tool_calls\":true,\"previous_response_id\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":17390,\"input_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":14208},\"output_tokens\":8334,\"output_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":4998},\"total_tokens\":25724}}
